            "  The boundary entropy log(g) of a critical one-dimensional \
quantum system (or\ntwo-dimensional conformal field theory) is known to \
decrease under\nrenormalization group (RG) flow of the boundary theory. We \
study instead the\nbehavior of the boundary entropy as the bulk theory flows \
between two nearby\ncritical points. We use conformal perturbation theory to \
calculate the change\nin g due to a slightly relevant bulk perturbation and \
find that it has no\npreferred sign. The boundary entropy log(g) can \
therefore increase during\nappropriate bulk flows. This is demonstrated \
explicitly in flows between\nminimal models. We discuss the applications of \
this result to D-branes in\nstring theory and to impurity problems in \
condensed matter.\n", 
