            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"0907.4118\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Martin Kruczenski\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"M. Kruczenski and A. Tirziu\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"On the dressing phase in the SL(2) Bethe Ansatz\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"23 pages, 1 figure, LaTeX. V2: References added\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Phys.Rev.D80:086002,2009\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.80.086002\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  In this paper we study the function chi(x1,x2,g) that \
determines the dressing\\nphase that appears in the all-loop Bethe Ansatz \
equations for the SL(2) sector\\nof N=4 super Yang-Mills theory. First, we \
consider the coefficients c_{r,s}(g)\\nof the expansion of chi(x1,x2,g) in \
inverse powers of x1, x2. We obtain an\\nexpression in terms of a single \
integral valid for all values of the coupling\\ng. The expression is such \
that the small and large coupling expansion can be\\nsimply computed in \
agreement with the expected results. This proves the, up to\\nnow \
conjectured, equivalence of both expansions of the phase. The \
strong\\ncoupling expansion is only asymptotic but we find an exact \
expression for the\\nvalue of the residue which can be seen to decrease \
exponentially with g.\\n  After that, we consider the function chi(x1,x2,g) \
itself and, using the same\\nmethod, expand it for small and large coupling. \
All small and large coupling\\ncoefficients chi^(n)(x1,x2), for even and odd \
n, are explicitly given in terms\\nof finite sums or, alternatively, in terms \
of the residues of generating\\nfunctions at certain poles.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Thu, 23 Jul 2009 16:46:56 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v2\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Thu, 6 Aug 2009 20:53:33 GMT\""}]}], "}"}]}], 
                   "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2009-10-26\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Kruczenski\"", ",", "\"M.\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Tirziu\"", ",", "\"A.\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}]}], "}"}]}]}}, RowSpacings -> 1, ColumnAlignments -> 
             Left, ColumnAlignments -> Left], Column], 
