                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"S\\\\'ebastien Leurent\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Vladimir Kazakov and Sebastien Leurent\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Finite Size Spectrum of SU(N) Principal Chiral Field from \
Discrete\\n  Hirota Dynamics\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"32 pages, 6 figures; version 2 fixes typos, improves the \
numeric\\n  results, and adds a discussion of mode numbers and twisted \
case\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Nuclear Physics, Section B (2016), pp. 354-386\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"10.1016/j.nuclphysb.2015.11.012\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Using recently proposed method of discrete Hirota \
dynamics for integrable\\n(1+1)D quantum field theories on a finite space \
circle of length L, we derive\\nand test numerically a finite system of \
nonlinear integral equations for the\\nexact spectrum of energies of \
SU(N)xSU(N) principal chiral field model as\\nfunctions of m L, where m is \
the mass scale. We propose a determinant solution\\nof the underlying \
Y-system, or Hirota equation, in terms of determinants\\n(Wronskians) of NxN \
matrices parameterized by N-1 functions of the spectral\\nparameter, with the \
known analytical properties at finite L. Although the\\nmethod works in \
principle for any state, the explicit equations are written for\\nstates in \
the U(1) sector only. For N>2, we encounter and clarify a few\\nsubtleties in \
these equations related to the presence of bound states, absent\\nin the \
previously considered N=2 case. In particular, we solve these \
equations\\nnumerically for a few low-lying states for N=3 in a wide range of \
m L.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Sun, 11 Jul 2010 11:28:44 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v2\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Wed, 22 Apr 2015 15:04:14 GMT\""}]}], "}"}]}], 
                   "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2015-12-09\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
