               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1105.3231\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Arthur Lipstein\""}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Arthur E. Lipstein\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Integrability of N = 6 Chern-Simons Theory\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Ph.D. thesis, 125 pages; v3: references added\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  In 2008, Aharony, Bergman, Jafferis, and Maldacena \
(ABJM) discovered a\\nthree-dimensional Chern-Simons theory with N = 6 \
supersymmetry and conjectured\\nthat in a certain limit, this theory is dual \
to type IIA string theory on\\nAdS4xCP3. Since then, a great deal of evidence \
has been accumulated which\\nsuggests that the ABJM theory is integrable in \
the planar limit. Integrability\\nis a very useful property that allows many \
physical observables, such as\\nanomalous dimensions and scattering \
amplitudes, to be computed efficiently. In\\nthe first half of this thesis, \
we will explain how to use integrabilty to\\ncompute the anomalous dimensions \
of long, single-trace operators in the ABJM\\ntheory. In particular, we will \
describe how to compute them at weak coupling\\nusing a Bethe Ansatz, and how \
to compute them at strong coupling using string\\ntheory. The latter approach \
involves using algebraic curve and world-sheet\\ntechniques to compute the \
energies of string states dual to gauge theory\\noperators. In the second \
half of this thesis, we will discuss integrability\\nfrom the point of view \
of on-shell scattering amplitudes in the ABJM theory. In\\nparticular, we \
will describe how to parameterize the amplitudes in terms of\\nsupertwistors \
and how to relate higher-point tree-level amplitudes to\\nlower-point \
tree-level amplitudes using a recursion relation. We will also\\nexplain how \
this recursion relation can be used to show that all tree-level\\namplitudes \
of the ABJM theory are invariant under dual superconformal symmetry.\\nThis \
symmetry is hidden from the point of the action and implies that the\\ntheory \
has Yangian symmetry, which is a key feature of integrability. This\\nthesis \
is mainly based on the material in [101], [83], and [84].\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
