            "TBA, NLO Luscher correction, and double wrapping in twisted \
AdS/CFT", "comments" -> 
            "42 pages, 2 figures, v2: references added, v3: minor \
corrections", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP12(2011)059", 
            "report-no" -> "UMTG-271", "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  The ground-state energy of integrably-twisted theories is \
analyzed in finite\nvolume. We derive the leading and next-to-leading order \
(NLO) L\\\"uscher-type\ncorrections for large volumes of the vacuum energy \
for integrable theories with\ntwisted boundary conditions and twisted \
S-matrix. We then derive the twisted\nthermodynamic Bethe ansatz (TBA) \
equations to describe exactly the ground\nstate, from which we obtain an \
untwisted Y-system. The two approaches are\ncompared by expanding the TBA \
equations to NLO, and exact agreement is found.\nWe give explicit results for \
the O(4) model and for the three-parameter family\nof $\\gamma$-deformed \
(non-supersymmetric) planar AdS/CFT model, where the\nground-state energy can \
be nontrivial and can acquire finite-size corrections.\nThe NLO corrections, \
which correspond to double-wrapping diagrams, are\nexplicitly evaluated for \
the latter model at six loops.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Wed, 24 Aug 2011 19:09:42 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 1 Sep 2011 19:20:07 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v3", "created" -> 
               "Sun, 30 Oct 2011 21:08:08 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2015-05-30", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Ahn", "Changrim", ""}, {
              "Bajnok", "Zoltan", ""}, {"Bombardelli", "Diego", ""}, {
              "Nepomechie", "Rafael I.", ""}}}], "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[
          DiskBox[{7.082292928686257, -7.3335149508012805`}, 
           0.10102143756118451],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1109.5401\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Manuel Losi\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Manuel Losi\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Spiky strings and the AdS/CFT correspondence\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"200 pages, 37 figures, PhD thesis\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We first focus on the finite-gap formalism for type IIB \
strings in AdS_3 x\\nS^1, which allows to encode the semiclassical spectrum \
of a very large family\\nof string solutions in a Riemann surface, the \
spectral curve. Then, we show\\nthat, in the large angular momentum limit, it \
separates into two distinct\\nsurfaces, allowing the derivation of an \
explicit expression for the spectrum,\\nwhich is correspondingly \
characterised by two separate branches. The latter may\\nbe interpreted in \
terms of two kinds of spikes appearing on the strings:\\n\\\"large\\\" \
spikes, yielding an infinite contribution to the energy and \
angular\\nmomentum of the string, and \\\"small\\\" spikes, representing \
finite excitations\\nover the background of the \\\"large\\\" spikes.\\n  On \
the other side of the AdS/CFT correspondence, we consider the sl(2) \
sector\\nof N=4 super Yang-Mills theory. The corresponding 1-loop spectrum, \
in the large\\nconformal spin limit, is also encoded in a spectral curve and \
characterised in\\nterms of the so-called holes. We show that, with the \
appropriate\\nidentifications and with the usual extrapolation from weak to \
strong 't Hooft\\ncoupling described by the cusp anomalous dimension, the \
large-S spectra of\\ngauge theory and of string theory coincide. Furthermore, \
we explain how \\\"small\\\"\\nand \\\"large\\\" holes may be identified with \
\\\"small\\\" and \\\"large\\\" spikes.\\n  Finally, we discuss several \
explicit spiky string solutions in AdS_3 which\\ndisplay the finite-gap \
spectrum. We compute their spectral curves in the large\\nS limit, finding \
that they correspond to specific regions of the moduli space\\nof the \
finite-gap curves. We also explain how \\\"large\\\" spikes may be used \
in\\norder to extract a discrete system of degrees of freedom from string \
theory,\\nwhich can then be matched with the degrees of freedom of the dual \
gauge theory\\noperators, and how \\\"small\\\" spikes are in fact very \
similar to the Giant Magnons\\nliving in R x S^2.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
