            "  We first focus on the finite-gap formalism for type IIB \
strings in AdS_3 x\nS^1, which allows to encode the semiclassical spectrum of \
a very large family\nof string solutions in a Riemann surface, the spectral \
curve. Then, we show\nthat, in the large angular momentum limit, it separates \
into two distinct\nsurfaces, allowing the derivation of an explicit \
expression for the spectrum,\nwhich is correspondingly characterised by two \
separate branches. The latter may\nbe interpreted in terms of two kinds of \
spikes appearing on the strings:\n\"large\" spikes, yielding an infinite \
contribution to the energy and angular\nmomentum of the string, and \"small\" \
spikes, representing finite excitations\nover the background of the \"large\" \
spikes.\n  On the other side of the AdS/CFT correspondence, we consider the \
sl(2) sector\nof N=4 super Yang-Mills theory. The corresponding 1-loop \
spectrum, in the large\nconformal spin limit, is also encoded in a spectral \
curve and characterised in\nterms of the so-called holes. We show that, with \
the appropriate\nidentifications and with the usual extrapolation from weak \
to strong 't Hooft\ncoupling described by the cusp anomalous dimension, the \
large-S spectra of\ngauge theory and of string theory coincide. Furthermore, \
we explain how \"small\"\nand \"large\" holes may be identified with \
\"small\" and \"large\" spikes.\n  Finally, we discuss several explicit spiky \
string solutions in AdS_3 which\ndisplay the finite-gap spectrum. We compute \
their spectral curves in the large\nS limit, finding that they correspond to \
specific regions of the moduli space\nof the finite-gap curves. We also \
explain how \"large\" spikes may be used in\norder to extract a discrete \
system of degrees of freedom from string theory,\nwhich can then be matched \
with the degrees of freedom of the dual gauge theory\noperators, and how \
\"small\" spikes are in fact very similar to the Giant Magnons\nliving in R x \
S^2.\n", "versions" -> {{
