            "  We review recent developments in the study of gluon scattering \
amplitudes of\nthe four-dimensional maximally supersymmetric Yang-Mills \
theory at strong\ncoupling based on the gauge/string duality and its \
underlying integrability.\nThe scattering amplitudes are given by the area of \
minimal surfaces in\nfive-dimensional anti-de Sitter space with a null \
polygonal boundary. These\nminimal surfaces are described by integral \
equations of the form of the\nthermodynamic Bethe ansatz equations. \
Generalizing the result regarding the\nsix-point amplitudes, we observe a \
general connection between the minimal\nsurfaces and the homogenous \
sine-Gordon model, which is a class of\ntwo-dimensional integrable models \
associated with certain coset conformal field\ntheories. We also demonstrate \
that the identification of the underlying\nintegrable models is useful for \
analyzing the amplitudes by explicitly deriving\nan expansion of the \
six-point amplitudes around a special kinematic point.\n", 
