                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Matteo Beccaria, Guido Macorini\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"On a discrete symmetry of the Bremsstrahlung function in \
N=4 SYM\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"13 pages, 2 figures, v2: 3 more references, v3: 2 more \
figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We consider the quark anti-quark potential on the three \
sphere in planar N=4\\nSYM and the associated vacuum potential in the near \
BPS limit with L units of\\nR-charge. The associated Bremsstrahlung function \
B_L has been recently computed\\nanalytically by means of the Thermodynamical \
Bethe Ansatz. We discuss it at\\nstrong coupling by computing it at large but \
finite L. We provide strong\\nsupport to a special symmetry of the \
Bremsstrahlung function under the formal\\ndiscrete Z_2 symmetry L -> -1-L. \
In this context, it is the counterpart of the\\nreciprocity invariance \
discovered in the past in the spectrum of various gauge\\ninvariant composite \
operators. The Z_2 symmetry has remarkable consequences in\\nthe scaling \
limit where L is taken to be large with fixed ratio to the 't \
Hooft\\ncoupling. This limit organizes in inverse powers of the coupling and \
resembles\\nthe semiclassical expansion of the dual string theory which is \
indeed known to\\ncapture the leading classical term. We show that the \
various higher-order\\ncontributions to the Bremsstrahlung function obey \
several constraints and, in\\nparticular, the next-to-leading term, formally \
associated with the string\\none-loop correction, is completely determined by \
the classical contribution.\\nThe large L limit at strong coupling is also \
discussed.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
