            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We consider the quark anti-quark potential on the three sphere \
in planar N=4\nSYM and the associated vacuum potential in the near BPS limit \
with L units of\nR-charge. The associated Bremsstrahlung function B_L has \
been recently computed\nanalytically by means of the Thermodynamical Bethe \
Ansatz. We discuss it at\nstrong coupling by computing it at large but finite \
L. We provide strong\nsupport to a special symmetry of the Bremsstrahlung \
function under the formal\ndiscrete Z_2 symmetry L -> -1-L. In this context, \
it is the counterpart of the\nreciprocity invariance discovered in the past \
in the spectrum of various gauge\ninvariant composite operators. The Z_2 \
symmetry has remarkable consequences in\nthe scaling limit where L is taken \
to be large with fixed ratio to the 't Hooft\ncoupling. This limit organizes \
in inverse powers of the coupling and resembles\nthe semiclassical expansion \
of the dual string theory which is indeed known to\ncapture the leading \
classical term. We show that the various higher-order\ncontributions to the \
Bremsstrahlung function obey several constraints and, in\nparticular, the \
next-to-leading term, formally associated with the string\none-loop \
correction, is completely determined by the classical contribution.\nThe \
large L limit at strong coupling is also discussed.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 21 May 2013 14:40:40 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Wed, 22 May 2013 12:07:51 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v3", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 28 May 2013 08:22:52 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2013-05-29", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Beccaria", "Matteo", ""}, {
              "Macorini", "Guido", ""}}}], "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[
          DiskBox[{3.585875407707298, -2.638241101676904}, 
           0.10102143756118451],
