          DiskBox[{8.65824782464582, -4.280861681093258}, 0.10102143756118451],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1501.00111\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Saulius Valatka\""}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Saulius Valatka\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Exact Results in Supersymmetric Gauge Theories\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"PhD thesis, 154 pages\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  In this thesis we discuss supersymmetric gauge \
theories, focusing on exact\\nresults achieved using methods of \
integrability. For the larger portion of the\\nthesis we study the N=4 super \
Yang-Mills theory in the planar limit, a\\nrecurring topic being the Konishi \
anomalous dimension, which is roughly the\\nanalogue for the mass of the \
proton in quantum chromodynamics. The N=4\\nsupersymmetric Yang-Mills theory \
is known to be integrable in the planar limit,\\nwhich opens up a wealth of \
techniques one can employ in order to find results\\nin this limit valid at \
any value of the coupling. We begin with perturbation\\ntheory where the \
integrability of the theory first manifests itself. Here we\\nshowcase the \
first exact result, the so-called slope function, which is the\\nlinear small \
spin expansion coefficient of the generalized Konishi anomalous\\ndimension. \
We then move on to exact results mainly achieved using the novel\\nquantum \
spectral curve approach, the method allowing one to find scaling\\ndimensions \
of operators at arbitrary values of the coupling. As an example we\\nfind the \
second coefficient in the small spin expansion after the slope, which\\nwe \
call the curvature function. This allows us to extract \
non-trivial\\ninformation about the Konishi operator. Methods of \
integrability are also\\napplicable to other supersymmetric gauge theories \
such as ABJM, which in fact\\nshares many similarities with N=4 super \
Yang-Mills. We briefly review these\\nparallel developments in the last \
chapter of the thesis.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Wed, 31 Dec 2014 10:59:14 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2015-01-05\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Valatka\"", ",", "\"Saulius\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}], "}"}]}]}}, RowSpacings -> 1, ColumnAlignments -> 
             Left, ColumnAlignments -> Left], Column], 
