         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "1501.00111", "submitter" -> "Saulius Valatka", "authors" -> 
            "Saulius Valatka", "title" -> 
            "Exact Results in Supersymmetric Gauge Theories", "comments" -> 
            "PhD thesis, 154 pages", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null, 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            
            "  In this thesis we discuss supersymmetric gauge theories, \
focusing on exact\nresults achieved using methods of integrability. For the \
larger portion of the\nthesis we study the N=4 super Yang-Mills theory in the \
planar limit, a\nrecurring topic being the Konishi anomalous dimension, which \
is roughly the\nanalogue for the mass of the proton in quantum \
chromodynamics. The N=4\nsupersymmetric Yang-Mills theory is known to be \
integrable in the planar limit,\nwhich opens up a wealth of techniques one \
can employ in order to find results\nin this limit valid at any value of the \
coupling. We begin with perturbation\ntheory where the integrability of the \
theory first manifests itself. Here we\nshowcase the first exact result, the \
so-called slope function, which is the\nlinear small spin expansion \
coefficient of the generalized Konishi anomalous\ndimension. We then move on \
to exact results mainly achieved using the novel\nquantum spectral curve \
approach, the method allowing one to find scaling\ndimensions of operators at \
arbitrary values of the coupling. As an example we\nfind the second \
coefficient in the small spin expansion after the slope, which\nwe call the \
curvature function. This allows us to extract non-trivial\ninformation about \
the Konishi operator. Methods of integrability are also\napplicable to other \
supersymmetric gauge theories such as ABJM, which in fact\nshares many \
similarities with N=4 super Yang-Mills. We briefly review these\nparallel \
developments in the last chapter of the thesis.\n", 
