           "id" -> "1502.03976", "submitter" -> "Omar El Deeb", "authors" -> 
            "Omar El Deeb", "title" -> 
            "Lee-Yang Model in Presence of Defects", "comments" -> 
            "PhD Thesis 122 pages, 42 figure, 6 tables Note: ABF Lattice \
model\n  based on arXiv:hep-th/0012223 by Paul A. Pearce, Leung Chim, \
Changrim Ahn", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null, "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  I choose the Lee-Yang model and go through different \
approaches to analyze it\nusing the form factor approach and the bootstrap \
program, the lattice\ndescription and the lattice TBA equations for a full \
understanding of the\nmodel.\n  The bootstrap program aims to explicitly \
solve 1+1 dimensional integrable\nquantum field theories. In the first step, \
called the S-matrix bootstrap, the\nscattering matrix, is determined from its \
properties. In developing a defect\nform factor program the first step is the \
T-matrix bootstrap. Interacting\nintegrable defect theories are purely \
transmitting and topological. We analyze\nboth operators localized in the \
bulk and also on the defect. By finding their\nsolutions, the spectral \
representation of any correlator can be determined.\n  Next we consider the \
model on the lattice. The lattice approach allows to\nobtain both massive and \
massless excited TBA equations by studying the\ncontinuum scaling limit of \
the associated integrable lattice models. The most\nimportant input from the \
lattice approach is an insight into the analytic\nstructure of the excited \
state solutions of the TBA equations. Previously this\nstructure had to be \
guessed. In this thesis we turn our attention to the\nsimplest example of a \
non-unitary minimal theory, namely, the Lee-Yang minimal\nmodel M(2,5). We \
study the Lee-Yang model on the lattice. We analyze the\nperiodic, boundary \
and the seam cases in both massive and massless regimes. We\nderive their \
ground state TBA equations and analyze the flows between different\nboundary \
and defect conditions.\n  Finally, in the large volume limit, we calculate \
the L\\\"uscher correction\nterms of the TBA energy of a one-particle state. \
We display those ideas in the\nend of the thesis as this is an important \
direction of work in string theory,\nparticularly in the AdS/CFT \
correspondence.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
