               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Stijn van Tongeren\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Stijn J. van Tongeren\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Introduction to the thermodynamic Bethe ansatz\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"introductory review article, v3, 46 pages, 9 figures, \
minor\\n  typographical changes to match published version\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"J. Phys. A: Math. Theor. 49 (2016) 323005\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"10.1088/1751-8113/49/32/323005\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"HU-EP-16/07, HU-MATH-16/03\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"hep-th cond-mat.str-el math-ph math.MP nlin.SI\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We give a pedagogical introduction to the thermodynamic \
Bethe ansatz, a\\nmethod that allows us to describe the thermodynamics of \
integrable models whose\\nspectrum is found via the (asymptotic) Bethe \
ansatz. We set the stage by\\nderiving the Fermi-Dirac distribution and \
associated free energy of free\\nelectrons, and then in a similar though \
technically more complicated fashion\\ntreat the thermodynamics of integrable \
models, focusing on the one dimensional\\nBose gas with delta function \
interaction as a clean pedagogical example,\\nsecondly the XXX spin chain as \
an elementary (lattice) model with prototypical\\ncomplicating features in \
the form of bound states, and finally the SU(2) chiral\\nGross-Neveu model as \
a field theory example. Throughout this discussion we\\nemphasize the central \
role of particle and hole densities, whose relations\\ndetermine the model \
under consideration. We then discuss tricks that allow us\\nto use the same \
methods to describe the exact spectra of integrable field\\ntheories on a \
circle, in particular the chiral Gross-Neveu model. We moreover\\ndiscuss the \
simplification of TBA equations to Y systems, including the\\ntransition back \
to integral equations given sufficient analyticity data, in\\nsimple \
examples.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
