           "id" -> "1606.02951", "submitter" -> "Stijn van Tongeren", 
            "authors" -> "Stijn J. van Tongeren", "title" -> 
            "Introduction to the thermodynamic Bethe ansatz", "comments" -> 
            "introductory review article, v3, 46 pages, 9 figures, minor\n  \
typographical changes to match published version", "journal-ref" -> 
            "J. Phys. A: Math. Theor. 49 (2016) 323005", "doi" -> 
            "10.1088/1751-8113/49/32/323005", "report-no" -> 
            "HU-EP-16/07, HU-MATH-16/03", "categories" -> 
            "hep-th cond-mat.str-el math-ph math.MP nlin.SI", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We give a pedagogical introduction to the thermodynamic Bethe \
ansatz, a\nmethod that allows us to describe the thermodynamics of integrable \
models whose\nspectrum is found via the (asymptotic) Bethe ansatz. We set the \
stage by\nderiving the Fermi-Dirac distribution and associated free energy of \
free\nelectrons, and then in a similar though technically more complicated \
fashion\ntreat the thermodynamics of integrable models, focusing on the one \
dimensional\nBose gas with delta function interaction as a clean pedagogical \
example,\nsecondly the XXX spin chain as an elementary (lattice) model with \
prototypical\ncomplicating features in the form of bound states, and finally \
the SU(2) chiral\nGross-Neveu model as a field theory example. Throughout \
this discussion we\nemphasize the central role of particle and hole \
densities, whose relations\ndetermine the model under consideration. We then \
discuss tricks that allow us\nto use the same methods to describe the exact \
spectra of integrable field\ntheories on a circle, in particular the chiral \
Gross-Neveu model. We moreover\ndiscuss the simplification of TBA equations \
to Y systems, including the\ntransition back to integral equations given \
sufficient analyticity data, in\nsimple examples.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 9 Jun 2016 13:16:55 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 13 Jun 2016 12:57:58 GMT"}, {
