            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1608.05499\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Feodor A. Smirnov\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"F.A. Smirnov, A.B. Zamolodchikov\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"On space of integrable quantum field theories\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"25 pages\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"10.1016/j.nuclphysb.2016.12.014\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study deformations of 2D Integrable Quantum Field \
Theories (IQFT) which\\npreserve integrability (the existence of infinitely \
many local integrals of\\nmotion). The IQFT are understood as \\\"effective \
field theories\\\", with finite\\nultraviolet cutoff. We show that for any \
such IQFT there are infinitely many\\nintegrable deformations generated by \
scalar local fields $X_s$, which are in\\none-to-one correspondence with the \
local integrals of motion; moreover, the\\nscalars $X_s$ are built from the \
components of the associated conserved\\ncurrents in a universal way. The \
first of these scalars, $X_1$, coincides with\\nthe composite field \
$(T{\\\\bar T})$ built from the components of the\\nenergy-momentum tensor. \
The deformations of quantum field theories generated by\\n$X_1$ are \
\\\"solvable\\\" in a certain sense, even if the original theory is \
not\\nintegrable. In a massive IQFT the deformations $X_s$ are identified \
with the\\ndeformations of the corresponding factorizable S-matrix via the \
CDD factor. The\\nsituation is illustrated by explicit construction of the \
form factors of the\\noperators $X_s$ in sine-Gordon theory. We also make \
some remarks on the problem\\nof UV completeness of such integrable \
deformations.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
