Hernandez-Chifflet\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Undressing Confining Flux Tubes with $T\\\\bar T$\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"added some comments in the introduction, matches \
published version\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Phys. Rev. D 98, 114024 (2018)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.98.114024\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th hep-lat hep-ph\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Lattice QCD simulations provide crucial information \
about the worldsheet\\ndynamics of confining strings (flux tubes). An \
accurate extraction of the\\nworldsheet $S$-matrix from lattice spectra \
requires accounting for polarization\\neffects. Approximate integrability of \
the low energy worldsheet theory makes it\\npossible to apply the \
Thermodynamic Bethe Ansatz to incorporate polarization\\neffects at all \
orders in the number of windings and at the leading order in the\\nderivative \
expansion. However, a systematic application of this technique in\\nthe \
presence of non-integrable effects and for multiparticle states \
becomes\\nincreasingly challenging. We point out that a recently understood \
equivalence\\nbetween gravitational dressing and $T\\\\bar{T}$ deformation \
provides a fully\\nsystematic and straightforward recipe to incorporate the \
leading polarization\\neffects in the presence of an arbitrary inelasticity \
and for any number of\\nparticles. We illustrate this technique with several \
examples.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Fri, 3 Aug 2018 20:15:35 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v2\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Wed, 19 Dec 2018 17:21:36 GMT\""}]}], "}"}]}], 
                   "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2018-12-26\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
