            "Riccardo Conti, Stefano Negro and Roberto Tateo", "title" -> 
            "The $\\textrm{T}\\bar{\\textrm{T}}$ perturbation and its \
geometric\n  interpretation", "comments" -> 
            "v2 : Expanded version with new comments, numerical results and \
16\n  figures added. Minor typos corrected. Extra references added. 25 pages. \
4\n  figures v3 : JHEP version. Section 6 added. Minor typos corrected. Extra\
\n  reference added. 30 pages. 5 figures", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/JHEP02(2019)085", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "hep-th math-ph math.MP", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Starting from the recently-discovered \
$\\textrm{T}\\bar{\\textrm{T}}$-perturbed\nLagrangians, we prove that the \
deformed solutions to the classical EoMs for\nbosonic field theories are \
equivalent to the unperturbed ones but for a\nspecific field-dependent local \
change of coordinates. This surprising geometric\noutcome is fully consistent \
with the identification of\n$\\textrm{T}\\bar{\\textrm{T}}$-deformed 2D \
quantum field theories as topological\nJT gravity coupled to generic matter \
fields. Although our conclusion is valid\nfor generic interacting potentials, \
it first emerged from a detailed study of\nthe sine-Gordon model and in \
particular from the fact that solitonic\npseudo-spherical surfaces embedded \
in $\\mathbb R^3$ are left invariant by the\ndeformation. Analytic and \
numerical results concerning the perturbation of\nspecific sine-Gordon \
soliton solutions are presented.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 25 Sep 2018 17:05:12 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 16 Oct 2018 15:59:30 GMT"}, {
