           "id" -> "1902.00320", "submitter" -> "Benjamin Doyon", "authors" -> 
            "Benjamin Doyon and Jason Myers", "title" -> 
            "Fluctuations in ballistic transport from Euler hydrodynamics", 
            "comments" -> "27+22 pages, one figure", "journal-ref" -> 
            "J. Ann. Henri Poincar\\'e 21, 255-302 (2020)", "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/s00023-019-00860-w", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "cond-mat.stat-mech hep-th math-ph math.MP", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We propose a general formalism, within large deviation theory, \
giving access\nto the exact statistics of fluctuations of ballistically \
transported conserved\nquantities in homogeneous, stationary states. The \
formalism is expected to\napply to any system with an Euler hydrodynamic \
description, classical or\nquantum, integrable or not, in or out of \
equilibrium. We express the exact\nscaled cumulant generating function (or \
full counting statistics) for any\n(quasi-)local conserved quantity in terms \
of the flux Jacobian. We show that\nthe \"extended fluctuation relations\" of \
Bernard and Doyon follow from the\nlinearity of the hydrodynamic equations, \
forming a marker of \"freeness\" much\nlike the absence of hydrodynamic \
diffusion does. We show how an extension of\nthe formalism gives exact \
exponential behaviours of spatio-temporal two-point\nfunctions of twist \
fields, with applications to order-parameter dynamical\ncorrelations in \
arbitrary homogeneous, stationary state. We explain in what\nsituations the \
large deviation principle at the basis of the results fail, and\ndiscuss how \
this connects with nonlinear fluctuating hydrodynamics. Applying\nthe \
formalism to conformal hydrodynamics, we evaluate the exact cumulants of\n\
energy transport in quantum critical systems of arbitrary dimension at low \
but\nnonzero temperatures, observing a phase transition for Lorentz boosts at \
the\nsound velocity.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
