          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "1903.08179", "submitter" -> "Vincent Caudrelier", 
            "authors" -> "Vincent Caudrelier, Nicolas Crampe", "title" -> 
            "New integrable boundary conditions for the Ablowitz-Ladik model: \
from\n  Hamiltonian formalism to nonlinear mirror image method", "comments" -> 
            "32 pages. References added. Accepted authors version", 
            "journal-ref" -> "Nucl. Phys. B946 (2019) 114720", "doi" -> 
            "10.1016/j.nuclphysb.2019.114720", "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> "math-ph math.MP nlin.SI", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            
            "  Using Sklyanin's classical theory of integrable boundary \
conditions, we use\nthe Hamiltonian approach to derive new integrable \
boundary conditions for the\nAblowitz-Ladik model on the finite and half \
infinite lattice. In the case of\nhalf infinite lattice, the special and new \
emphasis of this paper is to connect\ndirectly the Hamiltonian approach, \
based on the classical $r$-matrix, with the\nzero curvature representation \
and B\\\"acklund transformation approach that\nallows one to implement a \
nonlinear mirror image method and construct explicit\nsolutions. It is shown \
that for our boundary conditions, which generalise\n(discrete) Robin boundary \
conditions, a nontrivial extension of the known\nmirror image method to what \
we call {\\it time-dependent boundary conditions} is\nneeded. A careful \
discussion of this extension is given and is facilitated by\nintroducing the \
notion of intrinsic and extrinsic picture for describing\nboundary \
conditions. This gives the specific link between Sklyanin's reflection\n\
matrices and B\\\"acklund transformations combined with folding, {\\it in the \
case\nof non-diagonal reflection matrices}. All our results reproduce the \
known Robin\nboundary conditions setup as a special case: the diagonal case. \
Explicit\nformulas for constructing multisoliton solutions on the \
half-lattice with our\ntime-dependent boundary conditions are given and some \
examples are plotted.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 19 Mar 2019 18:00:11 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 19 Aug 2019 17:27:56 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2019-08-20", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Caudrelier", "Vincent", ""}, {
              "Crampe", "Nicolas", ""}}}], "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
