               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Conserved currents and \
$\\\\text{T}\\\\bar{\\\\text{T}}_s$ irrelevant\\n  deformations of 2D \
integrable field theories\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"39 pages, 3 figures. v2: typos corrected, extended \
version with more\\n  results on the link between the classical and the \
quantum analysis\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP11(2019)120\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  It has been recently discovered that the $\\\\text{T}\\\
\\bar{\\\\text{T}}$ deformation\\nis closely-related to Jackiw-Teitelboim \
gravity. At classical level, the\\nintroduction of this perturbation induces \
an interaction between the\\nstress-energy tensor and space-time and the \
deformed EoMs can be mapped,\\nthrough a field-dependent change of \
coordinates, onto the corresponding\\nundeformed ones. The effect of this \
perturbation on the quantum spectrum is\\nnon-perturbatively described by an \
inhomogeneous Burgers equation. In this\\npaper, we point out that there \
exist infinite families of models where the\\ngeometry couples instead to \
generic combinations of local conserved currents\\nlabelled by the Lorentz \
spin. In spirit, these generalisations are similar to\\nthe \
$\\\\text{J}\\\\bar{\\\\text{T}}$ model as the resulting theories and \
the\\ncorresponding scattering phase factors are not Lorentz invariant. The \
link with\\nthe $\\\\text{J}\\\\bar{\\\\text{T}}$ model is discussed in \
detail. While the classical\\nsetup described here is very general, we shall \
use the sine-Gordon model and\\nits CFT limit as explanatory quantum \
examples. Most of the final equations and\\nconsiderations are, however, of \
broader validity or easily generalisable to\\nmore complicated \
systems.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
