            "Riccardo Conti, Stefano Negro, Roberto Tateo", "title" -> 
            "Conserved currents and $\\text{T}\\bar{\\text{T}}_s$ irrelevant\n\
  deformations of 2D integrable field theories", "comments" -> 
            "39 pages, 3 figures. v2: typos corrected, extended version with \
more\n  results on the link between the classical and the quantum analysis", 
            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP11(2019)120", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  It has been recently discovered that the \
$\\text{T}\\bar{\\text{T}}$ deformation\nis closely-related to \
Jackiw-Teitelboim gravity. At classical level, the\nintroduction of this \
perturbation induces an interaction between the\nstress-energy tensor and \
space-time and the deformed EoMs can be mapped,\nthrough a field-dependent \
change of coordinates, onto the corresponding\nundeformed ones. The effect of \
this perturbation on the quantum spectrum is\nnon-perturbatively described by \
an inhomogeneous Burgers equation. In this\npaper, we point out that there \
exist infinite families of models where the\ngeometry couples instead to \
generic combinations of local conserved currents\nlabelled by the Lorentz \
spin. In spirit, these generalisations are similar to\nthe \
$\\text{J}\\bar{\\text{T}}$ model as the resulting theories and the\n\
corresponding scattering phase factors are not Lorentz invariant. The link \
with\nthe $\\text{J}\\bar{\\text{T}}$ model is discussed in detail. While the \
classical\nsetup described here is very general, we shall use the sine-Gordon \
model and\nits CFT limit as explanatory quantum examples. Most of the final \
equations and\nconsiderations are, however, of broader validity or easily \
generalisable to\nmore complicated systems.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 19 Apr 2019 10:49:04 GMT"}, {
