         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "1909.00697", "submitter" -> "Calum Milloy", "authors" -> 
            "Giulio Falcioni, Einan Gardi and Calum Milloy", "title" -> 
            "Relating amplitude and PDF factorisation through Wilson-line \
geometries", "comments" -> 
            "53 pages, 5 figures; references added, typos fixed, journal \
version", "journal-ref" -> "JHEP11(2019)100", "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/JHEP11(2019)100", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "hep-ph hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We study long-distance singularities governing different \
physical quantities\ninvolving massless partons in perturbative QCD by using \
factorisation in terms\nof Wilson-line correlators. By isolating the \
process-independent hard-collinear\nsingularities from quark and gluon form \
factors, and identifying these with the\nones governing the elastic limit of \
the perturbative Parton Distribution\nFunctions (PDFs) -- $\\delta(1-x)$ in \
the large-$x$ limit of DGLAP splitting\nfunctions -- we extract the anomalous \
dimension controlling soft singularities\nof the PDFs, verifying that it \
admits Casimir scaling. We then perform an\nindependent diagrammatic \
computation of the latter using its definition in\nterms of Wilson lines, \
confirming explicitly the above result through two\nloops. By comparing our \
eikonal PDF calculation to that of the eikonal form\nfactor by Erdogan and \
Sterman and the classical computation of the closed\nparallelogram by \
Korchemsky and Korchemskaya, a consistent picture emerges\nwhereby all \
singularities emerge in diagrammatic configurations localised at\nthe cusps \
or along lightlike lines, but where distinct contributions to the\nanomalous \
dimensions are associated with finite (closed) lightlike segments as\n\
compared to infinite (open) ones. Both are relevant for resumming large\n\
logarithms in physical quantities, notably the anomalous dimension \
controlling\nDrell-Yan or Higgs production near threshold on the one hand, \
and the gluon\nRegge trajectory controlling the high-energy limit of partonic \
scattering on\nthe other.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 2 Sep 2019 12:52:37 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 21 Nov 2019 15:58:39 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2019-11-22", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Falcioni", "Giulio", ""}, {
              "Gardi", "Einan", ""}, {"Milloy", "Calum", ""}}}], 
