                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The four dimensional $\\\\mathcal{N}=4$ \
super-Yang-Mills (SYM) theory exhibits\\nrich dynamics in the presence of \
codimension-one conformal defects. The new\\nstructure constants of the \
extended operator algebra consist of one-point\\nfunctions of local operators \
which are nonvanishing due to the defect insertion\\nand carry nontrivial \
coupling dependence. We study an important class of\\nhalf-BPS superconformal \
defects engineered by D5 branes that share three common\\ndirections with the \
D3 branes and involve Nahm pole configurations for the SYM\\nfields on the D3 \
brane worldvolume. In the planar large $N$ limit, we \
obtain\\nnon-perturbative results in the 't Hooft coupling $\\\\lambda$ for \
the defect\\none-point functions of both BPS and non-BPS operators, building \
upon recent\\nprogress in localization and integrability methods. For BPS \
operator insertions\\nin the SYM with D5-brane type boundary or interface, we \
derive an effective two\\ndimensional defect-Yang-Mills (dYM) theory from \
supersymmetric localization,\\nwhich gives an efficient way to extract defect \
observables and generates a\\nnovel matrix model for the defect one-point \
function. By solving the matrix\\nmodel in the large $N$ limit, we obtain \
exact results in $\\\\lambda$ which\\ninterpolate between perturbative \
Feynman diagram contributions in the weak\\ncoupling limit and IIB string \
theory predictions on $AdS_5\\\\times S^5$ in the\\nstrong coupling regime, \
providing a precision test of AdS/CFT with interface\\ndefects. For general \
non-BPS operators, we develop a non-perturbative\\nbootstrap-type program for \
integrable boundary states on the worldsheet of the\\nIIB string theory, \
corresponding to the interface defects in the planar SYM.\\nSuch integrable \
boundary states are constrained by a set of general consistency\\nconditions \
for which we present explicit solutions that reproduce and extend\\nthe known \
results at weak coupling from integrable spin-chain methods.\\n\""}]}, {
