               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2006.00361\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Johannes Henn\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Johannes M. Henn\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"What can we learn about QCD and collider physics from N=4 \
super\\n  Yang-Mills?\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"30 pages, 8 figures. Invited review to appear in Annual \
Review of\\n  Nuclear and Particle Science; v2: presentation improved\""}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"10.1146/annurev-nucl-102819-100428\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th hep-ex hep-ph\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Tremendous ongoing theory efforts are dedicated to \
developing new methods for\\nQCD calculations. Qualitative rather than \
incremental advances are needed to\\nfully exploit data still to be collected \
at the LHC. The maximally\\nsupersymmetric Yang-Mills theory (${\\\\mathcal \
N}=4$ sYM) shares with QCD the\\ngluon sector, which contains the most \
complicated Feynman graphs, but at the\\nsame time has many special \
properties, and is believed to be solvable exactly.\\nIt is natural to ask \
what we can learn from advances in ${\\\\mathcal N}=4$ sYM\\nfor addressing \
difficult problems in QCD. With this in mind, we review here\\nseveral \
remarkable developments and highlights of recent results in \
${\\\\mathcal\\nN}=4$ sYM. This includes all-order results for certain \
scattering amplitudes,\\nnovel symmetries, surprising geometrical structures \
of loop integrands, novel\\ntools for the calculation of Feynman integrals, \
and bootstrap methods. While\\nseveral insights and tools have already been \
carried over to QCD and have\\ncontributed to state-of-the-art calculations \
for LHC physics, we argue that\\nthere is a host of further fascinating ideas \
waiting to be explored.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
