            "  Tremendous ongoing theory efforts are dedicated to developing \
new methods for\nQCD calculations. Qualitative rather than incremental \
advances are needed to\nfully exploit data still to be collected at the LHC. \
The maximally\nsupersymmetric Yang-Mills theory (${\\mathcal N}=4$ sYM) \
shares with QCD the\ngluon sector, which contains the most complicated \
Feynman graphs, but at the\nsame time has many special properties, and is \
believed to be solvable exactly.\nIt is natural to ask what we can learn from \
advances in ${\\mathcal N}=4$ sYM\nfor addressing difficult problems in QCD. \
With this in mind, we review here\nseveral remarkable developments and \
highlights of recent results in ${\\mathcal\nN}=4$ sYM. This includes \
all-order results for certain scattering amplitudes,\nnovel symmetries, \
surprising geometrical structures of loop integrands, novel\ntools for the \
calculation of Feynman integrals, and bootstrap methods. While\nseveral \
insights and tools have already been carried over to QCD and have\n\
contributed to state-of-the-art calculations for LHC physics, we argue that\n\
there is a host of further fascinating ideas waiting to be explored.\n", 
