 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The $\\\\text{T}\\\\bar{\\\\text{T}}$-deformed \
classical Lagrangian of a 2D Lorentz\\ninvariant theory can be derived from \
the original one, perturbed only at first\\norder by the bare \
$\\\\text{T}\\\\bar{\\\\text{T}}$ composite field, through \
a\\nfield-dependent change of coordinates. Considering, as an example, \
the\\nnonlinear Schr\\\\\\\"odinger (NLS) model with generic potential, we \
apply this idea\\nto non-relativistic models. The form of the deformed \
Lagrangian contains a\\nsquare-root and is similar but different from that \
for relativistic bosons. We\\nstudy the deformed bright, grey and Peregrine's \
soliton solutions. Contrary to\\nnaive expectations, the $\\\\text{T}\\\\bar{\
\\\\text{T}}$-perturbation of nonlinear\\nSchr\\\\\\\"odinger NLS with \
quartic potential does not trivially emerge from a\\nstandard \
non-relativistic limit of the deformed sinh-Gordon field theory. The\\n$c \
\\\\rightarrow \\\\infty$ outcome corresponds to a different type of \
irrelevant\\ndeformation. We derive the corresponding Poisson bracket \
structure, the\\nequations of motion and discuss various interesting aspects \
of this alternative\\ntype of perturbation, including links with the recent \
literature.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
