               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"hep-th cond-mat.stat-mech math-ph math.MP nlin.SI\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study solutions of the Thermodynamic Bethe Ansatz \
equations for\\nrelativistic theories defined by the factorizable $S$-matrix \
of an integrable\\nQFT deformed by CDD factors. Such $S$-matrices appear \
under generalized TTbar\\ndeformations of integrable QFT by special \
irrelevant operators. The TBA\\nequations, of course, determine the ground \
state energy $E(R)$ of the\\nfinite-size system, with the spatial coordinate \
compactified on a circle of\\ncircumference $R$. We limit attention to \
theories involving just one kind of\\nstable particles, and consider \
deformations of the trivial (free fermion or\\nboson) $S$-matrix by CDD \
factors with two elementary poles and regular high\\nenergy asymptotics -- \
the \\\"2CDD model\\\". We find that for all values of the\\nparameters \
(positions of the CDD poles) the TBA equations exhibit two real\\nsolutions \
at $R$ greater than a certain parameter-dependent value $R_*$, which\\nwe \
refer to as the primary and secondary branches. The primary branch \
is\\nidentified with the standard iterative solution, while the secondary one \
is\\nunstable against iterations and needs to be accessed through an \
alternative\\nnumerical method known as pseudo-arc-length continuation. The \
two branches\\nmerge at the \\\"turning point\\\" $R_*$ (a square-root \
branching point). The\\nsingularity signals a Hagedorn behavior of the \
density of high energy states of\\nthe deformed theories, a feature \
incompatible with the Wilsonian notion of a\\nlocal QFT originating from a UV \
fixed point, but typical for string theories.\\nThis behavior of $E(R)$ is \
qualitatively the same as the one for standard TTbar\\ndeformations of local \
QFT.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
