Michelangelo Preti\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Bootstrability in Defect CFT: Integrated Correlators and \
Sharper Bounds\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Appendix F added showing how another integral relation \
can be used to\\n  derive the bps part of the correlator from integrability \
alone. Ancillary\\n  file added with numerical and analytical conformal data. \
Other minor\\n  corrections\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP05(2022)164\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We continue to develop Bootstrability -- a method \
merging Integrability and\\nConformal Bootstrap to extract CFT data in \
integrable conformal gauge theories\\nsuch as $\\\\mathcal{N}$=4 SYM. In this \
paper, we consider the 1D defect CFT\\ndefined on a $\\\\frac{1}{2}$-BPS \
Wilson line in the theory, whose\\nnon-perturbative spectrum is governed by \
the Quantum Spectral Curve (QSC). In\\naddition, we use that the deformed \
setup of a cusped Wilson line is also\\ncontrolled by the QSC. In terms of \
the defect CFT, this translates into two\\nnontrivial relations connecting \
integrated 4-point correlators to cusp spectral\\ndata, such as the \
Bremsstrahlung and Curvature functions -- known analytically\\nfrom the QSC. \
Combining these new constraints and the spectrum of the $10$\\nlowest-lying \
states with the Numerical Conformal Bootstrap, we obtain very\\nsharp \
rigorous numerical bounds for the structure constant of the \
first\\nnon-protected state, giving this observable with seven digits \
precision for the\\n't Hooft coupling in the intermediate coupling region\\n$\
\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{\\\\lambda}}{4\\\\pi}\\\\sim 1$, with the error decreasing \
quickly at large\\n't Hooft coupling. Furthermore, for the same structure \
constant we obtain a\\n$4$-loop analytic result at weak coupling. We also \
present results for excited\\nstates.\\n\""}]}, {
