                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Refining the Cutoff 3d Gravity / $T\\\\bar{T}$ \
Correspondence\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"30 pages, minor typos fixed, new subsection added to make \
contact\\n  with previous literature, version accepted for publication in \
JHEP\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Pure gravity in AdS$_3$ is a theory of boundary \
excitations, most simply\\nexpressed as a constrained free scalar with an \
improved stress tensor that is\\nneeded to match the Brown--Henneaux central \
charge. Excising a finite part of\\nAdS gives rise to a static gauge \
Nambu--Goto action for the boundary graviton.\\nWe show that this is the $T\\\
\\bar{T}$ deformation of the infinite volume theory,\\nas the effect of the \
improvement term on the deformed action can be absorbed\\ninto a field \
redefinition. The classical gravitational stress tensor is\\nreproduced order \
by order by the $T\\\\bar{T}$ trace equation. We calculate the\\nfinite \
volume energy spectrum in static gauge and find that the trace \
equation\\nimposes sufficient constraints on the ordering ambiguities to \
guarantee\\nagreement with the light-cone gauge prediction. The correlation \
functions,\\nhowever, are not completely fixed by the trace equation. We show \
how both the\\ngravitational action and the $T\\\\bar{T}$ deformation allow \
for finite\\nimprovement terms, and we match these to the undetermined total \
derivative\\nterms in Zamolodchikov's point splitting definition of the \
$T\\\\bar{T}$ operator.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
