           "id" -> "2206.03415", "submitter" -> "Riccardo Conti", "authors" -> 
            "Riccardo Conti, Jacopo Romano, Roberto Tateo", "title" -> 
            "Metric approach to a $\\mathrm{T}\\bar{\\mathrm{T}}-$like \
deformation in\n  arbitrary dimensions", "comments" -> 
            "V3: 23 pages, one-parameter extension of the previous results in\
\n  section 2; new results in section 3; references and a note added", 
            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP09(2022)085", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th math-ph math.MP", 
            "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We consider a one-parameter family of composite fields -- \
bi-linear in the\ncomponents of the stress-energy tensor -- which generalise \
the\n$\\mathrm{T}\\bar{\\mathrm{T}}$ operator to arbitrary space-time \
dimension $d\\geq\n2$. We show that they induce a deformation of the \
classical action which is\nequivalent -- at the level of the dynamics -- to a \
field-dependent modification\nof the background metric tensor according to a \
specific flow equation. Even\nthough the starting point is the flat space, \
the deformed metric is generally\ncurved for any $d>2$, thus implying that \
the corresponding deformation can not\nbe interpreted as a coordinate \
transformation. The central part of the paper is\ndevoted to the development \
of a recursive algorithm to compute the coefficients\nof the power series \
expansion of the solution to the metric flow equation. We\nshow that, under \
some quite restrictive assumptions on the stress-energy\ntensor, the power \
series yields an exact solution. Finally, we consider a class\nof theories in \
$d=4$ whose stress-energy tensor fulfils the assumptions above\nmentioned, \
namely the family of abelian gauge theories in $d=4$. For such\ntheories, we \
obtain the exact expression of the deformed metric and the\nvierbein. In \
particular, the latter result implies that ModMax theory in a\nspecific \
curved space is dynamically equivalent to its Born-Infeld-like\nextension in \
flat space. We also discuss a dimensional reduction of the latter\ntheories \
from $d=4$ to $d=2$ in which an interesting marginal deformation of\n$d=2$ \
field theories emerges.\n", 
