           "id" -> "2211.07810", "submitter" -> "Paul Ryan", "authors" -> 
            "Andrea Cavagli\\`a, Simon Ekhammar, Nikolay Gromov, Paul Ryan", 
            "title" -> 
            "Exploring the Quantum Spectral Curve for AdS${}_3$/CFT${}_2$", 
            "comments" -> Null, "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null, 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th math-ph math.MP", 
            "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Despite the rich and fruitful history of the integrability \
approach to string\ntheory on the $AdS_3\\times S^3\\times T^4$ background, \
it has not been possible\nto extract many concrete predictions from \
integrability, except in a strict\nasymptotic regime of large quantum \
numbers, due to the severity of wrapping\neffects. The situation changed \
radically with two independent and identical\nproposals for the Quantum \
Spectral Curve (QSC) for this system in a background\nof pure Ramond-Ramond \
flux. This formulation is expected to capture all\nwrapping effects exactly \
and describe the full planar spectrum. Massless modes\nconjecturally manifest \
themselves in a new property of this QSC: the\nnon-quadratic nature of the \
branch-cut singularities of the QSC Q-functions.\nThis feature implies new \
technical challenges in solving the QSC equations as\ncompared to the \
well-studied case of N=4 SYM. In this paper we resolve these\ndifficulties \
and obtain the first ever predictions for generic unprotected\nstring \
excitations. We explain how to extract a systematic expansion around the\n\
analogue of the weak 't Hooft coupling limit in N=4 SYM and also obtain\n\
high-precision numerical results. This concrete data and others obtainable \
from\nthe QSC could help to identify the so-far mysterious dual CFT.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
