                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},
               {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The large-scale behaviour of entanglement entropy in \
finite-density states,\\nin and out of equilibrium, can be understood using \
the physical picture of\\nparticle pairs. However, the full theoretical \
origin of this picture is not\\nfully established yet. In this work, we \
clarify this picture by investigating\\nentanglement entropy using its \
connection with the large-deviation theory for\\nthermodynamic and \
hydrodynamic fluctuations. We apply the universal framework\\nof Ballistic \
Fluctuation Theory (BFT), based the Euler hydrodynamics of the\\nmodel, to \
correlation functions of \\\\emph{branch-point twist fields}, the\\nstarting \
point for computing R\\\\'enyi entanglement entropies within the \
replica\\napproach. Focusing on free fermionic systems in order to illustrate \
the ideas,\\nwe show that both the equilibrium behavior and the dynamics of R\
\\\\'enyi\\nentanglement entropies can be fully derived from the BFT. In \
particular, we\\nemphasise that long-range correlations develop after quantum \
quenches, and\\naccounting for these explain the structure of the \
entanglement growth. We\\nfurther show that this growth is related to \
fluctuations of charge transport,\\ngeneralising to quantum quenches the \
relation between charge fluctuations and\\nentanglement observed earlier. The \
general ideas we introduce suggest that the\\nlarge-scale behaviour of \
entanglement has its origin within hydrodynamic\\nfluctuations.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
