            "Giuseppe Del Vecchio Del Vecchio and Benjamin Doyon and Paola \
Ruggiero", "title" -> 
            "Entanglement R\\'enyi Entropies from Ballistic Fluctuation \
Theory: the\n  free fermionic case", "comments" -> "53 pages, 2 figures", 
            "journal-ref" -> "SciPost Phys. Core 7, 005 (2024)", "doi" -> 
            "10.21468/SciPostPhysCore.7.1.005", "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> "quant-ph cond-mat.stat-mech", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  The large-scale behaviour of entanglement entropy in \
finite-density states,\nin and out of equilibrium, can be understood using \
the physical picture of\nparticle pairs. However, the full theoretical origin \
of this picture is not\nfully established yet. In this work, we clarify this \
picture by investigating\nentanglement entropy using its connection with the \
large-deviation theory for\nthermodynamic and hydrodynamic fluctuations. We \
apply the universal framework\nof Ballistic Fluctuation Theory (BFT), based \
the Euler hydrodynamics of the\nmodel, to correlation functions of \
\\emph{branch-point twist fields}, the\nstarting point for computing R\\'enyi \
entanglement entropies within the replica\napproach. Focusing on free \
fermionic systems in order to illustrate the ideas,\nwe show that both the \
equilibrium behavior and the dynamics of R\\'enyi\nentanglement entropies can \
be fully derived from the BFT. In particular, we\nemphasise that long-range \
correlations develop after quantum quenches, and\naccounting for these \
explain the structure of the entanglement growth. We\nfurther show that this \
growth is related to fluctuations of charge transport,\ngeneralising to \
quantum quenches the relation between charge fluctuations and\nentanglement \
observed earlier. The general ideas we introduce suggest that the\n\
large-scale behaviour of entanglement has its origin within hydrodynamic\n\
fluctuations.\n", 
