            "Painlev\\'e Kernels and Surface Defects at Strong Coupling", 
            "comments" -> 
            "Version 2: 50 pages, 1 figure. Minor corrections and \
clarifications,\n  additional references. Comments welcome", "journal-ref" -> 
            "Ann. Henri Poincar\\'e (2024)", "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/s00023-024-01469-4", "report-no" -> "CERN-TH-2023-169", 
            "categories" -> "hep-th math-ph math.MP math.SP", "license" -> 
            "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  It is well established that the spectral analysis of \
canonically quantized\nfour-dimensional Seiberg-Witten curves can be \
systematically studied via the\nNekrasov-Shatashvili functions. In this \
paper, we explore another aspect of the\nrelation between $\\mathcal{N}=2$ \
supersymmetric gauge theories in four\ndimensions and operator theory. \
Specifically, we study an example of an\nintegral operator associated with \
Painlev\\'e equations and whose spectral\ntraces are related to correlation \
functions of the 2d Ising model. This\noperator does not correspond to a \
canonically quantized Seiberg-Witten curve,\nbut its kernel can nevertheless \
be interpreted as the density matrix of an\nideal Fermi gas. Adopting the \
approach of Tracy and Widom, we provide an\nexplicit expression for its \
eigenfunctions via an $\\mathrm{O}(2)$ matrix model.\nWe then show that these \
eigenfunctions are computed by surface defects in\n$\\mathrm{SU}(2)$ super \
Yang-Mills in the self-dual phase of the\n$\\Omega$-background. Our result \
also yields a strong coupling expression for\nsuch defects which resums the \
instanton expansion. Even though we focus on one\nconcrete example, we expect \
these results to hold for a larger class of\noperators arising in the context \
of isomonodromic deformation equations.\n", 
