         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "hep-th/0606138", "submitter" -> "Matteo Beccaria", 
            "authors" -> "Matteo Beccaria, Carmine Ortix", "title" -> 
            "Strong coupling anomalous dimensions of ${\\cal N} = 4$ super \
Yang-Mills", "comments" -> "38 pages, 16 eps figures", "journal-ref" -> 
            "JHEP0609:016,2006", "doi" -> "10.1088/1126-6708/2006/09/016", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> Null, 
            "abstract" -> 
            "  We study the strong coupling behaviour of fixed length single \
trace operators\nin the scalar SU(2) sector of ${\\cal N} = 4$ SYM. We assume \
the recently\nproposed connection with a twisted half-filled Hubbard model. \
By explicit\ndirect diagonalization of operators with length $L=4, 6, 8$ we \
study the full\n{\\em perturbative multiplet} of those lattice states which \
have a clear\ncorrespondence with gauge theory composite operators. For this \
multiplet, we\nfollow the weak-strong coupling flow to free fermion states \
and identify in\nparticular the precise asymptotic fermion configuration. \
Next, we analyze the\nLieb-Wu equations of the twisted Hubbard model. For the \
antiferromagnetic state\nwe derive its strong coupling expansion working at \
$L$ up to 32. We also study\nthe lightest state in the perturbative \
multiplet. This state is non trivial\nsince involves complex solutions of the \
Lieb-Wu equations. It is particularly\ninteresting for $AdS_5\\times S^5$ \
duality since it is dual to the folded string\nsemiclassical solution in the \
thermodynamical limit. We are able to perform the\nfull analysis and compute \
the next-to-next-to leading terms in the strong\ncoupling expansion for the \
non trivial lengths L=12 and L=20. A general formula\nis proposed for the NLO \
expansion for any $L=4(2k+1)$, $k\\in\\mathbb{N}$.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 15 Jun 2006 15:05:34 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2009-11-11", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Beccaria", "Matteo", ""}, {
              "Ortix", "Carmine", ""}}}], "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[
          DiskBox[{5.673989974737717, -5.957976360452669}, 
           0.10102143756118451],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th/0607236\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Matteo Beccaria\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Matteo Beccaria, Luigi Del Debbio\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Bethe Ansatz solutions for highest states in ${\\\\cal \
N}=4$ SYM and\\n  AdS/CFT duality\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"42 pages, JHEP style LaTeX\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP 0609:025,2006\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1088/1126-6708/2006/09/025\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We consider the operators with highest anomalous \
dimension $\\\\Delta$ in the\\ncompact rank-one sectors \
$\\\\mathfrak{su}(1|1)$ and $\\\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ of ${\\\\cal\\nN}=4$ super \
Yang-Mills. We study the flow of $\\\\Delta$ from weak to strong 't\\nHooft \
coupling $\\\\lambda$ by solving (i) the all-loop gauge Bethe Ansatz, \
(ii)\\nthe quantum string Bethe Ansatz. The two calculations are carefully \
compared in\\nthe strong coupling limit and exhibit different exponents \
$\\\\nu$ in the leading\\norder expansion $\\\\Delta\\\\sim \
\\\\lambda^{\\\\nu}$. We find $\\\\nu = 1/2$ and $\\\\nu = 1/4$\\nfor the \
gauge or string solution. This strong coupling discrepancy is \
not\\nunexpected, and it provides an explicit example where the gauge Bethe \
Ansatz\\nsolution cannot be trusted at large $\\\\lambda$. Instead, the \
string solution\\nperfectly reproduces the Gubser-Klebanov-Polyakov law \
$\\\\Delta = 2\\\\sqrt{n}\\n\\\\lambda^{1/4}$. In particular, we provide an \
analytic expression for the\\ninteger level $n$ as a function of the U(1) \
charge in both sectors.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Fri, 28 Jul 2006 11:11:21 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
