         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "0710.1568", "submitter" -> "Arutyunov Gleb E", "authors" -> 
            "Gleb Arutyunov and Sergey Frolov", "title" -> 
            "On String S-matrix, Bound States and TBA", "comments" -> 
            "77 pages, 6 figures, v2: the statement about the periodicity\n  \
condition for mirror fermions corrected; typos corrected; references added,\n \
 v3: misprints corrected", "journal-ref" -> "JHEP 0712:024,2007", "doi" -> 
            "10.1088/1126-6708/2007/12/024", "report-no" -> 
            "ITP-UU-07-50, SPIN-07-37, TCDMATH 07-15", "categories" -> 
            "hep-th", "license" -> Null, "abstract" -> 
            "  The study of finite J effects for the light-cone AdS \
superstring by means of\nthe Thermodynamic Bethe Ansatz requires an \
understanding of a companion 2d\ntheory which we call the mirror model. It is \
obtained from the original string\nmodel by the double Wick rotation. The \
S-matrices describing the scattering of\nphysical excitations in the string \
and mirror models are related to each other\nby an analytic continuation. We \
show that the unitarity requirement for the\nmirror S-matrix fixes the \
S-matrices of both theories essentially uniquely. The\nresulting string \
S-matrix S(z_1,z_2) satisfies the generalized unitarity\ncondition and, up to \
a scalar factor, is a meromorphic function on the elliptic\ncurve associated \
to each variable z. The double Wick rotation is then\naccomplished by \
shifting the variables z by quarter of the imaginary period of\nthe torus. We \
discuss the apparent bound states of the string and mirror\nmodels, and show \
that depending on a choice of the physical region there are\none, two or \
2^{M-1} solutions of the M-particle bound state equations sharing\nthe same \
conserved charges. For very large but finite values of J, most of\nthese \
solutions, however, exhibit various signs of pathological behavior. In\n\
particular, they might receive a finite J correction to their energy which is\
\ncomplex, or the energy correction might exceed corrections arising due to\n\
finite J modifications of the Bethe equations thus making the asymptotic \
Bethe\nansatz inapplicable.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 8 Oct 2007 14:53:59 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 22 Oct 2007 13:09:41 GMT"}, {
