         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "0802.0027", "submitter" -> "Davide Fioravanti", "authors" -> 
            "Diego Bombardelli, Davide Fioravanti and Marco Rossi", "title" -> 
            "Large spin corrections in ${\\cal N}=4$ SYM sl(2): still a \
linear\n  integral equation", "comments" -> 
            "Latex file, typos corrected, 2 figures, appendix, references and\
\n  clarifications added", "journal-ref" -> "Nucl.Phys.B810:460-490,2009", 
            "doi" -> "10.1016/j.nuclphysb.2008.09.030", "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Anomalous dimension and higher conserved charges in the \
$sl(2)$ sector of\n${\\cal N}=4$ SYM for generic spin $s$ and twist $L$ are \
described by using a\nnovel kind of non-linear integral equation (NLIE). The \
latter can be derived\nunder typical situations of the SYM sectors, i.e. when \
the scattering need not\ndepend on the difference of the rapidities and \
these, in their turn, may also\nlie on a bounded range. Here the non-linear \
(finite range) integral terms,\nappearing in the NLIE and in the dimension \
formula, go to zero as $s\\to\n\\infty$. Therefore they can be neglected at \
least up to the $O(s^0)$ order,\nthus implying a linear integral equation \
(LIE) and a linear dimension/charge\nformula respectively, likewise the \
'thermodynamic' (i.e. infinite spin) case.\nImportantly, these non-linear \
terms go faster than any inverse logarithm power\n$(\\ln s)^{-n}$, $n>0$, \
thus extending the linearity validity.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 31 Jan 2008 22:43:57 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Sat, 7 Jun 2008 13:54:01 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
