            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"0803.0943\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Anastasia Doikou\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Anastasia Doikou\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"$A_n^{(1)}$ affine Toda field theories with integrable \
boundary\\n  conditions revisited\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"26 pages, Latex. Typos corrected\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP0805:091,2008\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1088/1126-6708/2008/05/091\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"hep-th math-ph math.MP nlin.SI\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Generic classically integrable boundary conditions for \
the $A_{n}^{(1)}$\\naffine Toda field theories (ATFT) are investigated. The \
present analysis rests\\nprimarily on the underlying algebra, defined by the \
classical version of the\\nreflection equation. We use as a prototype example \
the first non-trivial model\\nof the hierarchy i.e. the $A_2^{(1)}$ ATFT, \
however our results may be\\ngeneralized for any $A_{n}^{(1)}$ ($n>1$). We \
assume here two distinct types of\\nboundary conditions called some times \
soliton preserving (SP), and soliton\\nnon-preserving (SNP) associated to two \
distinct algebras, i.e. the reflection\\nalgebra and the ($q$) twisted \
Yangian respectively. The boundary local\\nintegrals of motion are then \
systematically extracted from the asymptotic\\nexpansion of the associated \
transfer matrix. In the case of SNP boundary\\nconditions we recover \
previously known results. The other type of boundary\\nconditions (SP), \
associated to the reflection algebra, are novel in this\\ncontext and lead to \
a different set of conserved quantities that depend on free\\nboundary \
parameters. It also turns out that the number of local integrals of\\nmotion \
for SP boundary conditions is `double' compared to those of the \
SNP\\ncase.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Thu, 6 Mar 2008 19:06:12 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
