               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Michael Abbott\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Michael C. Abbott, In\\\\^es Aniceto\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Vibrating giant spikes and the large-winding \
sector\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"30 pages, 5 figures. v2 has extra references and \
thanks\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP0806:088,2008\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1088/1126-6708/2008/06/088\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"BROWN-HET 1497\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The single spike is a rigidly rotating classical string \
configuration closely\\nrelated to the giant magnon. We calculate bosonic and \
fermionic modes of this\\nsolution, from which we see that it is not \
supersymmetric. It can be viewed as\\nan excitation above a hoop of string \
wound around the equator, in the same\\nsense that the magnon is an \
excitation above an orbiting point particle. We\\nfind the operator which \
plays the role of the Hamiltonian for this sector,\\nwhich compared to the \
magnon's E-J has the angular momentum replaced by a\\nwinding charge. The \
single spike solution is unstable, and we use the modes to\\nattempt a \
semi-classical computation of its lifetime.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
