            "  We consider correlator of two concentric Wilson loops, a small \
and large ones\nrelated to the problem of flux-tube formation. There are \
three mechanisms which\ncan contribute to the connected correlator and yield \
different dependences on\nthe radius of the small loop. The first one is \
quite standard and concerns\nexchange by supergravity modes. We also consider \
a novel mechanism when the\nflux-tube formation is described by a barrier \
transition in the string\nlanguage, dual to the field-theoretic formulation \
of Yang-Mills theories. The\nmost interesting possibility within this \
approach is resonant tunneling which\nwould enhance the correlator of the \
Wilson loops for particular geometries. The\nthird possibility involves \
exchange by a dyonic string supplied with the string\njunction. We introduce \
also t'Hooft and composite dyonic loops as probes of the\nflux tube. \
Implications for lattice measurements are briefly discussed.\n", 
