        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{18.842212568007813`, -4.114322849346247}, 0.],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"0806.1047\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Pedro Vieira G.\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Joao Penedones, Pedro Vieira\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Toy models for wrapping effects\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"39 pages, 5 figures\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP 0808:020,2008\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1088/1126-6708/2008/08/020\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The anomalous dimensions of local single trace gauge \
invariant operators in\\nN=4 supersymmetric Yang-Mills theory can be computed \
by diagonalizing a long\\nrange integrable Hamiltonian by means of \
perturbative asymptotic Bethe ansatz.\\nThis formalism breaks down when the \
number of fields of the composite operator\\nis smaller than the range of the \
Hamiltonian which coincides with the order in\\nperturbation theory at study. \
We analyze two spin chain toy models which might\\nshed some light on the \
physics behind these wrapping effects. One of them, the\\nHubbard model, is \
known to be closely related to N=4 SYM. In this example, we\\nfind that the \
knowledge of the effective spin chain description is insufficient\\nto \
reconstruct the finite size effects of the underlying electron theory. \
We\\ncompute the wrapping corrections for generic states and relate them to a\
\\nLuscher like approach. The second toy models are long range \
integrable\\nHamiltonians built from the standard algebraic Bethe anstatz \
formalism. This\\nconstruction is valid for any symmetry group. In \
particular, for non-compact\\ngroups it exhibits an interesting relation \
between wrapping interactions and\\ntranscendentality.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Fri, 6 Jun 2008 19:04:09 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2009-12-10\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Penedones\"", ",", "\"Joao\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Vieira\"", ",", "\"Pedro\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}]}], "}"}]}]}}, RowSpacings -> 1, ColumnAlignments -> 
             Left, ColumnAlignments -> Left], Column], 
           Function[BoxForm`e$, 
            TableForm[BoxForm`e$]]]],
         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "0806.1047", "submitter" -> "Pedro Vieira G.", "authors" -> 
            "Joao Penedones, Pedro Vieira", "title" -> 
            "Toy models for wrapping effects", "comments" -> 
            "39 pages, 5 figures", "journal-ref" -> "JHEP 0808:020,2008", 
            "doi" -> "10.1088/1126-6708/2008/08/020", "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  The anomalous dimensions of local single trace gauge invariant \
operators in\nN=4 supersymmetric Yang-Mills theory can be computed by \
diagonalizing a long\nrange integrable Hamiltonian by means of perturbative \
asymptotic Bethe ansatz.\nThis formalism breaks down when the number of \
fields of the composite operator\nis smaller than the range of the \
Hamiltonian which coincides with the order in\nperturbation theory at study. \
We analyze two spin chain toy models which might\nshed some light on the \
physics behind these wrapping effects. One of them, the\nHubbard model, is \
known to be closely related to N=4 SYM. In this example, we\nfind that the \
knowledge of the effective spin chain description is insufficient\nto \
reconstruct the finite size effects of the underlying electron theory. We\n\
compute the wrapping corrections for generic states and relate them to a\n\
Luscher like approach. The second toy models are long range integrable\n\
Hamiltonians built from the standard algebraic Bethe anstatz formalism. This\n\
construction is valid for any symmetry group. In particular, for non-compact\n\
groups it exhibits an interesting relation between wrapping interactions and\n\
transcendentality.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 6 Jun 2008 19:04:09 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2009-12-10", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Penedones", "Joao", ""}, {
              "Vieira", "Pedro", ""}}}], "Tooltip"]& ], 
