         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "0910.5158", "submitter" -> "Axel de Goursac", "authors" -> 
            "Axel de Goursac", "title" -> 
            "Noncommutative geometry, gauge theory and renormalization", 
            "comments" -> 
            "159 pages, 14 figures, PhD thesis, v2: revised version", 
            "journal-ref" -> "Verlag Dr Muller (2010)", "doi" -> Null, 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "math-ph hep-th math.MP math.OA math.QA math.RA", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Nowadays, noncommutative geometry is a growing domain of \
mathematics, which\ncan appear as a promising framework for modern physics. \
Quantum field theories\non \"noncommutative spaces\" are indeed much \
investigated, and suffer from a new\ntype of divergence called the \
ultraviolet-infrared mixing. However, this\nproblem has recently been solved \
by H. Grosse and R. Wulkenhaar by adding to\nthe action of a noncommutative \
scalar model a harmonic term, which renders it\nrenormalizable.\n  One aim of \
this thesis is the extension of this procedure to gauge theories\non the \
Moyal space. Indeed, we have introduced a new noncommutative gauge\ntheory, \
strongly related to the Grosse-Wulkenhaar model, and candidate to\n\
renormalizability. We have then studied the most important properties of this\
\naction, and in particular its vacuum configurations. Finally, we give a\n\
mathematical interpretation of this new action in terms of a derivation-based\
\ndifferential calculus associated to a superalgebra.\n  This work contains \
among the results of this PhD, an introduction to\nnoncommutative geometry, \
an introduction to epsilon-graded algebras, and an\nintroduction to \
renormalization of scalar (wilsonian and BPHZ point of view)\nand gauge \
quantum field theories.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
