               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"0911.2417\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Alin Tirziu\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"A. Tirziu and A.A. Tseytlin\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Semiclassical rigid strings with two spins in \
AdS_5\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"34 pages, 9 figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.81.026006\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Imperial-TP-AT-2009-5\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Semiclassical spinning string states in AdS_5 are, in \
general, characterised\\nby the three SO(2,4) conserved charges: the energy E \
and the two spins S_1 and\\nS_2. We discuss several examples of explicit \
classical solutions for rigid\\nclosed strings of (bended) circular shape \
with two non-zero spins. In\\nparticular, we identify a solution that should \
represent a state that has\\nminimal energy for large values of the two equal \
spins. Similarly to the spiky\\nstring in AdS_3, in the large spin limit this \
string develops long \\\"arcs\\\" that\\nstretch towards the boundary of \
AdS_5. This allows the string to increase the\\nspin while having the energy \
growing only logarithmically with S=S_1 +S_2. The\\nlarge spin asymptotics of \
such solutions is effectively controlled by their\\nnear-boundary parts \
which, as in the spiky string case, happen to be SO(2,4)\\nequivalent to \
segments of the straight folded spinning string. As a result, \
the\\ncoefficient of the \\\\log S term in the string energy should be given, \
up to an\\noverall 3/2 coefficient, by the same universal scaling function \
(cusp anomaly)\\nas in the folded string case, to all orders in the inverse \
string tension or\\nstrong-coupling expansion.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
