            "  Semiclassical spinning string states in AdS_5 are, in general, \
characterised\nby the three SO(2,4) conserved charges: the energy E and the \
two spins S_1 and\nS_2. We discuss several examples of explicit classical \
solutions for rigid\nclosed strings of (bended) circular shape with two \
non-zero spins. In\nparticular, we identify a solution that should represent \
a state that has\nminimal energy for large values of the two equal spins. \
Similarly to the spiky\nstring in AdS_3, in the large spin limit this string \
develops long \"arcs\" that\nstretch towards the boundary of AdS_5. This \
allows the string to increase the\nspin while having the energy growing only \
logarithmically with S=S_1 +S_2. The\nlarge spin asymptotics of such \
solutions is effectively controlled by their\nnear-boundary parts which, as \
in the spiky string case, happen to be SO(2,4)\nequivalent to segments of the \
straight folded spinning string. As a result, the\ncoefficient of the \\log S \
term in the string energy should be given, up to an\noverall 3/2 coefficient, \
by the same universal scaling function (cusp anomaly)\nas in the folded \
string case, to all orders in the inverse string tension or\nstrong-coupling \
expansion.\n", 
