           "id" -> "0911.4061", "submitter" -> "Arthur Lipstein", "authors" -> 
            "Miguel A. Bandres and Arthur E. Lipstein", "title" -> 
            "One-Loop Corrections to Type IIA String Theory in AdS4 x CP3", 
            "comments" -> 
            "58 pages, 3 figures; v3: minor changes and references added,\n  \
published in JHEP", "journal-ref" -> "JHEP 1004:059,2010", "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/JHEP04(2010)059", "report-no" -> "CALT 68-2760", 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We study various methods for computing the one-loop correction \
to the energy\nof classical solutions to type IIA string theory in AdS4 x \
CP3. This involves\ncomputing the spectrum of fluctuations and then adding up \
the fluctuation\nfrequencies. We focus on two classical solutions with \
support in CP3: a\nrotating point-particle and a circular spinning string \
with two angular momenta\nequal to J. For each of these solutions, we compute \
the spectrum of\nfluctuations using two techniques, known as the algebraic \
curve approach and\nthe world-sheet approach. If we use the same prescription \
for adding\nfluctuation frequencies that was used for type IIB string theory \
in AdS5 x S5,\nthen we find that the world-sheet spectrum gives convergent \
one-loop\ncorrections but the algebraic curve spectrum gives divergent ones. \
On the other\nhand, we find a new summation prescription which gives finite \
results when\napplied to both the algebraic curve and world-sheet spectra. \
Naively, this\ngives three predictions for the one-loop correction to the \
spinning string\nenergy (one from the algebraic curve and two from the \
world-sheet), however we\nfind that in the large-j limit (where \
j=J/sqrt(2pi^2 lambda)), the j^(-2n)\nterms in all three cases agree. We \
therefore obtain a unique prediction for the\none-loop correction to the \
spinning string energy.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
