               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Harold Steinacker\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Emergent Geometry and Gravity from Matrix Models: an \
Introduction\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Invited topical review for Classical and Quantum Gravity. \
57 pages, 5\\n  figures. V2,V3: minor corrections and improvements. V4,V5: \
some improvements,\\n  refs added\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Class.Quant.Grav.27:133001,2010\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"10.1088/0264-9381/27/13/133001\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"UWTHPh-2010-4\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th gr-qc\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  A introductory review to emergent noncommutative \
gravity within Yang-Mills\\nMatrix models is presented. Space-time is \
described as a noncommutative brane\\nsolution of the matrix model, i.e. as \
submanifold of \\\\R^D. Fields and matter on\\nthe brane arise as \
fluctuations of the bosonic resp. fermionic matrices around\\nsuch a \
background, and couple to an effective metric interpreted in terms \
of\\ngravity. Suitable tools are provided for the description of the \
effective\\ngeometry in the semi-classical limit. The relation to \
noncommutative gauge\\ntheory and the role of UV/IR mixing is explained. \
Several types of geometries\\nare identified, in particular \\\"harmonic\\\" \
and \\\"Einstein\\\" type of solutions. The\\nphysics of the harmonic branch \
is discussed in some detail, emphasizing the\\nnon-standard role of vacuum \
energy. This may provide new approach to some of\\nthe big puzzles in this \
context. The IKKT model with D=10 and close relatives\\nare singled out as \
promising candidates for a quantum theory of fundamental\\ninteractions \
including gravity.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
