            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  A introductory review to emergent noncommutative gravity \
within Yang-Mills\nMatrix models is presented. Space-time is described as a \
noncommutative brane\nsolution of the matrix model, i.e. as submanifold of \
\\R^D. Fields and matter on\nthe brane arise as fluctuations of the bosonic \
resp. fermionic matrices around\nsuch a background, and couple to an \
effective metric interpreted in terms of\ngravity. Suitable tools are \
provided for the description of the effective\ngeometry in the semi-classical \
limit. The relation to noncommutative gauge\ntheory and the role of UV/IR \
mixing is explained. Several types of geometries\nare identified, in \
particular \"harmonic\" and \"Einstein\" type of solutions. The\nphysics of \
the harmonic branch is discussed in some detail, emphasizing the\n\
non-standard role of vacuum energy. This may provide new approach to some of\n\
the big puzzles in this context. The IKKT model with D=10 and close relatives\
\nare singled out as promising candidates for a quantum theory of fundamental\
\ninteractions including gravity.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
