            "J.G. Russo and A.A. Tseytlin", "title" -> 
            "Large spin expansion of semiclassical 3-point correlators in \
AdS_5 x S^5", "comments" -> "17 pages", "journal-ref" -> "JHEP 1102:029,2011",
             "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP02(2011)029", "report-no" -> 
            "ICCUB-10-199, Imperial-TP-AT-2010-04", "categories" -> "hep-th", 
            "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  3-point correlators in AdS_5xS^5 string theory in which two \
states are\n\"heavy\" (have large quantum numbers) and the third is \"light\" \
(here chosen as\nchiral primary scalar) can be computed semiclassically in \
terms of the \"light\"\nvertex operator evaluated on the classical string \
solution sourced by the two\n\"heavy\" operators. We observe that in the case \
when the \"heavy\" operators\nrepresent BPS states there is an ambiguity in \
the computation depending on\nwhether the mass shell (or marginality) \
condition is imposed before or after\nintegration over the world sheet. We \
show that this ambiguity is resolved in a\nuniversal way by defining the BPS \
correlator as a limit of the one with non-BPS\n\"heavy\" states. We consider \
several examples with \"heavy\" states represented by\nfolded or circular \
spinning strings in AdS_5xS^5 that admit a point-like\nBMN-type limit when \
one S^5 spin J is much larger than the others. Remarkably,\nin all of these \
cases the large J expansion of the 3-point correlator has the\nsame structure \
as expected in perturbative (tree-level and one-loop) dual gauge\ntheory. We \
conjecture that, like the leading chiral primary correlator term,\nthe \
coefficients of the first few subleading terms are also protected, i.e.\n\
should be the same at strong and weak coupling.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 13 Dec 2010 15:29:06 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Wed, 22 Dec 2010 13:35:28 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
