               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Evgeny Buchbinder\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"E.I. Buchbinder and A.A. Tseytlin\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Semiclassical four-point functions in AdS_5 x S^5\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"26 pages, LaTeX; V2: minor corrections; v3: minor \
clarifications\\n  added\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP 1102:072,2011\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP02(2011)072\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Imperial-TP-AT-2010-05\""}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We consider a semiclassical (large string tension ~ \
\\\\lambda^1/2) limit of\\n4-point correlator of two \\\"heavy\\\" vertex \
operators with large quantum numbers\\nand two \\\"light\\\" operators. It \
can be written in a factorized form as a product\\nof two 3-point functions, \
each given by the integrated \\\"light\\\" vertex operator\\non the classical \
string solution determined by the \\\"heavy\\\" operators. We \
check\\nconsistency of this factorization in the case of a correlator with \
two dilatons\\nas \\\"light\\\" operators. We study in detail the example \
when all 4 operators are\\nchiral primary scalars, two of which carry large \
charge J of order of string\\ntension. In the large J limit this correlator \
is nearly extremal. Its\\nsemiclassical expression is, indeed, found to be \
consistent with the general\\nprotected form expected for an extremal \
correlator. We demonstrate explicitly\\nthat our semiclassical result matches \
the large J limit of the known free N=4\\nSYM correlator for 4 chiral primary \
operators with charges J,-J,2,-2; we also\\ncompare it with an existing \
supergravity expression. As an example of a 4-point\\nfunction with two \
non-BPS \\\"heavy\\\" operators, we consider the case when the\\nlatter are \
representing folded spinning with large AdS spin and two \
\\\"light\\\"\\nstates being chiral primary scalars.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
