         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "1012.3740", "submitter" -> "Evgeny Buchbinder", "authors" -> 
            "E.I. Buchbinder and A.A. Tseytlin", "title" -> 
            "Semiclassical four-point functions in AdS_5 x S^5", "comments" -> 
            "26 pages, LaTeX; V2: minor corrections; v3: minor clarifications\
\n  added", "journal-ref" -> "JHEP 1102:072,2011", "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/JHEP02(2011)072", "report-no" -> 
            "Imperial-TP-AT-2010-05", "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We consider a semiclassical (large string tension ~ \
\\lambda^1/2) limit of\n4-point correlator of two \"heavy\" vertex operators \
with large quantum numbers\nand two \"light\" operators. It can be written in \
a factorized form as a product\nof two 3-point functions, each given by the \
integrated \"light\" vertex operator\non the classical string solution \
determined by the \"heavy\" operators. We check\nconsistency of this \
factorization in the case of a correlator with two dilatons\nas \"light\" \
operators. We study in detail the example when all 4 operators are\nchiral \
primary scalars, two of which carry large charge J of order of string\n\
tension. In the large J limit this correlator is nearly extremal. Its\n\
semiclassical expression is, indeed, found to be consistent with the general\n\
protected form expected for an extremal correlator. We demonstrate explicitly\
\nthat our semiclassical result matches the large J limit of the known free \
N=4\nSYM correlator for 4 chiral primary operators with charges J,-J,2,-2; we \
also\ncompare it with an existing supergravity expression. As an example of a \
4-point\nfunction with two non-BPS \"heavy\" operators, we consider the case \
when the\nlatter are representing folded spinning with large AdS spin and two \
\"light\"\nstates being chiral primary scalars.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 16 Dec 2010 20:41:50 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 21 Dec 2010 15:18:23 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v3", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 18 Feb 2011 13:12:26 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2011-03-18", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Buchbinder", "E. I.", ""}, {
              "Tseytlin", "A. A.", ""}}}], "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{18.085512315640344`, -2.339147431458427}, 0.],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1012.3983\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Joseph A. Minahan\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Joseph A. Minahan\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Review of AdS/CFT Integrability, Chapter I.1: Spin Chains \
in N=4 Super\\n  Yang-Mills\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"25 pages, see also overview article arXiv:1012.3982; v2: \
references\\n  to other chapters updated; v3: references added, typos \
fixed\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/s11005-011-0522-9\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"UUITP-38/10\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  In this chapter of \\\"Review of AdS/CFT \
Integrability\\\" we introduce N=4 Super\\nYang-Mills. We discuss the global \
superalagebra PSU(2,2|4) and its action on\\ngauge invariant operators. We \
then discuss the computation of the correlators\\nof certain gauge invariant \
operators, the so-called single trace operators in\\nthe large N limit. We \
show that interactions in the gauge theory lead to mixing\\nof the operators. \
We compute this mixing at the one-loop level and show that\\nthe problem maps \
to a one-dimensional spin chain with nearest neighbor\\ninteractions. For \
operators in the SU(2) sector we show that the spin chain is\\nthe \
ferromagnetic Heisenberg spin chain whose eigenvalues are determined by \
the\\nBethe equations.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
