Donovan Young\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"On the Spectrum of the AdS_5xS^5 String at large \
lambda\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"24 pages. v2: typos fixed\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP 1103:046,2011\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP03(2011)046\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"HU-EP-10/85\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We put forth a program for perturbatively quantizing \
the bosonic sector of\\nthe IIB superstring on AdS_5xS^5 in the large radius \
of curvature R (i.e.\\nflat-space) limit, and in light-cone gauge. Using the \
quantization of the\\nmassive particle on AdS_5xS^5 as a guiding exercise, we \
read off the correct\\nscaling of the particle coordinates in the large \
radius limit. In the\\ncorresponding large \
\\\\sqrt{\\\\lambda}=R^2/\\\\alpha' limit of the string the\\noscillator \
modes must be scaled differently from the zero modes. We compute \
the\\nbosonic string Hamiltonian in this limit which gives the \
flat-space\\nmass-squared operator at leading order, followed by a harmonic \
oscillator\\npotential in the zero modes at subleading order. Using these \
ingredients we\\ncalculate the leading and sub-leading terms in the conformal \
dimension of the\\nlength-four Konishi state in the large-\\\\lambda limit. \
Furthermore we work out\\nthe relevant terms of the energy expansion to \
next-to-next-to leading order,\\nwhich are plagued by severe ordering \
ambiguities. This prevents us from\\ndetermining the corrections to the \
spectrum at order {\\\\lambda}^{-1/4}.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
