          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "1104.2429", "submitter" -> "J. Luis Miramontes", 
            "authors" -> "Timothy J. Hollowood and J. Luis Miramontes", 
            "title" -> 
            "The AdS(5)xS(5) Semi-Symmetric Space Sine-Gordon Theory", 
            "comments" -> "39 pages, references added", "journal-ref" -> 
            "JHEP 1105:136,2011", "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP05(2011)136", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  The generalized symmetric space sine-Gordon theories are a \
series of\n1+1-integrable field theories that are classically equivalent to \
superstrings\non symmetric space spacetimes F/G. They are formulated in terms \
of a\nsemi-symmetric space as a gauged WZW model with fermions and a \
potential term\nto deform it away from the conformal fixed point. We consider \
in particular the\ncase of PSU(2,2|4)/Sp(2,2)xSp(4) which corresponds to \
AdS(5)xS(5). We argue\nthat the infinite tower of conserved charges of these \
theories includes an\nexotic N=(8,8) supersymmetry that is realized in a \
mildy non-local way at the\nLagrangian level. The supersymmetry is associated \
to a double central extension\nof the superalgebra psu(2|2)+psu(2|2) and \
includes a non-trivial R symmetry\nalgebra corresponding to global gauge \
transformations, as well as 2-dimensional\nspacetime translations. We then \
explicitly construct soliton solutions and show\nthat they carry an internal \
moduli superspace CP(2|1)xCP(2|1) with both bosonic\nand Grassmann collective \
coordinates. We show how to semi-classical quantize\nthe solitons by writing \
an effective quantum mechanical system on the moduli\nspace which takes the \
form of a co-adjoint orbit of SU(2|2)xSU(2|2). The\nspectrum consists of a \
tower of massive states in the short, or atypical,\nsymmetric \
representations, just as the giant magnon states of the string world\nsheet \
theory, although here the tower is truncated.\n", 
