            "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  In 2008, Aharony, Bergman, Jafferis, and Maldacena (ABJM) \
discovered a\nthree-dimensional Chern-Simons theory with N = 6 supersymmetry \
and conjectured\nthat in a certain limit, this theory is dual to type IIA \
string theory on\nAdS4xCP3. Since then, a great deal of evidence has been \
accumulated which\nsuggests that the ABJM theory is integrable in the planar \
limit. Integrability\nis a very useful property that allows many physical \
observables, such as\nanomalous dimensions and scattering amplitudes, to be \
computed efficiently. In\nthe first half of this thesis, we will explain how \
to use integrabilty to\ncompute the anomalous dimensions of long, \
single-trace operators in the ABJM\ntheory. In particular, we will describe \
how to compute them at weak coupling\nusing a Bethe Ansatz, and how to \
compute them at strong coupling using string\ntheory. The latter approach \
involves using algebraic curve and world-sheet\ntechniques to compute the \
energies of string states dual to gauge theory\noperators. In the second half \
of this thesis, we will discuss integrability\nfrom the point of view of \
on-shell scattering amplitudes in the ABJM theory. In\nparticular, we will \
describe how to parameterize the amplitudes in terms of\nsupertwistors and \
how to relate higher-point tree-level amplitudes to\nlower-point tree-level \
amplitudes using a recursion relation. We will also\nexplain how this \
recursion relation can be used to show that all tree-level\namplitudes of the \
ABJM theory are invariant under dual superconformal symmetry.\nThis symmetry \
is hidden from the point of the action and implies that the\ntheory has \
Yangian symmetry, which is a key feature of integrability. This\nthesis is \
mainly based on the material in [101], [83], and [84].\n", 
