               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Correlation function of null polygonal Wilson loops with \
local operators\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"36 pages, 2 figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP09(2011)034\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We consider the correlator <W_n O(x)> of a light-like \
polygonal Wilson loop\\nwith n cusps with a local operator (like the dilaton \
or the chiral primary\\nscalar) in planar N =4 super Yang-Mills theory. As a \
consequence of conformal\\nsymmetry, the main part of such correlator is a \
function F of 3n-11 conformal\\nratios. The first non-trivial case is n=4 \
when F depends on just one conformal\\nratio \\\\zeta. This makes the \
corresponding correlator one of the simplest\\nnon-trivial observables that \
one would like to compute for generic values of\\nthe `t Hooft coupling \
\\\\lambda. We compute F(\\\\zeta,\\\\lambda) at leading order in\\nboth the \
strong coupling regime (using semiclassical AdS5 x S5 string theory)\\nand \
the weak coupling regime (using perturbative gauge theory). Some \
results\\nare also obtained for polygonal Wilson loops with more than four \
edges.\\nFurthermore, we also discuss a connection to the relation between a \
correlator\\nof local operators at null-separated positions and cusped Wilson \
loop suggested\\nin arXiv:1007.3243.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
