            "45 pages, 5 figures; v2: minor additions, JHEP version", 
            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP10(2011)112", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            
            "  We provide a first-principles, perturbative derivation of the \
AdS5/CFT4\nY-system that has been proposed to solve the spectrum problem of \
N=4 SYM. The\nproof relies on the computation of quantum effects in the \
fusion of some loop\noperators, namely the transfer matrices. More precisely \
we show that the\nleading quantum corrections in the fusion of transfer \
matrices induce the\ncorrect shifts of the spectral parameter in the \
T-system. As intermediate steps\nwe study UV divergences in line operators up \
to first order and compute the\nfusion of line operators up to second order \
for the pure spinor string in\nAdS5xS5. We also argue that the derivation can \
be easily extended to other\nintegrable models, some of which describe string \
theory on AdS4, AdS3 and AdS2\nspacetimes.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Wed, 24 Aug 2011 20:00:05 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 25 Oct 2011 15:15:03 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2015-05-30", "authors_parsed" -> {{"Benichou", "Raphael", ""}}}],
           "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{17.67247126441736, -6.720214844424493}, 0.],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1109.0872\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Io Kawaguchi\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Io Kawaguchi and Kentaroh Yoshida\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Classical integrability of Schrodinger sigma models and \
q-deformed\\n  Poincare symmetry\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"20 pages, no figure, further clarification and references \
added\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP11(2011)094\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"KUNS-2363\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th math-ph math.MP\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We discuss classical integrable structure of \
two-dimensional sigma models\\nwhich have three-dimensional Schrodinger \
spacetimes as target spaces. The\\nSchrodinger spacetimes are regarded as \
null-like deformations of AdS_3. The\\noriginal AdS_3 isometry SL(2,R)_L x \
SL(2,R)_R is broken to SL(2,R)_L x U(1)_R\\ndue to the deformation. According \
to this symmetry, there are two descriptions\\nto describe the classical \
dynamics of the system, 1) the SL(2,R)_L description\\nand 2) the enhanced \
U(1)_R description. In the former 1), we show that the\\nYangian symmetry is \
realized by improving the SL(2,R)_L Noether current. Then a\\nLax pair is \
constructed with the improved current and the classical\\nintegrability is \
shown by deriving the r/s-matrix algebra. In the latter 2), we\\nfind a \
non-local current by using a scaling limit of warped AdS_3 and that \
it\\nenhances U(1)_R to a q-deformed Poincare algebra. Then another Lax pair \
is\\npresented and the corresponding r/s-matrices are also computed. The \
two\\ndescriptions are equivalent via a non-local map.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
