            "Two-loop corrections to partition function of Pohlmeyer-reduced \
theory\n  for AdS_5 x S^5 superstring", "comments" -> "36 pages, 4 figures", 
            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1103/PhysRevD.84.126017", 
            "report-no" -> "Imperial-TP-YI-2011-01", "categories" -> "hep-th",
             "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            
            "  Pohlmeyer reduction of AdS_5 x S^5 superstring leads to a set \
of equations of\nmotion following from an action containing a bosonic Sp(2,2) \
x Sp(4)/[SU(2)]^4\ngauged WZW term, an integrable potential and a fermionic \
part coupling bosons\nfrom the two factors. The original superstring and the \
reduced model are in\ndirect correspondence at the classical level but their \
relation at the quantum\nlevel remains an open question. As was found \
earlier, the one-loop partition\nfunctions of the two theories computed on \
the respective classical backgrounds\nmatch; here we explore the fate of this \
relation at the two-loop level. We\nconsider the example of the reduced \
theory solution corresponding to the long\nfolded spinning string in AdS. The \
logarithm of the AdS_5 x S^5 superstring\npartition function computed on the \
spinning string background is known to be\nproportional to the universal \
scaling function which depends on the string\ntension ~ \\l^{1/2} where \
\\l=\\lambda is `t Hooft coupling. Its \"quantum\" part is\nf(\\l) = a1 + \
\\l^{-1/2} a2 + ... where the one-loop term is a1 = - 3 \\ln 2 and\nthe \
two-loop term is minus the Catalan's constant, a2=-K. We find that the\n\
counterpart of f(\\l) in the reduced theory is f'(k) =a1' + 2 k^{-1} a2' + \
...,\nwhere k is the coupling of the reduced theory. Here the one-loop \
coefficient is\nthe same as in the string theory, a1'= a1, while the two-loop \
one is a2' =a2 -\n1/4 (a1)^2. Remarkably, the first Catalan's constant term \
here matches the\nstring theory result if we identify the two couplings as k= \
2\\l^{1/2}.\nNevertheless, the presence of the additional (a1)^2 ~ (\\ln 2)^2 \
term implies\nthat a relation between the two quantum partition functions (if \
any) is not a\nsimple equality. Similar results are found in the case of \
AdS_3 x S^3\nsuperstring theory where a1= - 2 \\ln 2 and a2=0, while in the \
corresponding\nreduced theory a1'=a1, a2'= a2- 1/4 (a1)^2.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Sun, 25 Sep 2011 14:15:15 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2013-05-29", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Iwashita", "Y.", ""}, {
              "Roiban", "R.", ""}, {"Tseytlin", "A. A.", ""}}}], 
          "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{19.240052719022, -4.372101403528656}, 0.],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1109.5401\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Manuel Losi\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Manuel Losi\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Spiky strings and the AdS/CFT correspondence\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"200 pages, 37 figures, PhD thesis\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We first focus on the finite-gap formalism for type IIB \
strings in AdS_3 x\\nS^1, which allows to encode the semiclassical spectrum \
of a very large family\\nof string solutions in a Riemann surface, the \
spectral curve. Then, we show\\nthat, in the large angular momentum limit, it \
separates into two distinct\\nsurfaces, allowing the derivation of an \
explicit expression for the spectrum,\\nwhich is correspondingly \
characterised by two separate branches. The latter may\\nbe interpreted in \
terms of two kinds of spikes appearing on the strings:\\n\\\"large\\\" \
spikes, yielding an infinite contribution to the energy and \
angular\\nmomentum of the string, and \\\"small\\\" spikes, representing \
finite excitations\\nover the background of the \\\"large\\\" spikes.\\n  On \
the other side of the AdS/CFT correspondence, we consider the sl(2) \
sector\\nof N=4 super Yang-Mills theory. The corresponding 1-loop spectrum, \
in the large\\nconformal spin limit, is also encoded in a spectral curve and \
characterised in\\nterms of the so-called holes. We show that, with the \
appropriate\\nidentifications and with the usual extrapolation from weak to \
strong 't Hooft\\ncoupling described by the cusp anomalous dimension, the \
large-S spectra of\\ngauge theory and of string theory coincide. Furthermore, \
we explain how \\\"small\\\"\\nand \\\"large\\\" holes may be identified with \
\\\"small\\\" and \\\"large\\\" spikes.\\n  Finally, we discuss several \
explicit spiky string solutions in AdS_3 which\\ndisplay the finite-gap \
spectrum. We compute their spectral curves in the large\\nS limit, finding \
that they correspond to specific regions of the moduli space\\nof the \
finite-gap curves. We also explain how \\\"large\\\" spikes may be used \
in\\norder to extract a discrete system of degrees of freedom from string \
theory,\\nwhich can then be matched with the degrees of freedom of the dual \
gauge theory\\noperators, and how \\\"small\\\" spikes are in fact very \
similar to the Giant Magnons\\nliving in R x S^2.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Sun, 25 Sep 2011 21:34:33 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2011-09-27\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Losi\"", ",", "\"Manuel\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}], "}"}]}]}}, RowSpacings -> 1, ColumnAlignments -> 
