 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  A poorly constrained parameter in the Standard Model of \
Cosmology is the\\nlepton asymmetry l = \\\\sum_f \
l_f=\\\\sum_f(n_f+n_{\\\\nu_f})/s. Each flavour asymmetry\\nl_f with f=e, \
\\\\mu, {\\\\tau} is the sum of the net particle density of the \
charged\\nleptons n_f and their corresponding neutrinos, normalized with the \
entropy\\ndensity s. Constraints on l_f \\\\leq O(0.1) from BBN and CMB allow \
for lepton\\nflavour asymmetries orders of magnitudes larger then the baryon \
asymmetry b ~\\n10^{-10}. In this article we show how such large lepton \
(flavour) asymmetries\\ninfluence the early universe, in particular the \
freeze out of WIMPs and the\\ncosmic QCD transition.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
