            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1112.3309\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Donovan Young\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Donovan Young\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Wilson loops in Five-Dimensional Super-Yang-Mills\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"1+18 pages, 3 figures. v2 added a reference and made \
minor cosmetic\\n  changes, JHEP version. v3 added generalization to \
arbitrary smooth, closed\\n  contours, added references. v4 added references \
and clarified discussion\\n  beneath eq. (23)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP 1202 (2012) 052\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP02(2012)052\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We consider circular non-BPS Maldacena-Wilson loops in \
five-dimensional\\nsupersymmetric Yang-Mills theory (d = 5 SYM) both as \
macroscopic strings in the\\nD4-brane geometry and directly in gauge theory. \
We find that in the Dp-brane\\ngeometries for increasing p, p = 4 is the last \
value for which the radius of\\nthe string worldsheet describing the Wilson \
loop is independent of the UV\\ncut-off. It is also the last value for which \
the area of the worldsheet can be\\n(at least partially) regularized by the \
standard Legendre transformation. The\\nasymptotics of the string worldsheet \
allow the remaining divergence in the\\nregularized area to be determined, \
and it is found to be logarithmic in the UV\\ncut-off. We also consider the \
M2-brane in AdS_7 x S^4 which is the M-theory\\nlift of the Wilson loop, and \
dual to a \\\"Wilson surface\\\" in the (2,0), d = 6\\nCFT. We find that it \
has exactly the same logarithmic divergence in its\\nregularized action. The \
origin of the divergence has been previously understood\\nin terms of a \
conformal anomaly for surface observables in the CFT. Turning to\\nthe gauge \
theory, a similar picture is found in d = 5 SYM. The divergence and\\nits \
coefficient can be recovered for general smooth loops by summing \
the\\nleading divergences in the analytic continuation of dimensional \
regularization\\nof planar rainbow/ladder diagrams. These diagrams are finite \
in 5 - epsilon\\ndimensions. The interpretation is that the Wilson loop is \
renormalized by a\\nfactor containing this leading divergence of \
six-dimensional origin, and also\\nsubleading divergences, and that the \
remaining part of the Wilson loop\\nexpectation value is a finite, \
scheme-dependent quantity. We substantiate this\\nclaim by showing that the \
interacting diagrams at one loop are finite in our\\nregularization scheme in \
d = 5 dimensions, but not for d greater than or equal\\nto 6.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
