            "Donovan Young", "title" -> 
            "Wilson loops in Five-Dimensional Super-Yang-Mills", "comments" -> 
            "1+18 pages, 3 figures. v2 added a reference and made minor \
cosmetic\n  changes, JHEP version. v3 added generalization to arbitrary \
smooth, closed\n  contours, added references. v4 added references and \
clarified discussion\n  beneath eq. (23)", "journal-ref" -> 
            "JHEP 1202 (2012) 052", "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP02(2012)052", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We consider circular non-BPS Maldacena-Wilson loops in \
five-dimensional\nsupersymmetric Yang-Mills theory (d = 5 SYM) both as \
macroscopic strings in the\nD4-brane geometry and directly in gauge theory. \
We find that in the Dp-brane\ngeometries for increasing p, p = 4 is the last \
value for which the radius of\nthe string worldsheet describing the Wilson \
loop is independent of the UV\ncut-off. It is also the last value for which \
the area of the worldsheet can be\n(at least partially) regularized by the \
standard Legendre transformation. The\nasymptotics of the string worldsheet \
allow the remaining divergence in the\nregularized area to be determined, and \
it is found to be logarithmic in the UV\ncut-off. We also consider the \
M2-brane in AdS_7 x S^4 which is the M-theory\nlift of the Wilson loop, and \
dual to a \"Wilson surface\" in the (2,0), d = 6\nCFT. We find that it has \
exactly the same logarithmic divergence in its\nregularized action. The \
origin of the divergence has been previously understood\nin terms of a \
conformal anomaly for surface observables in the CFT. Turning to\nthe gauge \
theory, a similar picture is found in d = 5 SYM. The divergence and\nits \
coefficient can be recovered for general smooth loops by summing the\nleading \
divergences in the analytic continuation of dimensional regularization\nof \
planar rainbow/ladder diagrams. These diagrams are finite in 5 - epsilon\n\
dimensions. The interpretation is that the Wilson loop is renormalized by a\n\
factor containing this leading divergence of six-dimensional origin, and also\
\nsubleading divergences, and that the remaining part of the Wilson loop\n\
expectation value is a finite, scheme-dependent quantity. We substantiate \
this\nclaim by showing that the interacting diagrams at one loop are finite \
in our\nregularization scheme in d = 5 dimensions, but not for d greater than \
or equal\nto 6.\n", 
