            "133 pages, accepted for publication in Reviews in Mathematical\n \
 Physics", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1142/S0129055X12300014", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "hep-th math-ph math.MP math.QA", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  In the following paper, which is based on the authors PhD \
thesis submitted to\nImperial College London, we explore the applicability of \
Yangian symmetry to\nvarious integrable models, in particular, in relation \
with S-matrices. One of\nthe main themes in this work is that, after a \
careful study of the mathematics\nof the symmetry algebras one finds that in \
an integrable model, one can\ndirectly reconstruct S-matrices just from the \
algebra. It has been known for a\nlong time that S-matrices in integrable \
models are fixed by symmetry. However,\nLie algebra symmetry, the Yang-Baxter \
equation, crossing and unitarity, which\nare what constrains the S-matrix in \
integrable models, are often taken to be\nseparate, independent properties of \
the S-matrix. Here, we construct scattering\nmatrices purely from the \
Yangian, showing that the Yangian is the right\nalgebraic object to unify all \
required symmetries of many integrable models. In\nparticular, we reconstruct \
the S-matrix of the principal chiral field, and, up\nto a CDD factor, of \
other integrable field theories with su(n) symmetry.\nFurthermore, we study \
the AdS/CFT correspondence, which is also believed to be\nintegrable in the \
planar limit. We reconstruct the S-matrices at weak and at\nstrong coupling \
from the Yangian or its classical limit. We give a pedagogical\nintroduction \
into the subject, presenting a unified perspective of Yangians and\ntheir \
applications in physics. This paper should hence be accessible to\n\
mathematicians who would like to explore the application of algebraic objects\
\nto physics as well as to physicists interested in a deeper understanding of \
the\nmathematical origin of physical quantities.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 9 Jan 2012 19:37:13 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2015-03-20", "authors_parsed" -> {{"Spill", "Fabian", ""}}}], 
