OSp(4|6)/(SO(1,3)\n  x U(3)) sigma-model", "comments" -> 
            "LaTeX, 22 pages; v2: minor improvements in the text, typos \
corrected,\n  references added", "journal-ref" -> 
            "Int. J. Mod. Phys. A v.27 (2012) 1250118", "doi" -> 
            "10.1142/S0217751X12501187", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Basing upon experience from performing double-dimensional \
reduction of the\nD=11 supermembrane on AdS_4 x S^7 background to Type IIA \
superstring on AdS_4 x\nCP^3 we introduce Kaluza-Klein (partial) \
kappa-symmetry gauge as a vanishing\ncondition of the contribution to the \
D=11 supervielbein components tangent to\nD=10 space-time proportional to the \
differential of the coordinate\nparametrizing compact 11-th space-time \
dimension, that is identified with the\nsupermembrane world-volume compact \
dimension. For AdS_4 x S^7 supermembrane\nKaluza-Klein gauge removes half \
Grassmann coordinates associated with 8\nspace-time supersymmetries, broken \
by the AdS_4 x CP^3 superbackground, by\nimposing D=3 (anti-)Majorana \
condition on them. The consideration relies on the\nrealization of osp(4|8) \
isometry superalgebra of the AdS_4 x S^7\nsuperbackground as D=3 N=8 \
superconformal algebra. Requiring further vanishing\nof the D=10 dilaton \
leaves in the sector of broken supersymmetries just two\nGrassmann \
coordinates organized into D=3 (anti-)Majorana spinor that defines\nminimal \
SL(2,R)-covariant extension of the OSp(4|6)/(SO(1,3)x U(3))\nsigma-model. \
Among 4 possibilities of such a minimal extension we consider in\ndetail one, \
that corresponds to picking out D=3 Majorana coordinate related to\nbroken \
Poincare supersymmetry, and show that the AdS_4 x CP^3 superstring\nequations \
of motion in this partial kappa-symmetry gauge are integrable. Also\nthe \
relation between the OSp(4|6)/(SO(1,3) x U(3)) sigma-model and the AdS_4 x\n\
CP^3 superstring is revisited.\n", 
