                 "\"Holographic three-point functions for short \
operators\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"24 pages; v2: References added, typos fixed, minor \
changes\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP07(2012)187\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"UUITP-02/12\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We consider holographic three-point functions for \
operators dual to short\\nstring states at strong coupling in N=4 super \
Yang-Mills. We treat the states\\nas point-like as they come in from the \
boundary but as strings in the\\ninteraction region in the bulk. The \
interaction position is determined by\\nsaddle point, which is equivalent to \
conservation of the canonical momentum for\\nthe interacting particles, and \
leads to conservation of their conformal\\ncharges. We further show that for \
large dimensions the rms size of the\\ninteraction region is small compared \
to the radius of curvature of the AdS\\nspace, but still large compared to \
the string Compton wave-length. Hence, one\\ncan approximate the string \
vertex operators as flat-space vertex operators with\\na definite momentum, \
which depends on the conformal and R-charges of the\\noperator. We then argue \
that the string vertex operator dual to a primary\\noperator is chosen by \
satisfying a twisted version of Q^L=Q^R, up to spurious\\nterms. This leads \
to a unique choice for a scalar vertex operator with the\\nappropriate \
charges at the first massive level. We then comment on some\\nfeatures of the \
corresponding three-point functions, including the application\\nof these \
results to Konishi operators.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
