               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP11(2012)058\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"SI-HEP-2012-12\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th hep-ph\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study the velocity-dependent cusp anomalous \
dimension in supersymmetric\\nYang-Mills theory. In a paper by Correa, \
Maldacena, Sever, and one of the\\npresent authors, a scaling limit was \
identified in which the ladder diagrams\\nare dominant and are mapped onto a \
Schrodinger problem. We show how to solve\\nthe latter in perturbation theory \
and provide an algorithm to compute the\\nsolution at any loop order. The \
answer is written in terms of harmonic\\npolylogarithms. Moreover, we give \
evidence for two curious properties of the\\nresult. Firstly, we observe that \
the result can be written using a subset of\\nharmonic polylogarithms only, \
at least up to six loops. Secondly, we show that\\nin a light-like limit, \
only single zeta values appear in the asymptotic\\nexpansion, again up to six \
loops. We then extend the analysis of the scaling\\nlimit to systematically \
include subleading terms. This leads to a\\nSchrodinger-type equation, but \
with an inhomogeneous term. We show how its\\nsolution can be computed in \
perturbation theory, in a way similar to the\\nleading order case. Finally, \
we analyze the strong coupling limit of these\\nsubleading contributions and \
compare them to the string theory answer. We find\\nagreement between the two \
calculations.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
