 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study holographic entanglement entropy in the \
background of charged\\ndilatonic black holes which can be viewed as \
holographic duals of certain\\nfinite density states of N=4 super Yang-Mills. \
These charged black holes are\\ndistinguished in that they have vanishing \
ground state entropy. The\\nentanglement entropy for a slab experiences a \
second order phase transition as\\nthe thickness of the slab is varied, while \
the entanglement entropy for a\\nsphere is a smooth function of the radius. \
This suggests that the second scale\\nintroduced by the anisotropy of the \
slab plays an important role in driving the\\nphase transition. In both cases \
we do not observe any logarithmic violation of\\nthe area law indicative of \
hidden Fermi surfaces. We investigate how these\\nresults are affected by the \
inclusion of the Gauss-Bonnet term in the bulk\\ngravitational action. We \
also observe that such addition to the bulk action\\ndoes not change the \
logarithmic violation of the area law in the backgrounds\\nwith hyperscaling \
violation.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
