            "84 pages, 35 figures, 2 tables; submitted to \
Prog.Part.Nucl.Phys;\n  typos removed, wording corrected, references added, \
some parts significantly\n  elaborated", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null,
             "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-ph nucl-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We review theoretical approaches to explore the phase diagram \
of nuclear and\nquark matter at high baryon density. We first look over the \
basic properties of\nquantum chromodynamics (QCD) and address how to describe \
various states of QCD\nmatter. In our discussions on nuclear matter we cover \
the relativistic\nmean-field model, the chiral perturbation theory, and the \
approximation based\non the large-Nc limit where Nc is the number of colors. \
We then explain the\nliquid-gas phase transition and the inhomogeneous meson \
condensation in nuclear\nmatter with emphasis put on the relevance to quark \
matter. We commence the next\npart focused on quark matter with the bootstrap \
model and the Hagedorn\ntemperature. Then we turn to properties associated \
with chiral symmetry and\nexposit theoretical descriptions of the chiral \
phase transition. There emerge\nsome quark-matter counterparts of phenomena \
seen in nuclear matter such as the\nliquid-gas phase transition and the \
inhomogeneous structure of the chiral\ncondensate. The third regime that is \
being recognized recently is what is\ncalled quarkyonic matter, which has \
both aspects of nuclear and quark matter.\nWe closely elucidate the basic \
idea of quarkyonic matter in the large-Nc limit\nand its physics \
implications. Finally, we discuss some experimental indications\nfor the QCD \
phase diagram and close the review with outlooks.\n", 
