         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "1302.0555", "submitter" -> "Amit Dekel", "authors" -> 
            "Amit Dekel", "title" -> 
            "Algebraic Curves for Factorized String Solutions", "comments" -> 
            "22 pages", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/JHEP04(2013)119", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We show how to construct an algebraic curve for factorized \
string solution in\nthe context of the AdS/CFT correspondence. We define \
factorized solutions to be\nsolutions where the flat-connection becomes \
independent of one of the\nworldsheet variables by a similarity \
transformation with a matrix $S$\nsatisfying $S^{-1}d S=const$. Using the \
factorization property we construct a\nwell defined Lax operator and an \
associated algebraic curve. The construction\nprocedure is local and does not \
require the introduction of a monodromy matrix.\nThe procedure can be applied \
for string solutions with any boundary conditions.\nWe study the properties \
of the curve and give several examples for the\napplication of the procedure.\
\n", "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 4 Feb 2013 00:16:19 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2015-06-12", "authors_parsed" -> {{"Dekel", "Amit", ""}}}], 
          "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{8.709057067557168, -5.689531213164299}, 0.],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1302.0859\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Takeshi Morita\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Gautam Mandal and Takeshi Morita\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Quantum quench in matrix models: Dynamical phase \
transitions, Selective\\n  equilibration and the Generalized Gibbs \
Ensemble\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"46 pages, 28 figures; v2: minor corrections, references \
added;\\n  related movies are on\\n  \
http://www2.yukawa.kyoto-u.ac.jp/~mtakeshi/MQM/index.html\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP10(2013)197\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"TIFR/TH/09-14, KEK-TH-1603\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"hep-th cond-mat.stat-mech\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Quantum quench dynamics is considered in a one \
dimensional unitary matrix\\nmodel with a single trace potential. This model \
is integrable and has been\\nstudied in the context of non-critical string \
theory. We find dynamical phase\\ntransitions, and study the role of the \
quantum critical point. In course of the\\ntime evolutions, we find evidence \
of selective equilibration for a certain\\nclass of observables. The \
equilibrium is governed by the Generalized Gibbs\\nEnsemble (GGE) and differs \
from the standard Gibbs ensemble. We compute the\\nproduction of entropy \
which is O(N) for large N matrices. An important feature\\nof the \
equilibration is the appearance of an energy cascade, reminiscent of \
the\\nRichardson cascade in turbulence, where we find flow of energy from \
initial\\nlong wavelength modes to progressively shorter wavelength \
excitations. We\\ndiscuss possible implication of the equilibration and of \
GGE in string theories\\nand higher spin theories. In another related study, \
we compute time evolutions\\nin a double trace unitary matrix model, which \
arises as an effective theory of\\nD2 branes in IIA string theory in the \
confinement phase. We find similar\\nequilibrations and dynamical transitions \
in this matrix model. The dynamical\\ntransitions are related to \
Gregory-Laflamme transitions in string theory and\\nare potentially connected \
with the issue of appearance of naked singularities.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Mon, 4 Feb 2013 21:01:52 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v2\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Thu, 6 Jun 2013 14:44:51 GMT\""}]}], "}"}]}], 
                   "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2015-06-12\""}]}, {
