            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Quantum quench dynamics is considered in a one dimensional \
unitary matrix\nmodel with a single trace potential. This model is integrable \
and has been\nstudied in the context of non-critical string theory. We find \
dynamical phase\ntransitions, and study the role of the quantum critical \
point. In course of the\ntime evolutions, we find evidence of selective \
equilibration for a certain\nclass of observables. The equilibrium is \
governed by the Generalized Gibbs\nEnsemble (GGE) and differs from the \
standard Gibbs ensemble. We compute the\nproduction of entropy which is O(N) \
for large N matrices. An important feature\nof the equilibration is the \
appearance of an energy cascade, reminiscent of the\nRichardson cascade in \
turbulence, where we find flow of energy from initial\nlong wavelength modes \
to progressively shorter wavelength excitations. We\ndiscuss possible \
implication of the equilibration and of GGE in string theories\nand higher \
spin theories. In another related study, we compute time evolutions\nin a \
double trace unitary matrix model, which arises as an effective theory of\nD2 \
branes in IIA string theory in the confinement phase. We find similar\n\
equilibrations and dynamical transitions in this matrix model. The dynamical\n\
transitions are related to Gregory-Laflamme transitions in string theory and\n\
are potentially connected with the issue of appearance of naked \
singularities.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
