                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"50 pages; paper as published in JHEP; added a comment on \
AdS String/\\n  Gauge Theory correspondence in the abstract; a factor of 1/2 \
in the\\n  definition of the ASD correlator corrected\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP 08 (2013) 064\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP08(2013)064\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th hep-lat hep-ph\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We point out that perturbation theory in conjunction \
with the renormalization\\ngroup (RG) puts a severe constraint on the \
structure of the large-N\\nnon-perturbative glueball propagators in SU(N) \
pure YM, in QCD and in n=1 SUSY\\nQCD with massless quarks, or in any \
confining asymptotically-free gauge theory\\nmassless in perturbation theory. \
For the scalar and pseudoscalar glueball\\npropagators in pure YM and QCD \
with massless quarks we check in detail the\\nRG-improved estimate to the \
order of the leading and next-to-leading logarithms\\nby means of a \
remarkable three-loop computation by Chetyrkin et al. We\\ninvestigate as to \
whether the aforementioned constraint is satisfied by any of\\nthe scalar or \
pseudoscalar glueball propagators computed in the framework of\\nthe AdS \
String/ large-N Gauge Theory correspondence and of a recent proposal\\nbased \
on a Topological Field Theory underlying the large-N limit of YM. We \
find\\nthat none of the proposals for the scalar or the pseudoscalar glueball\
\\npropagators based on the AdS String/ large-N Gauge Theory \
correspondence\\nsatisfies the constraint, actually as expected, since the \
gravity side of the\\ncorrespondence is in fact strongly coupled in the \
ultraviolet. On the contrary,\\nthe Topological Field Theory satisfies the \
constraint that follows by the\\nasymptotic freedom.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
