               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Martin Kruczenski\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Martin Kruczenski and Sannah Ziama\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Wilson loops and Riemann theta functions II\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"LaTeX, 45 pages, 10 figures. v2: typos corrected, \
references added\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP05(2014)037\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  In this paper we extend and simplify previous results \
regarding the\\ncomputation of Euclidean Wilson loops in the context of the \
AdS/CFT\\ncorrespondence, or, equivalently, the problem of finding minimal \
area surfaces\\nin hyperbolic space (Euclidean AdS3). If the Wilson loop is \
given by a boundary\\ncurve X(s) we define, using the integrable properties \
of the system, a family\\nof curves X(lambda,s) depending on a complex \
parameter lambda known as the\\nspectral parameter. This family has \
remarkable properties. As a function of\\nlambda, X(lambda,s) has cuts and \
therefore is appropriately defined on a\\nhyperelliptic Riemann surface, \
namely it determines the spectral curve of the\\nproblem. Moreover, \
X(lambda,s) has an essential singularity at the origin\\nlambda=0. The \
coefficients of the expansion of X(lambda,s) around lambda=0,\\nwhen \
appropriately integrated along the curve give the area of the\\ncorresponding \
minimal area surface.\\n  Furthermore we show that the same construction \
allows the computation of\\ncertain surfaces with one or more boundaries \
corresponding to Wilson loop\\ncorrelators. We extend the area formula for \
that case and give some concrete\\nexamples. As the main example we consider \
a surface ending on two concentric\\ncircles and show how the boundary \
circles can be deformed by introducing extra\\ncuts in the spectral curve.\\n\
\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
