           "id" -> "1311.4950", "submitter" -> "Martin Kruczenski", "authors" -> 
            "Martin Kruczenski and Sannah Ziama", "title" -> 
            "Wilson loops and Riemann theta functions II", "comments" -> 
            "LaTeX, 45 pages, 10 figures. v2: typos corrected, references \
added", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP05(2014)037", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  In this paper we extend and simplify previous results \
regarding the\ncomputation of Euclidean Wilson loops in the context of the \
AdS/CFT\ncorrespondence, or, equivalently, the problem of finding minimal \
area surfaces\nin hyperbolic space (Euclidean AdS3). If the Wilson loop is \
given by a boundary\ncurve X(s) we define, using the integrable properties of \
the system, a family\nof curves X(lambda,s) depending on a complex parameter \
lambda known as the\nspectral parameter. This family has remarkable \
properties. As a function of\nlambda, X(lambda,s) has cuts and therefore is \
appropriately defined on a\nhyperelliptic Riemann surface, namely it \
determines the spectral curve of the\nproblem. Moreover, X(lambda,s) has an \
essential singularity at the origin\nlambda=0. The coefficients of the \
expansion of X(lambda,s) around lambda=0,\nwhen appropriately integrated \
along the curve give the area of the\ncorresponding minimal area surface.\n  \
Furthermore we show that the same construction allows the computation of\n\
certain surfaces with one or more boundaries corresponding to Wilson loop\n\
correlators. We extend the area formula for that case and give some concrete\n\
examples. As the main example we consider a surface ending on two concentric\n\
circles and show how the boundary circles can be deformed by introducing \
extra\ncuts in the spectral curve.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
