            "title" -> 
            "Bethe Ansatz for Yangian Invariants: Towards Super Yang-Mills \
Scattering\n  Amplitudes", "comments" -> 
            "56 pages, 14 figures; v2: appendix B added, references updated \
and\n  typos fixed; v3: published version", "journal-ref" -> 
            "Nucl. Phys. B 883 (2014) 373", "doi" -> 
            "10.1016/j.nuclphysb.2014.03.015", "report-no" -> 
            "HU-Mathematik-2013-14, HU-EP-13/34, AEI-2013-234, DCPT-13/47", 
            "categories" -> 
            "math-ph cond-mat.stat-mech hep-th math.MP nlin.SI", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We propose that Baxter's Z-invariant six-vertex model at the \
rational gl(2)\npoint on a planar but in general not rectangular lattice \
provides a way to\nstudy Yangian invariants. These are identified with \
eigenfunctions of certain\nmonodromies of an auxiliary inhomogeneous spin \
chain. As a consequence they are\nspecial solutions to the eigenvalue problem \
of the associated transfer matrix.\nExcitingly, this allows to construct them \
using Bethe ansatz techniques.\nConceptually, our construction generalizes to \
general (super) Lie algebras and\ngeneral representations. Here we present \
the explicit form of sample invariants\nfor totally symmetric, \
finite-dimensional representations of gl(n) in terms of\noscillator algebras. \
In particular, we discuss invariants of three- and\nfour-site monodromies \
that can be understood respectively as intertwiners of\nthe bootstrap and \
Yang-Baxter equation. We state a set of functional relations\nsignificant for \
these representations of the Yangian and discuss their\nsolutions in terms of \
Bethe roots. They arrange themselves into exact strings\nin the complex \
plane. In addition, it is shown that the sample invariants can\nbe expressed \
analogously to Grassmannian integrals. This aspect is closely\nrelated to a \
recent on-shell formulation of scattering amplitudes in planar N=4\nsuper \
Yang-Mills theory.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
