               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"9 pages, latex; v2:13 pages. A Section on the fractional \
brane setup\\n  has been added. Clarifications and references added; v3: \
published version,\\n  references added\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP 1403 (2014) 072\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP03(2014)072\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"IFUP-TH/2013-25\""}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We precisely reproduce the perimeter law obeyed by \
Wilson loops on large\\nspatial contours in planar N=2 SYM at strong \
coupling, as recently deduced\\nusing localization, by means of a dual \
holographic model. The relevant\\nsupergravity background is sourced by \
D5-branes wrapped on a two-sphere in a\\nCalabi-Yau two-manifold. Thus, \
localization and holography are cross-checked,\\nfor the first time, in a non \
conformal context where the gravity background is\\nnot asymptotically Anti \
de Sitter and the dual gauge theory has a\\nlogarithmically running coupling. \
We also notice that the same cross-check can\\nbe performed considering an \
alternative holographic description of N=2 SYM\\nbased on a background \
sourced by fractional D3-branes.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
