           "id" -> "1406.4945", "submitter" -> "Martin Kruczenski", "authors" -> 
            "Martin Kruczenski", "title" -> 
            "Wilson loops and minimal area surfaces in hyperbolic space", 
            "comments" -> "LateX, 39 pages, 1 figure", "journal-ref" -> Null, 
            "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP11(2014)065", "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  The AdS/CFT correspondence relates Wilson loops in $N$=4 SYM \
theory to\nminimal area surfaces in AdS space. If the loop is a plane curve \
the minimal\nsurface lives in hyperbolic space $H_3$ (or equivalently \
Euclidean AdS$_3$\nspace). We argue that finding the area of such extremal \
surface can be easily\ndone if we solve the following problem: given two real \
periodic functions\n$V_{0,1}(s)$, $V_{0,1}(s+2\\pi)=V_{0,1}(s)$, a third \
periodic function $V_2(s)$\nis to be found such that all solutions to the \
equation $- \\phi\"(s) +\n\\big[V_0+{1\\over 2} (\\lambda+{1 \\over \
\\lambda}) V_1 + {i\\over 2} (\\lambda-{1\n\\over \\lambda}) V_2\\big] \
\\phi(s)=0$ are anti-periodic in $s\\in[0,2\\pi]$ for any\nvalue of \
$\\lambda$. This problem is equivalent to the statement that the\nmonodromy \
matrix is trivial. It can be restated as that of finding a one\ncomplex \
parameter family of curves $X(\\lambda,s)$ where $X(\\lambda=1,s)$ is the\n\
given shape of the Wilson loop and such that the Schwarzian derivative\n\
$\\{X(\\lambda,s),s\\}$ is meromorphic in $\\lambda$ with only two simple \
poles. We\npresent a formula for the area in terms of the functions \
$V_{0,1,2}$ and\ndiscuss solutions to these equivalent problems in terms of \
theta functions.\nFinally, we also consider the near circular Wilson loop \
clarifying its\nintegrability properties and rederiving its area using the \
methods described in\nthis paper.\n", 
