            "Resurgent Analysis of Localizable Observables in Supersymmetric \
Gauge\n  Theories", "comments" -> 
            "53 pages, 8 figures; v2: added refs, small changes, improved\n  \
discussion on resurgence triangle, final version for JHEP", "journal-ref" -> 
            Null, "doi" -> Null, "report-no" -> "CERN-PH-TH-2014-118", 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Localization methods have recently led to a plethora of new \
exact results in\nsupersymmetric gauge theories, as certain observables may \
be computed in terms\nof matrix integrals. These can then be evaluated by \
making use of standard\nlarge N techniques, or else via perturbative \
expansions in the gauge coupling.\nEither approximation often leads to \
observables given in terms of asymptotic\nseries, which need to be properly \
defined in order to obtain nonperturbative\nresults. At the same time, \
resurgent analysis has recently been successfully\napplied to several \
problems, e.g., in quantum, field and string theories,\nprecisely to overcome \
this issue and construct nonperturbative answers out of\nasymptotic \
perturbative expansions. The present work uses exact results from\n\
supersymmetric localization to address the resurgent structure of the free\n\
energy and partition function of Chern-Simons and ABJM gauge theories in \
three\ndimensions, and of N=2 supersymmetric Yang-Mills theories in four \
dimensions.\nFor each case, the complete structure of Borel singularities is \
exactly\ndetermined, and the relation of these singularities with the \
large-order\nbehavior of (multi-instanton) perturbative expansions is made \
fully precise.\n", 
