            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP11(2015)086", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th math-ph math.MP", 
            "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  The exact partition function in ABJM theory on three-sphere \
can be regarded\nas a canonical partition function of a non-interacting \
Fermi-gas with an\nunconventional Hamiltonian. All the information on the \
partition function is\nencoded in the discrete spectrum of this Hamiltonian. \
We explain how (quantum\nmechanical) non-perturbative corrections in the \
Fermi-gas system appear from a\nspectral consideration. Basic tools in our \
analysis are a Mellin-Barnes type\nintegral representation and a spectral \
zeta function. From a consistency with\nknown results, we conjecture that the \
spectral zeta function in the ABJM\nFermi-gas has an infinite number of \
\"non-perturbative\" poles, which are\ninvisible in the semi-classical \
expansion of the Planck constant. We observe\nthat these poles indeed appear \
after summing up perturbative corrections. As a\nconsequence, the \
perturbative resummation of the spectral zeta function causes\n\
non-perturbative corrections to the grand canonical partition function. We \
also\npresent another example associated with a spectral problem in \
topological\nstring theory. A conjectured non-perturbative free energy on the \
resolved\nconifold is successfully reproduced in this framework.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 26 Mar 2015 20:04:01 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Sat, 4 Jun 2016 10:21:36 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2016-06-07", "authors_parsed" -> {{"Hatsuda", "Yasuyuki", ""}}}],
           "Tooltip"]& ], 
