               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Mirror Symmetry And Loop Operators\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"92 pages, v2: minor clarifications in the introduction, \
to be\\n  published in JHEP\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Wilson loops in gauge theories pose a fundamental \
challenge for dualities.\\nWilson loops are labeled by a representation of \
the gauge group and should map\\nunder duality to loop operators labeled by \
the same data, yet generically, dual\\ntheories have completely different \
gauge groups. In this paper we resolve this\\nconundrum for three dimensional \
mirror symmetry. We show that Wilson loops are\\nexchanged under mirror \
symmetry with Vortex loop operators, whose microscopic\\ndefinition in terms \
of a supersymmetric quantum mechanics coupled to the theory\\nencode in a \
non-trivial way a representation of the original gauge group,\\ndespite that \
the gauge groups of mirror theories can be radically different.\\nOur \
predictions for the mirror map, which we derive guided by branes in \
string\\ntheory, are confirmed by the computation of the exact expectation \
value of\\nWilson and Vortex loop operators on the three-sphere.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
