            "Michael Cooke, Nadav Drukker, Diego Trancanelli", "title" -> 
            "A profusion of $1/2$ BPS Wilson loops in $\\mathcal{N}=4$\n  \
Chern-Simons-matter theories", "comments" -> "40 pages", "journal-ref" -> 
            "JHEP 1510 (2015) 140", "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP10(2015)140", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We initiate the study of $1/2$ BPS Wilson loops in \
$\\mathcal{N}=4$\nChern-Simons-matter theories in three dimensions. We \
consider a circular or\nlinear quiver with Chern-Simons levels $k$, $-k$ and \
$0$, and focus on loops\npreserving one of the two $SU(2)$ subgroups of the \
$R$-symmetry. In the cases\nwith no vanishing Chern-Simons levels, we find a \
pair of Wilson loops for each\npair of adjacent nodes on the quiver connected \
by a hypermultiplet (nodes\nconnected by twisted hypermultiplets have Wilson \
loops preserving another set\nof supercharges). We expect this classical \
pairwise degeneracy to be lifted by\nquantum corrections. In the case with \
nodes with vanishing Chern-Simons terms\nconnected by twisted \
hypermultiplets, we find that the usual $1/4$ BPS Wilson\nloops are \
automatically enlarged to $1/2$ BPS, as happens also in 3-dimensional\n\
Yang-Mills theory. When the nodes with vanishing Chern-Simons levels are\n\
connected by untwisted hypermultiplets, we do not find any Wilson loops\n\
coupling to those nodes which are classically invariant. Rather, we find\n\
several loops whose supersymmetry variation, while non zero, vanishes in any\n\
correlation function, so is weakly zero. We expect only one linear \
combination\nof those Wilson loops to remain BPS when quantum corrections are \
included. We\nanalyze the M-theory duals of those Wilson loops and comment on \
their\ndegeneracy. We also show that these Wilson loops are cohomologically \
equivalent\nto certain $1/4$ BPS Wilson loops whose expectation value can be \
evaluated by\nthe appropriate localized matrix model.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
