            "authors" -> 
            "Andrey Grozin, Johannes M. Henn, Gregory P. Korchemsky, Peter \
Marquard", "title" -> 
            "The three-loop cusp anomalous dimension in QCD and its \
supersymmetric\n  extensions", "comments" -> 
            "63 pages, 14 figures; v2: minor changes, references added; v3: \
minor\n  changes, eq.(D.7) corrected", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/JHEP01(2016)140", "report-no" -> 
            "DESY 15-186, IPhT-T15/174, MITP/15-088", "categories" -> 
            "hep-ph hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We present the details of the analytic calculation of the \
three-loop\nangle-dependent cusp anomalous dimension in QCD and its \
supersymmetric\nextensions, including the maximally supersymmetric \
$\\mathcal{N}=4$ super\nYang-Mills theory. The three-loop result in the \
latter theory is new and\nconfirms a conjecture made in our previous paper. \
We study various physical\nlimits of the cusp anomalous dimension and discuss \
its relation to the\nquark-antiquark potential including the effects of \
broken conformal symmetry in\nQCD. We find that the cusp anomalous dimension \
viewed as a function of the cusp\nangle and the new effective coupling given \
by light-like cusp anomalous\ndimension reveals a remarkable universality \
property -- it takes the same form\nin QCD and its supersymmetric extensions, \
to three loops at least. We exploit\nthis universality property and make use \
of the known result for the three-loop\nquark-antiquark potential to predict \
the special class of nonplanar corrections\nto the cusp anomalous dimensions \
at four loops. Finally, we also discuss in\ndetail the computation of all \
necessary Wilson line integrals up to three loops\nusing the method of \
leading singularities and differential equations.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
