            "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  In this work we obtain the non-abelian T-dual geometry of the \
well-known\nPilch-Warner supergravity solution in its infrared point. We \
derive the dual\nmetric and the NS two-form by gauging the isometry group of \
the initial theory\nand integrating out the introduced auxiliary gauge \
fields. Then we use the\nFourier-Mukai transform from algebraic geometry to \
find the transformation\nrules of the R-R fields. The dual background \
preserves the $\\mathcal{N}=1$\nsupersymmetry of the original one due to the \
fact that the Killing spinor does\nnot depend on the directions on which the \
N-AT-D is performed. Finally, we\nconsider two different pp-wave limits of \
the T-dual geometry by performing\nPenrose limits for two light-like \
geodesics.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Sun, 1 Nov 2015 16:33:05 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Wed, 13 Jan 2016 18:11:20 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v3", "created" -> 
               "Sat, 6 Aug 2016 13:06:48 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2016-08-25", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Dimov", "Hristo", ""}, {
              "Mladenov", "Stefan", ""}, {"Rashkov", "Radoslav C.", ""}, {
              "Vetsov", "Tsvetan", ""}}}], "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{12.938515621852705`, -7.015949581278555}, 0.],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1511.00616\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Bartomeu Fiol\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Bartomeu Fiol, Blai Garolera and Genis Torrents\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Probing N=2 superconformal field theories with \
localization\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"20 pages\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP01(2016)168\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We use supersymmetric localization to study probes of \
four dimensional\\nLagrangian N=2 superconformal field theories. We first \
derive a unique equation\\nfor the eigenvalue density of these theories. We \
observe that these theories\\nhave a Wigner eigenvalue density precisely when \
they satisfy a necessary\\ncondition for having a holographic dual with a \
sensible higher-derivative\\nexpansion. We then compute in the saddle-point \
approximation the vacuum\\nexpectation value of 1/2-BPS circular Wilson \
loops, and the two-point functions\\nof these Wilson loops with the \
Lagrangian density and with the stress-energy\\ntensor. This last computation \
also provides the corresponding Bremsstrahlung\\nfunctions and entanglement \
entropies. As expected, whenever a finite fraction\\nof the matter is in the \
fundamental representation, the results are drastically\\ndifferent from \
those of N=4 supersymmetric Yang-Mills theory.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Mon, 2 Nov 2015 17:55:02 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
              
