            "hep-th math-ph math.MP", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  In this paper, we develop a new method of computing \
three-point functions in\nthe SU(2) sector of the $\\mathcal{N}=4$ super \
Yang-Mills theory in the\nsemi-classical regime at weak coupling, which \
closely parallels the strong\ncoupling analysis. The structure threading two \
disparate regimes is the\nso-called monodromy relation, an identity \
connecting the three-point functions\nwith and without the insertion of the \
monodromy matrix. We shall show that this\nrelation can be put to use \
directly for the semi-classical regime, where the\ndynamics is governed by \
the classical Landau-Lifshitz sigma model.\nSpecifically, it reduces the \
problem to a set of functional equations, which\ncan be solved once the \
analyticity in the spectral parameter space is\nspecified. To determine the \
analyticity, we develop a new universal logic\napplicable at both weak and \
strong couplings. As a result, compact\nsemi-classical formulas are obtained \
for a general class of three-point\nfunctions at weak coupling including the \
ones whose semi-classical behaviors\nwere not known before. In addition, the \
new analyticity argument applied to the\nstrong coupling analysis leads to a \
modification of the integration contour,\nproducing the results consistent \
with the recent hexagon bootstrap approach.\nThis modification also makes the \
Frolov-Tseytlin limit perfectly agree with the\nweak coupling form.\n", 
