           "id" -> "1603.03596", "submitter" -> "Matteo Beccaria", "authors" -> 
            "Matteo Beccaria, Giorgio Metafune, Diego Pallara", "title" -> 
            "The ground state of long-range Schrodinger equations and static\n\
  $q\\bar{q}$ potential", "comments" -> "30 pages, 1 figure", "journal-ref" -> 
            Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP05(2016)040", "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Motivated by the recent results in arXiv:1601.05679 about the \
quark-antiquark\npotential in $\\mathcal N=4$ SYM, we reconsider the problem \
of computing the\nasymptotic weak-coupling expansion of the ground state \
energy of a certain\nclass of 1d Schr\\\"odinger operators \
$-\\frac{d^{2}}{dx^{2}}+\\lambda\\,V(x)$ with\nlong-range potential $V(x)$. \
In particular, we consider even potentials obeying\n$\\int_{\\mathbb R}dx\\, \
V(x)<0$ with large $x$ asymptotics $V\\sim\n-a/x^{2}-b/x^{3}+\\cdots$. The \
associated Schr\\\"odinger operator is known to\nadmit a bound state for \
$\\lambda\\to 0^{+}$, but the binding energy is\nrigorously non-analytic at $\
\\lambda=0$. Its asymptotic expansion starts at\norder $\\mathcal \
O(\\lambda)$, but contains higher corrections\n\
$\\lambda^{n}\\,\\log^{m}\\lambda$ with all $0\\le m\\le n-1$ and standard\n\
Rayleigh-Schr\\\"odinger perturbation theory fails order by order in \
$\\lambda$.\nWe discuss various analytical tools to tame this problem and \
provide the\ngeneral expansion of the binding energy at $\\mathcal \
O(\\lambda^{3})$ in terms\nof quadratures. The method is tested on a soluble \
potential that is fully under\ncontrol, and on various non-soluble cases as \
well. A supersymmetric case,\narising in the study of the quark-antiquark \
potential in $\\mathcal N=6$ ABJ(M)\ntheory, is also exploited to provide a \
further non-trivial consistency check.\nOur analytical results confirm at \
third order a remarkable exponentiation of\nthe leading infrared logarithms, \
first noticed in $\\mathcal N=4$ SYM where it\nmay be proved by \
Renormalization Group arguments. We prove this interesting\nfeature at all \
orders at the level of the Schr\\\"odinger equation for general\npotentials \
in the considered class.\n", 
