               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We give a pedagogical introduction to the methodology \
of the Quench Action,\\nwhich is an effective representation for the \
calculation of time-dependent\\nexpectation values of physical operators \
following a generic out-of-equilibrium\\nstate preparation protocol (for \
example a quantum quench). The representation,\\noriginally introduced in \
[Caux and Essler, Phys. Rev. Lett. 110, 257203\\n(2013)], is founded on a \
mixture of exact data for overlaps together with\\nvariational reasonings. It \
is argued to be quite generally valid and\\nthermodynamically exact for \
arbitrary times after the quench (from short times\\nall the way up to the \
steady state), and applicable to a wide class of\\nphysically relevant \
observables. Here, we introduce the method and its\\nlanguage, give an \
overview of some recent results, suggest a roadmap and offer\\nsome \
perspectives on possible future research directions.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
