               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1604.00078\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Martin Kruczenski\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Changyu Huang, Yifei He, Martin Kruczenski\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Minimal area surfaces dual to Wilson loops and the \
Mathieu equation\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"32 pages, 3 figure, LaTeX. Version2: References \
added\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP08(2016)088\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The AdS/CFT correspondence relates Wilson loops in N=4 \
SYM to minimal area\\nsurfaces in $AdS_5\\\\times S^5$ space. Recently, a new \
approach to study minimal\\narea surfaces in $AdS_3 \\\\subset AdS_5$ was \
discussed based on a Schroedinger\\nequation with a periodic potential \
determined by the Schwarzian derivative of\\nthe shape of the Wilson loop. \
Here we use the Mathieu equation, a standard\\nexample of a periodic \
potential, to obtain a class of Wilson loops such that\\nthe area of the dual \
minimal area surface can be computed analytically in terms\\nof eigenvalues \
of such equation. As opposed to previous examples, these minimal\\nsurfaces \
have an umbilical point (where the principal curvatures are equal) and\\nare \
invariant under $\\\\lambda$-deformations. In various limits they reduce \
to\\nthe single and multiple wound circular Wilson loop and to the \
regular\\nlight-like polygons studied by Alday and Maldacena. In this last \
limit, the\\nperiodic potential becomes a series of deep wells each related \
to a light-like\\nsegment. Small corrections are described by a \
tight--binding approximation. In\\nthe circular limit they are well \
approximated by an expansion developed by\\nA.Dekel. In the particular case \
of no umbilical points they reduce to a\\nprevious solution proposed by J. \
Toledo. The construction works both in\\nEuclidean and Minkowski signature of \
$AdS_3$.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Thu, 31 Mar 2016 23:31:39 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v2\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Tue, 3 May 2016 18:16:11 GMT\""}]}], "}"}]}], 
                   "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2016-09-21\""}]}, {
