           "id" -> "1604.00078", "submitter" -> "Martin Kruczenski", 
            "authors" -> "Changyu Huang, Yifei He, Martin Kruczenski", 
            "title" -> 
            "Minimal area surfaces dual to Wilson loops and the Mathieu \
equation", "comments" -> 
            "32 pages, 3 figure, LaTeX. Version2: References added", 
            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP08(2016)088", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  The AdS/CFT correspondence relates Wilson loops in N=4 SYM to \
minimal area\nsurfaces in $AdS_5\\times S^5$ space. Recently, a new approach \
to study minimal\narea surfaces in $AdS_3 \\subset AdS_5$ was discussed based \
on a Schroedinger\nequation with a periodic potential determined by the \
Schwarzian derivative of\nthe shape of the Wilson loop. Here we use the \
Mathieu equation, a standard\nexample of a periodic potential, to obtain a \
class of Wilson loops such that\nthe area of the dual minimal area surface \
can be computed analytically in terms\nof eigenvalues of such equation. As \
opposed to previous examples, these minimal\nsurfaces have an umbilical point \
(where the principal curvatures are equal) and\nare invariant under \
$\\lambda$-deformations. In various limits they reduce to\nthe single and \
multiple wound circular Wilson loop and to the regular\nlight-like polygons \
studied by Alday and Maldacena. In this last limit, the\nperiodic potential \
becomes a series of deep wells each related to a light-like\nsegment. Small \
corrections are described by a tight--binding approximation. In\nthe circular \
limit they are well approximated by an expansion developed by\nA.Dekel. In \
the particular case of no umbilical points they reduce to a\nprevious \
solution proposed by J. Toledo. The construction works both in\nEuclidean and \
Minkowski signature of $AdS_3$.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 31 Mar 2016 23:31:39 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 3 May 2016 18:16:11 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2016-09-21", 
