            "  In the ordered phase for an Ising ferromagnet, the magnons are \
attractive to\nform a series of bound states with the mass gaps, $m_2<m_3 < \
\\dots$. Each ratio\n$m_{2,3,\\dots}/m_1$ ($m_1$: the single-magnon mass) is \
expected to be a\nuniversal constant in the vicinity of the critical point. \
In this paper, we\ndevote ourselves to the $(2+1)$-dimensional counterpart, \
for which the\nuniversal hierarchical character remains unclear. We employed \
the exact\ndiagonalization method, which enables us to calculate the \
dynamical\nsusceptibility via the continued-fraction expansion. Thereby, we \
observe a\nvariety of signals including $m_{2,3,4}$, and the spectrum is \
analyzed with the\nfinite-size-scaling method to estimate the universal \
mass-gap ratios.\n", 
