            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1608.01634\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Brenda Penante\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Brenda Penante\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"On-shell methods for off-shell quantities in N=4 Super \
Yang-Mills: from\\n  scattering amplitudes to form factors and the dilatation \
operator\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"206 pages, PhD thesis, references accounted for until \
10/04/16\\n  (submission date)\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Planar maximally supersymmetric Yang-Mills theory (N=4 \
SYM) is a special\\nquantum field theory. A few of its remarkable features \
are conformal symmetry\\nat the quantum level, evidence of integrability and, \
moreover, it is a prime\\nexample of the AdS/CFT duality. Triggered by \
Witten's twistor string theory,\\nthe past 15 years have witnessed enormous \
progress in reformulating this theory\\nto make as many of these special \
features manifest, from the choice of\\nconvenient variables to recursion \
relations that allowed new mathematical\\nstructures to appear, like the \
Grassmannian. These methods are collectively\\nreferred to as on-shell \
methods. The ultimate hope is that, by understanding\\nN=4 SYM in depth, one \
can learn about other, more realistic quantum field\\ntheories. The \
overarching theme of this thesis is the investigation of how\\non-shell \
methods can aid the computation of quantities other than \
scattering\\namplitudes. In this spirit we study form factors and correlation \
functions,\\nsaid to be partially and completely off-shell quantities, \
respectively. More\\nexplicitly, we compute form factors of half-BPS \
operators up to two loops, and\\nstudy the dilatation operator in the SO(6) \
and SU(2|3) sectors using techniques\\noriginally designed for amplitudes. A \
second part of the work is dedicated to\\nthe study of scattering amplitudes \
beyond the planar limit, an area of research\\nwhich is still in its infancy, \
and not much is known about which special\\nfeatures of the planar theory \
survive in the non-planar regime. In this\\ncontext, we generalise some \
aspects of the on-shell diagram formulation of\\nArkani-Hamed et al. to take \
into account non-planar corrections.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Thu, 4 Aug 2016 18:20:06 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2016-08-05\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Penante\"", ",", "\"Brenda\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}], "}"}]}]}}, RowSpacings -> 1, ColumnAlignments -> 
