            "  Planar maximally supersymmetric Yang-Mills theory (N=4 SYM) is \
a special\nquantum field theory. A few of its remarkable features are \
conformal symmetry\nat the quantum level, evidence of integrability and, \
moreover, it is a prime\nexample of the AdS/CFT duality. Triggered by \
Witten's twistor string theory,\nthe past 15 years have witnessed enormous \
progress in reformulating this theory\nto make as many of these special \
features manifest, from the choice of\nconvenient variables to recursion \
relations that allowed new mathematical\nstructures to appear, like the \
Grassmannian. These methods are collectively\nreferred to as on-shell \
methods. The ultimate hope is that, by understanding\nN=4 SYM in depth, one \
can learn about other, more realistic quantum field\ntheories. The \
overarching theme of this thesis is the investigation of how\non-shell \
methods can aid the computation of quantities other than scattering\n\
amplitudes. In this spirit we study form factors and correlation functions,\n\
said to be partially and completely off-shell quantities, respectively. More\n\
explicitly, we compute form factors of half-BPS operators up to two loops, \
and\nstudy the dilatation operator in the SO(6) and SU(2|3) sectors using \
techniques\noriginally designed for amplitudes. A second part of the work is \
dedicated to\nthe study of scattering amplitudes beyond the planar limit, an \
area of research\nwhich is still in its infancy, and not much is known about \
