               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1608.03188\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Ryo Suzuki\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Yusuke Kimura, Sanjaye Ramgoolam, Ryo Suzuki\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Flavour singlets in gauge theory as Permutations\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"50 pages, v2: typos corrected, v3: to appear in \
JHEP\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP12(2016)142\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"OIQP-16-03, QMUL-PH-16-15\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th math.RT\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Gauge-invariant operators can be specified by \
equivalence classes of\\npermutations. We develop this idea concretely for \
the singlets of the flavour\\ngroup $SO(N_f)$ in $U(N_c)$ gauge theory by \
using Gelfand pairs and Schur-Weyl\\nduality. The singlet operators, when \
specialised at $N_f =6$, belong to the\\nscalar sector of ${\\\\cal N}=4$ \
SYM. A simple formula is given for the two-point\\nfunctions in the free \
field limit of $g_{YM}^2 =0$. The free two-point\\nfunctions are shown to be \
equal to the partition function on a 2-complex with\\nboundaries and a \
defect, in a topological field theory of permutations. The\\npermutation \
equivalence classes are Fourier transformed to a representation\\nbasis which \
is orthogonal for the two-point functions at finite $N_c , N_f$.\\nCounting \
formulae for the gauge-invariant operators are described. The \
one-loop\\nmixing matrix is derived as a linear operator on the permutation \
equivalence\\nclasses.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
