               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Bootstrap equations for $\\\\mathcal{N}=4$ SYM with \
defects\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"44 pages, 2 figures, v3: typos fixed, to appear in \
JHEP\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP01(2017)122\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  This paper focuses on the analysis of $4d$ \
$\\\\mathcal{N}=4$ superconformal\\ntheories in the presence of a defect from \
the point of view of the conformal\\nbootstrap. We will concentrate first on \
the case of codimension one, where the\\ndefect is a boundary that preserves \
half of the supersymmetry. After studying\\nthe constraints imposed by \
supersymmetry, we will obtain the Ward identities\\nassociated to two-point \
functions of $\\\\tfrac{1}{2}$-BPS operators and write\\ntheir solution as a \
superconformal block expansion. Due to a surprising\\nconnection between \
spacetime and R-symmetry conformal blocks, our results not\\nonly apply to \
$4d$ $\\\\mathcal{N}=4$ superconformal theories with a boundary, but\\nalso \
to three more systems that have the same symmetry algebra: \
$4d$\\n$\\\\mathcal{N}=4$ superconformal theories with a line defect, \
$3d$\\n$\\\\mathcal{N}=4$ superconformal theories with no defect, and \
$OSP(4^*|4)$\\nsuperconformal quantum mechanics. The superconformal algebra \
implies that all\\nthese systems possess a closed subsector of operators in \
which the bootstrap\\nequations become polynomial constraints on the CFT \
data. We derive these\\ntruncated equations and initiate the study of their \
solutions.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
