               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1609.04430\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Gilly Elor\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Timothy Cohen, Gilly Elor, and Andrew J. Larkoski\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Soft-Collinear Supersymmetry\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"v2: Journal version; major improvements to section on the \
Wess-Zumino\\n  model; minor changes otherwise\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP03(2017)017\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"MIT--CTP 4826\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th hep-ph\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Soft-Collinear Effective Theory (SCET) is a framework \
for modeling the\\ninfrared structure of theories whose long distance \
behavior is dominated by\\nsoft and collinear divergences. This paper \
demonstrates that SCET can be made\\ncompatible with supersymmetry (SUSY). \
Explicitly, the effective Lagrangian for\\n$\\\\mathcal{N} = 1$ SUSY \
Yang-Mills is constructed and shown to be a complete\\ndescription for the \
infrared of this model. For contrast, we also construct the\\neffective \
Lagrangian for chiral SUSY theories with Yukawa couplings,\\nspecifically the \
single flavor Wess-Zumino model. Only a subset of the infrared\\ndivergences \
are reproduced by the Lagrangian -- to account for the complete low\\nenergy \
description requires the inclusion of local operators. SCET is\\nformulated \
by expanding fields along a light-like direction and then\\nsubsequently \
integrating out degrees-of-freedom that are away from the\\nlight-cone. \
Defining the theory with respect to a specific frame obfuscates\\nLorentz \
invariance -- given that SUSY is a space-time symmetry, this presents \
a\\npossible obstruction. The cleanest language with which to expose the \
congruence\\nbetween SUSY and SCET requires exploring two novel formalisms: \
collinear\\nfermions as two-component Weyl spinors, and SCET in light-cone \
gauge. By\\nexpressing SUSY Yang-Mills in \\\"collinear superspace\\\", a \
slice of superspace\\nderived by integrating out half the fermionic \
coordinates, the light-cone gauge\\nSUSY SCET theory can be written in terms \
of superfields. As a byproduct,\\nbootstrapping up to the full theory yields \
the first algorithmic approach for\\ndetermining the SUSY Yang-Mills on-shell \
superspace action. This work paves the\\nway toward discovering the effective \
theory for the collinear limit of\\n$\\\\mathcal{N} = 4$ SUSY \
Yang-Mills.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
