            "  Soft-Collinear Effective Theory (SCET) is a framework for \
modeling the\ninfrared structure of theories whose long distance behavior is \
dominated by\nsoft and collinear divergences. This paper demonstrates that \
SCET can be made\ncompatible with supersymmetry (SUSY). Explicitly, the \
effective Lagrangian for\n$\\mathcal{N} = 1$ SUSY Yang-Mills is constructed \
and shown to be a complete\ndescription for the infrared of this model. For \
contrast, we also construct the\neffective Lagrangian for chiral SUSY \
theories with Yukawa couplings,\nspecifically the single flavor Wess-Zumino \
model. Only a subset of the infrared\ndivergences are reproduced by the \
Lagrangian -- to account for the complete low\nenergy description requires \
the inclusion of local operators. SCET is\nformulated by expanding fields \
along a light-like direction and then\nsubsequently integrating out \
degrees-of-freedom that are away from the\nlight-cone. Defining the theory \
with respect to a specific frame obfuscates\nLorentz invariance -- given that \
SUSY is a space-time symmetry, this presents a\npossible obstruction. The \
cleanest language with which to expose the congruence\nbetween SUSY and SCET \
requires exploring two novel formalisms: collinear\nfermions as two-component \
Weyl spinors, and SCET in light-cone gauge. By\nexpressing SUSY Yang-Mills in \
\"collinear superspace\", a slice of superspace\nderived by integrating out \
half the fermionic coordinates, the light-cone gauge\nSUSY SCET theory can be \
written in terms of superfields. As a byproduct,\nbootstrapping up to the \
full theory yields the first algorithmic approach for\ndetermining the SUSY \
Yang-Mills on-shell superspace action. This work paves the\nway toward \
discovering the effective theory for the collinear limit of\n$\\mathcal{N} = \
4$ SUSY Yang-Mills.\n", 
