            "  In this paper and upcoming ones, we initiate a systematic \
study of Bethe\nansatz equations for integrable models by modern \
computational algebraic\ngeometry. We show that algebraic geometry provides a \
natural mathematical\nlanguage and powerful tools for understanding the \
structure of solution space\nof Bethe ansatz equations. In particular, we \
find novel efficient methods to\ncount the number of solutions of Bethe \
ansatz equations based on Gr\\\"obner\nbasis and quotient ring. We also \
develop analytical approach based on companion\nmatrix to perform the sum of \
on-shell quantities over all physical solutions\nwithout solving Bethe ansatz \
equations explicitly. To demonstrate the power of\nour method, we revisit the \
completeness problem of Bethe ansatz of Heisenberg\nspin chain, and calculate \
the sum rules of OPE coefficients in planar\n$\\mathcal{N}=4$ \
super-Yang-Mills theory.\n", 
