               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1711.01119\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Michele Caselle\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Michele Caselle and Alessandro Nada\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"The $\\\\xi/\\\\xi_{2nd}$ ratio as a test for Effective \
Polyakov Loop Actions\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 
                 "\"8 pages, 1 figure. Talk presented at the 35th \
International Symposium\\n  on Lattice Field Theory, 18-24 June 2017, \
Granada, Spain\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1051/epjconf/201817507040\""}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-lat\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Effective Polyakov line actions are a powerful tool to \
study the finite\\ntemperature behaviour of lattice gauge theories. They are \
much simpler to\\nsimulate than the original (3+1) dimensional LGTs and are \
affected by a milder\\nsign problem. However it is not clear to which extent \
they really capture the\\nrich spectrum of the original theories, a feature \
which is instead of great\\nimportance if one aims to address the sign \
problem. We propose here a simple\\nway to address this issue based on the so \
called second moment correlation\\nlength $\\\\xi_{2nd}$. The ratio \
$\\\\xi/\\\\xi_{2nd}$ between the exponential\\ncorrelation length and the \
second moment one is equal to 1 if only a single\\nmass is present in the \
spectrum, and becomes larger and larger as the\\ncomplexity of the spectrum \
increases. Since both $\\\\xi_{exp}$ and $\\\\xi_{2nd}$\\nare easy to measure \
on the lattice, this is an economic and effective way to\\nkeep track of the \
spectrum of the theory. In this respect we show using both\\nnumerical \
simulation and effective string calculations that this ratio\\nincreases \
dramatically as the temperature decreases. This non-trivial \
behaviour\\nshould be reproduced by the Polyakov loop effective \
action.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Fri, 3 Nov 2017 12:09:57 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2018-04-18\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Caselle\"", ",", "\"Michele\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Nada\"", ",", "\"Alessandro\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}]}], "}"}]}]}}, RowSpacings -> 1, ColumnAlignments -> 
