         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "1711.09993", "submitter" -> "Juan Miguel Nieto", 
            "authors" -> "Juan Miguel Nieto", "title" -> 
            "Spinning strings and correlation functions in the AdS/CFT \
correspondence", "comments" -> 
            "219 pages, 6 figures. PhD thesis based on the publications\n  \
arXiv:1403.6651, arXiv:1407.7475, arXiv:1502.05203, arXiv:1707.08032. v2:\n  \
references corrected", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null, "report-no" -> 
            Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  In this thesis we present some computations made in both sides \
of the AdS/CFT\nholographic correspondence using the integrability of both \
theories.\n  Regarding the string theory side, this thesis is focused in the \
computation\nof the dispersion relation of closed spinning strings in some \
deformed $AdS_3\n\\times S^3$ backgrounds. In particular we are going to \
focus in the deformation\nprovided by the mixing of R-R and NS-NS fluxes and \
the so-called\n$\\eta$-deformation. These computations are made using the \
classical\nintegrability of these two deformed string theories, which is \
provided by the\npresence of a set of conserved quantities called \"Uhlenbeck \
constants\". The\nexistence of the Uhlenbeck constants is central for the \
method used to derive\nthe dispersion relations.\n  Regarding the gauge \
theory side, we are interested in the computation of two\nand three-point \
correlation functions. Concerning the two-point function a\ncomputation of \
correlation functions involving different operators and\ndifferent number of \
excitations is performed using the Algebraic Bethe Ansatz\nand the Quantum \
Inverse Scattering Method. These results are compared with\ncomputations done \
with the Coordinate Bethe Ansatz and with\nZamolodchikov-Faddeev operators. \
Concerning the three-point functions, we will\nexplore the novel construction \
given by the hexagon framework. In particular we\nare going to start from the \
already proposed hexagon form factor and rewrite it\nin a language more \
resembling of the Algebraic Bethe Ansatz. For this intent we\nconstruct an \
invariant vertex using Zamolodchikov-Faddeev operators, which is\nchecked for \
some simple cases.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 27 Nov 2017 20:54:46 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 12 Dec 2017 09:10:32 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2017-12-13", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Nieto", "Juan Miguel", ""}}}], 
          "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{14.778970663391359`, -3.084696861571488}, 0.],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1712.00412\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Andrei Onishchenko\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"R.N. Lee and A.I. Onishchenko\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"ABJM quantum spectral curve and Mellin transform\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"33 pages, typos corrected and clarifications added\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP05(2018)179\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th hep-ph\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The present techniques for the perturbative solution of \
quantum spectral\\ncurve problems in N=4 SYM and ABJM models are limited to \
the situation when the\\nstates quantum numbers are given explicitly as some \
integer numbers. These\\ntechniques are sufficient to recover full analytical \
structure of the conserved\\ncharges provided that we know a finite basis of \
functions in terms of which\\nthey could be written explicitly. It is known \
that in the case of N=4 SYM both\\nthe contributions of asymptotic Bethe \
ansatz and wrapping or finite size\\ncorrections are expressed in terms of \
the harmonic sums. However, in the case\\nof ABJM model only the asymptotic \
contribution can still be written in the\\nharmonic sums basis, while the \
wrapping corrections part can not. Moreover, the\\ngeneralization of harmonic \
sums basis for this problem is not known. In this\\npaper we present a Mellin \
space technique for the solution of multiloop Baxter\\nequations, which is \
the main ingredient for the solution of corresponding\\nquantum spectral \
problems, and provide explicit results for the solution of\\nABJM quantum \
spectral curve in the case of twist 1 operators in sl(2) sector\\nfor \
arbitrary spin values up to four loop order with explicit account \
for\\nwrapping corrections. It is shown that the result for anomalous \
dimensions\\ncould be expressed in terms of harmonic sums decorated by the \
fourth root of\\nunity factors, so that maximum transcendentality principle \
holds.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Fri, 1 Dec 2017 17:21:21 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v2\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Fri, 13 Jul 2018 20:19:23 GMT\""}]}], "}"}]}], 
                   "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2018-07-17\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Lee\"", ",", "\"R. N.\"", ",", "\"\""}], "}"}],
                     ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"Onishchenko\"", ",", "\"A. I.\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}]}], "}"}]}]}}, RowSpacings -> 1, ColumnAlignments -> 
             Left, ColumnAlignments -> Left], Column], 
           Function[BoxForm`e$, 
            TableForm[BoxForm`e$]]]],
         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "1712.00412", "submitter" -> "Andrei Onishchenko", 
            "authors" -> "R.N. Lee and A.I. Onishchenko", "title" -> 
            "ABJM quantum spectral curve and Mellin transform", "comments" -> 
            "33 pages, typos corrected and clarifications added", 
            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP05(2018)179", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th hep-ph", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  The present techniques for the perturbative solution of \
quantum spectral\ncurve problems in N=4 SYM and ABJM models are limited to \
the situation when the\nstates quantum numbers are given explicitly as some \
integer numbers. These\ntechniques are sufficient to recover full analytical \
structure of the conserved\ncharges provided that we know a finite basis of \
functions in terms of which\nthey could be written explicitly. It is known \
that in the case of N=4 SYM both\nthe contributions of asymptotic Bethe \
ansatz and wrapping or finite size\ncorrections are expressed in terms of the \
harmonic sums. However, in the case\nof ABJM model only the asymptotic \
contribution can still be written in the\nharmonic sums basis, while the \
wrapping corrections part can not. Moreover, the\ngeneralization of harmonic \
sums basis for this problem is not known. In this\npaper we present a Mellin \
space technique for the solution of multiloop Baxter\nequations, which is the \
main ingredient for the solution of corresponding\nquantum spectral problems, \
and provide explicit results for the solution of\nABJM quantum spectral curve \
in the case of twist 1 operators in sl(2) sector\nfor arbitrary spin values \
up to four loop order with explicit account for\nwrapping corrections. It is \
shown that the result for anomalous dimensions\ncould be expressed in terms \
of harmonic sums decorated by the fourth root of\nunity factors, so that \
maximum transcendentality principle holds.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 1 Dec 2017 17:21:21 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 13 Jul 2018 20:19:23 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2018-07-17", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Lee", "R. N.", ""}, {
              "Onishchenko", "A. I.", ""}}}], "Tooltip"]& ], 
