         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "1712.06874", "submitter" -> "Matteo Beccaria", "authors" -> 
            "Matteo Beccaria, Simone Giombi, Arkady Tseytlin", "title" -> 
            "Non-supersymmetric Wilson loop in N=4 SYM and defect 1d CFT", 
            "comments" -> "36 pages, 3 figures. v4: minor corrections", 
            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP03(2018)131", 
            "report-no" -> 
            "PUPT-2543, NSF-ITP-17-141, Imperial-TP-AAT-2017-09", 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Following Polchinski and Sully (arXiv:1104.5077), we consider \
a generalized\nWilson loop operator containing a constant parameter $\\zeta$ \
in front of the\nscalar coupling term, so that $\\zeta=0$ corresponds to the \
standard Wilson\nloop, while $\\zeta=1$ to the locally supersymmetric one. We \
compute the\nexpectation value of this operator for circular loop as a \
function of $\\zeta$\nto second order in the planar weak coupling expansion \
in N=4 SYM theory. We\nthen explain the relation of the expansion near the \
two conformal points\n$\\zeta=0$ and $\\zeta=1$ to the correlators of scalar \
operators inserted on the\nloop. We also discuss the $AdS_5\\times S^5$ \
string 1-loop correction to the\nstrong-coupling expansion of the standard \
circular Wilson loop, as well as its\ngeneralization to the case of mixed \
boundary conditions on the five-sphere\ncoordinates, corresponding to general \
$\\zeta$. From the point of view of the\ndefect 1d CFT defined on the Wilson \
line, the $\\zeta$-dependent term can be\nseen as a perturbation driving a RG \
flow from the standard Wilson loop in the\nUV to the supersymmetric Wilson \
loop in the IR. Both at weak and strong\ncoupling we find that the logarithm \
of the expectation value of the standard\nWilson loop for the circular \
contour is larger than that of the supersymmetric\none, which appears to be \
in agreement with the 1d analog of the F-theorem.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 19 Dec 2017 11:42:32 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 9 Jan 2018 08:44:03 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v3", "created" -> 
               "Wed, 28 Feb 2018 15:18:10 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v4", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 11 Oct 2021 16:03:16 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2021-10-12", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Beccaria", "Matteo", ""}, {
              "Giombi", "Simone", ""}, {"Tseytlin", "Arkady", ""}}}], 
          "Tooltip"]& ], 
