, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  Large-N phase transitions occurring in massive N=2 \
theories can be probed by\\nWilson loops in large antisymmetric \
representations. The logarithm of the\\nWilson loop is effectively described \
by the free energy of a Fermi distribution\\nand exhibits second-order phase \
transitions (discontinuities in the second\\nderivatives) as the size of \
representation varies. We illustrate the general\\nfeatures of antisymmetric \
Wilson loops on a number of examples where the phase\\ntransitions are known \
to occur: N=2 SQCD with various mass arrangements and\\nN=2* theory. As a \
byproduct we solve planar N=2 SQCD with three independent\\nmass parameters. \
This model has two effective mass scales and undergoes two\\nphase \
transitions.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
