               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Robert de Mello Koch\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Shaun de Carvalho, Robert de Mello Koch and Augustine \
Larweh Mahu\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Anomalous dimensions from boson lattice models\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"26+1 pages\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Phys. Rev. D 97, 126004 (2018)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.97.126004\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Operators dual to strings attached to giant graviton \
branes in\\nAdS$_5\\\\times$S$^5$ can be described rather explicitly in the \
dual ${\\\\cal N} =\\n4$ super Yang-Mills theory. They have a bare dimension \
of order $N$ so that for\\nthese operators the large $N$ limit and the planar \
limit are distinct: summing\\nonly the planar diagrams will not capture the \
large $N$ dynamics. Focusing on\\nthe one-loop $SU(3)$ sector of the theory, \
we consider operators that are a\\nsmall deformation of a ${1\\\\over 2}-$BPS \
multi-giant graviton state. The\\ndiagonalization of the dilatation operator \
at one loop has been carried out,\\nbut explicit formulas for the operators \
of a good scaling dimension are only\\nknown when certain terms which were \
argued to be small, are neglected. In this\\narticle we include the terms \
which were neglected. The diagonalization is\\nachieved by a novel mapping \
which replaces the problem of diagonalizing the\\ndilatation operator with a \
system of bosons hopping on a lattice. The giant\\ngravitons define the sites \
of this lattice and the open strings stretching\\nbetween distinct giant \
gravitons define the hopping terms of the Hamiltonian.\\nUsing the lattice \
boson model, we argue that the lowest energy giant graviton\\nstates are \
obtained by distributing the momenta carried by the $X$ and $Y$\\nfields \
evenly between the giants with the condition that any particular \
giant\\ncarries only $X$ or $Y$ momenta, but not both.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Tue, 9 Jan 2018 07:03:26 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2018-06-20\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"de Carvalho\"", ",", "\"Shaun\"", ",", "\"\""}], "}"}],
                     ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"Koch\"", ",", "\"Robert de Mello\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"Mahu\"", ",", "\"Augustine Larweh\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}]}], "}"}]}]}}, RowSpacings -> 1, ColumnAlignments -> 
             Left, ColumnAlignments -> Left], Column], 
