               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1802.00427\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Rajesh Gupta\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Justin R. David, Edi Gava, Rajesh Kumar Gupta, Kumar \
Narain\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Boundary Conditions and Localization on AdS: Part \
1\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"34 pages\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP09(2018)063\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study the role of boundary conditions on the one \
loop partition function\\nof ${\\\\cal N}=2$ chiral multiplet of R-charge $\\\
\\Delta$ on $AdS_2\\\\times S^1$. The\\nchiral multiplet is coupled to a \
background vector multiplet which preserves\\nsupersymmetry. We implement \
normalizable boundary conditions in $AdS_2$ and\\ndevelop the Green's \
function method to obtain the one loop determinant. We\\nevaluate the one \
loop determinant for two different actions: the standard\\naction and the \
$Q$-exact deformed positive definite action used for\\nlocalization. We show \
that if there exists an integer $n$ in the interval $D: \
(\\n\\\\frac{\\\\Delta-1}{2L}, \\\\frac{\\\\Delta}{2L} )$, where $L$ being \
the ratio of radius\\nof $AdS_2$ to that of $S^1$, then the one loop \
determinants obtained for the\\ntwo actions differ. It is in this situation \
that fields which obey normalizable\\nboundary conditions do not obey \
supersymmetric boundary conditions. However if\\nthere are no integers in \
$D$, then fields which obey normalizable boundary\\nconditions also obey \
supersymmetric boundary conditions and the one loop\\ndeterminants of the two \
actions precisely agree. We also show that it is only\\nin the latter \
situation that the one loop determinant obtained by evaluating\\nthe index of \
the $D_{10}$ operator associated with the localizing action agrees\\nwith the \
one loop determinant obtained using Green's function method.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
