Gabriele\\n  Travaglini\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"$\\\\text{Tr}(F^3)$ supersymmetric form factors and \
maximal\\n  transcendentality Part II: $0<\\\\mathcal{N}<4$ super \
Yang-Mills\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Version accepted in JHEP\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP12(2018)077\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"QMUL-PH-18-05, CERN-TH-2018-064\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The study of form factors has many phenomenologically \
interesting\\napplications, one of which is Higgs plus gluon amplitudes in \
QCD. Through\\neffective field theory techniques these are related to form \
factors of various\\noperators of increasing classical dimension. In this \
paper we extend our\\nanalysis of the first finite top-mass correction, \
arising from the operator\\n${\\\\rm Tr} (F^3)$, from $\\\\mathcal{N}=4$ \
super Yang-Mills to theories with\\n$\\\\mathcal{N}<4$, for the case of three \
gluons and up to two loops. We confirm\\nour earlier result that the \
maximally transcendental part of the associated\\nCatani remainder is \
universal and equal to that of the form factor of a\\nprotected trilinear \
operator in the maximally supersymmetric theory. The terms\\nwith lower \
transcendentality deviate from the $\\\\mathcal{N}=4$ answer by \
a\\nsurprisingly small set of terms involving for example $\\\\zeta_2$, \
$\\\\zeta_3$ and\\nsimple powers of logarithms, for which we provide explicit \
expressions.\\n\""}]}, {
