            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1806.01862\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Pedro Liendo\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Pedro Liendo, Carlo Meneghelli, Vladimir Mitev\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Bootstrapping the half-BPS line defect\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"v2: typos corrected, references added\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP10(2018)077\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"DESY 18-087, MITP/18-043\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We use modern bootstrap techniques to study half-BPS \
line defects in 4d N=4\\nsuperconformal theories. Specifically, we consider \
the 1d CFT with OSP(4*|4)\\nsuperconformal symmetry living on such a defect. \
Our analysis is general and\\nbased only on symmetries, it includes however \
important examples like Wilson\\nand 't Hooft lines in N=4 super Yang-Mills. \
We present several numerical bounds\\non OPE coefficients and conformal \
dimensions. Of particular interest is a\\nnumerical island obtained from a \
mixed correlator bootstrap that seems to imply\\na unique solution to \
crossing. The island is obtained if some assumptions about\\nthe spectrum are \
made, and is consistent with Wilson lines in planar N=4 super\\nYang-Mills at \
strong coupling. We further analyze the vicinity of the\\nstrong-coupling \
point by calculating perturbative corrections using analytic\\nmethods. This \
perturbative solution has the sparsest spectrum and is expected\\nto saturate \
the numerical bounds, explaining some of the features of our\\nnumerical \
results.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
