           "id" -> "1806.10134", "submitter" -> "Ashmeet Singh", "authors" -> 
            "Ashmeet Singh, Sean M. Carroll", "title" -> 
            "Modeling Position and Momentum in Finite-Dimensional Hilbert \
Spaces via\n  Generalized Pauli Operators", "comments" -> 
            "23 pages, 5 figures, comments welcome. v2 contains some change \
in\n  terminology, added references", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null, 
            "report-no" -> "CALT-TH-2018-29", "categories" -> 
            "quant-ph gr-qc hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  The finite entropy of black holes suggests that local regions \
of spacetime\nare described by finite-dimensional factors of Hilbert space, \
in contrast with\nthe infinite-dimensional Hilbert spaces of quantum field \
theory. With this in\nmind, we explore how to cast finite-dimensional quantum \
mechanics in a form\nthat matches naturally onto the smooth case, especially \
the recovery of\nconjugate position/momentum variables, in the limit of large \
Hilbert-space\ndimension. A natural tool for this task are the Generalized \
Pauli operators\n(GPO). Based on an exponential form of Heisenberg's \
canonical commutation\nrelation, the GPO offers a finite-dimensional \
generalization of conjugate\nvariables without relying on any a priori \
structure on Hilbert space. We\nhighlight some features of the GPO, its \
importance in studying concepts such as\nspread induced by operators, and \
point out departures from infinite-dimensional\nresults (possibly with a \
cutoff) that might play a crucial role in our\nunderstanding of quantum \
gravity. We introduce the concept of \"Operator\nCollimation,\" which \
characterizes how the action of an operator spreads a\nquantum state along \
conjugate directions. We illustrate these concepts with a\nworked example of \
a finite-dimensional harmonic oscillator, demonstrating how\nthe energy \
spectrum deviates from the familiar infinite-dimensional case.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
