            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We study the structure of thermal spectral function of the \
stress-energy\ntensor in ${\\cal N}=4$ supersymmetric Yang-Mills theory at \
intermediate 't\nHooft coupling and infinite number of colors. In \
gauge-string duality, this\nanalysis reduces to the study of classical bulk \
supergravity with\nhigher-derivative corrections, which correspond to \
(inverse) coupling\ncorrections on the gauge theory side. We extrapolate the \
analysis of\nperturbative leading-order corrections to intermediate coupling \
by\nnon-perturbatively solving the equations of motion of metric fluctuations \
dual\nto the stress-energy tensor at zero spatial momentum. We observe the \
emergence\nof a separation of scales in the analytic structure of the thermal \
correlator\nassociated with two types of characteristic relaxation modes. As \
a consequence\nof this separation, the associated spectral function exhibits \
a narrow\nstructure in the small frequency region which controls the dynamics \
of\ntransport in the theory and may be described as a transport peak \
typically\nfound in perturbative, weakly interacting thermal field theories. \
We compare\nour results with generic expectations drawn from perturbation \
theory, where\nsuch a structure emerges as a consequence of the existence of \
quasiparticles.\n", 
