                 "\"Cyril Closset, Heeyeon Kim, Brian Willett\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Seifert fibering operators in 3d $\\\\mathcal{N}=2$ \
theories\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"135 pages + appendix; v2: fixed typos, added references, \
small\\n  corrections in section 9\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP11(2018)004\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"CERN-TH-2018-156\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study 3d $\\\\mathcal{N}=2$ supersymmetric gauge \
theories on closed oriented\\nSeifert manifold---circle bundles over an \
orbifold Riemann surface---, with a\\ngauge group G given by a product of \
simply-connected and/or unitary Lie groups.\\nOur main result is an exact \
formula for the supersymmetric partition function\\non any Seifert manifold, \
generalizing previous results on lens spaces. We\\nexplain how the result for \
an arbitrary Seifert geometry can be obtained by\\ncombining simple building \
blocks, the \\\"fibering operators.\\\" These operators are\\nhalf-BPS line \
defects, whose insertion along the $S^1$ fiber has the effect of\\nchanging \
the topology of the Seifert fibration. We also point out that \
most\\nsupersymmetric partition functions on Seifert manifolds admit a \
discrete\\nrefinement, corresponding to the freedom in choosing a \
three-dimensional spin\\nstructure. As a strong consistency check on our \
result, we show that the\\nSeifert partition functions match exactly across \
infrared dualities. The\\nduality relations are given by intricate (and \
seemingly new) mathematical\\nidentities, which we tested numerically. \
Finally, we discuss in detail the\\nsupersymmetric partition function on the \
lens space $L(p,q)_b$ with rational\\nsquashing parameter $b^2 \\\\in \
\\\\mathbb{Q}$, comparing our formalism to previous\\nresults, and explaining \
the relationship between the fibering operators and the\\nthree-dimensional \
holomorphic blocks.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
