          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "1807.02328", "submitter" -> "Cyril Closset", "authors" -> 
            "Cyril Closset, Heeyeon Kim, Brian Willett", "title" -> 
            "Seifert fibering operators in 3d $\\mathcal{N}=2$ theories", 
            "comments" -> 
            "135 pages + appendix; v2: fixed typos, added references, small\n \
 corrections in section 9", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/JHEP11(2018)004", "report-no" -> "CERN-TH-2018-156", 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We study 3d $\\mathcal{N}=2$ supersymmetric gauge theories on \
closed oriented\nSeifert manifold---circle bundles over an orbifold Riemann \
surface---, with a\ngauge group G given by a product of simply-connected \
and/or unitary Lie groups.\nOur main result is an exact formula for the \
supersymmetric partition function\non any Seifert manifold, generalizing \
previous results on lens spaces. We\nexplain how the result for an arbitrary \
Seifert geometry can be obtained by\ncombining simple building blocks, the \
\"fibering operators.\" These operators are\nhalf-BPS line defects, whose \
insertion along the $S^1$ fiber has the effect of\nchanging the topology of \
the Seifert fibration. We also point out that most\nsupersymmetric partition \
functions on Seifert manifolds admit a discrete\nrefinement, corresponding to \
the freedom in choosing a three-dimensional spin\nstructure. As a strong \
consistency check on our result, we show that the\nSeifert partition \
functions match exactly across infrared dualities. The\nduality relations are \
given by intricate (and seemingly new) mathematical\nidentities, which we \
tested numerically. Finally, we discuss in detail the\nsupersymmetric \
partition function on the lens space $L(p,q)_b$ with rational\nsquashing \
parameter $b^2 \\in \\mathbb{Q}$, comparing our formalism to previous\n\
results, and explaining the relationship between the fibering operators and \
the\nthree-dimensional holomorphic blocks.\n", 
