matrix\n  models", "comments" -> "17 pages", "journal-ref" -> 
            "J. High Energ. Phys. 2018 (2018) 163", "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/JHEP08(2018)163", "report-no" -> 
            "FIAN/TD-12/18; IITP/TH-12/18; ITEP/TH-18/18", "categories" -> 
            "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  One of the main features of eigenvalue matrix models is that \
the averages of\ncharacters are again characters, what can be considered as a \
far-going\ngeneralization of the Fourier transform property of Gaussian \
exponential. This\nis true for the standard Hermitian and unitary \
(trigonometric) matrix models\nand for their various deformations, classical \
and quantum ones. Arising\nexplicit formulas for the partition functions are \
very efficient for practical\ncomputer calculations. However, to handle them \
theoretically, one needs to tame\nthe remaining finite sums over \
representations of a given size, which turns\ninto an interesting conceptual \
problem. Already the semicircle distribution in\nthe large-$N$ limit implies \
interesting combinatorial sum rules for characters.\nWe describe also \
implications to $W$-representations, including a character\ndecomposition of \
cut-and-join operators, which unexpectedly involves only\nsingle-hook \
diagrams and also requires non-trivial summation identities.\n", 
