N=1$ multiplets in\\n  four dimensions\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"44 pages. v3: remarks on anomaly-free theories at the end \
of section\\n  5 corrected\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP10(2018)087\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Imperial-TP-AT-2018-04\""}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Supersymmetric partition function of $\\\\mathcal N=1$ \
superconformal theories\\non $S^1_{\\\\beta} \\\\times S^3$ is related to the \
superconformal index receiving\\ncontributions from short representations. \
The leading coefficients in the small\\n$\\\\beta$ (high \\\"temperature\\\") \
expansion of the index were previously related to\\nthe conformal anomaly \
coefficients of the theory. Assumptions underlying\\nuniversality of these \
relations were tested only for simplest low-spin unitary\\nmultiplets. Here \
we consider examples of higher derivative non-unitary\\n$\\\\mathcal N=1$ \
multiplets that naturally appear in the context of extended\\nconformal \
supergravities and compute their superconformal index. We compare \
the\\ncoefficients in the small $\\\\beta$ expansion of the index with those \
proposed\\nearlier for unitary multiplets and suggest some modifications that \
should apply\\nuniversally to all types of theories. We also comment on the \
structure of\\nsubleading terms and the case of $\\\\mathcal N=4$ conformal \
supergravity.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
