               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Leonardo Santilli and Miguel Tierz\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Phase transitions and Wilson loops in antisymmetric \
representations in\\n  Chern-Simons-matter theory\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"ii+49 pages, 6 figures. v2: one reference added. v3: some \
sections\\n  shortened; results, references and a table added; final \
published version\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"J. Phys. A: Math. Theor. 52 385401 (2019)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1088/1751-8121/ab335c\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study the phase transitions of three-dimensional $\\\
\\mathcal{N}=2$ $U(N)$\\nChern-Simons theory on $\\\\mathbb{S}^3$ with a \
varied number of massive\\nfundamental hypermultiplets and with a \
Fayet-Iliopoulos parameter. We\\ncharacterize the various phase diagrams in \
the decompactification limit,\\naccording to the number of different mass \
scales in the theory. For this, we\\nextend the known solution of the \
saddle-point equations to the setting where\\nthe one cut solution is \
characterized by asymmetric intervals. We then study\\nthe large $N$ limit of \
Wilson loops in antisymmetric representations, with the\\nadditional scaling \
corresponding to the variation of the size of the\\nrepresentation. We give \
explicit expressions, both with and without FI terms,\\nand study $1/R$ \
corrections for the different phases. These corrections break\\nthe perimeter \
law behavior, as they introduce a scaling with the size of \
the\\nrepresentation. We show how the phase transitions of the Wilson loops \
can\\neither be of first or second order and determine the underlying \
mechanism in\\nterms of the eventual asymmetry of the support of the solution \
of the\\nsaddle-point equation, at the critical points.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
