               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1808.09434\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Fedor Popov\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Igor R. Klebanov, Fedor Popov, Grigory Tarnopolsky\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"TASI Lectures on Large $N$ Tensor Models\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"43 pages, 31 figures. This is an extended write-up of \
lectures given\\n  at TASI 2017. v2: minor improvements, references \
added\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th cond-mat.str-el\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The first part of these lecture notes is mostly devoted \
to a comparative\\ndiscussion of the three basic large $N$ limits, which \
apply to fields which are\\nvectors, matrices, or tensors of rank three and \
higher. After a brief review of\\nsome physical applications of large $N$ \
limits, we present a few solvable\\nexamples in zero space-time dimension. \
Using models with fields in the\\nfundamental representation of $O(N)$, \
$O(N)^2$, or $O(N)^3$ symmetry, we\\ncompare their combinatorial properties \
and highlight a competition between the\\nsnail and melon diagrams. We \
exhibit the different methods used for solving the\\nvector, matrix, and \
tensor large $N$ limits. In the latter example we review\\nhow the dominance \
of melonic diagrams follows when a special \\\"tetrahedral\\\"\\ninteraction \
is introduced. The second part of the lectures is mostly about \
the\\nfermionic quantum mechanical tensor models, whose large $N$ limits are \
similar\\nto that in the Sachdev-Ye-Kitaev (SYK) model. The minimal Majorana \
model with\\n$O(N)^3$ symmetry and the tetrahedral Hamiltonian is reviewed in \
some detail;\\nit is the closest tensor counterpart of the SYK model. Also \
reviewed are\\ngeneralizations to complex fermionic tensors, including a \
model with\\n$SU(N)^2\\\\times O(N)\\\\times U(1)$ symmetry, which is a \
tensor counterpart of the\\ncomplex SYK model. The bosonic large $N$ tensor \
models, which are formally\\ntractable in continuous spacetime dimension, are \
reviewed briefly at the end.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Tue, 28 Aug 2018 17:48:13 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v2\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Wed, 5 Sep 2018 19:10:55 GMT\""}]}], "}"}]}], 
                   "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2018-09-07\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Klebanov\"", ",", "\"Igor R.\"", ",", "\"\""}],
                     "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Popov\"", ",", "\"Fedor\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}], ",", 
