         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "1809.05705", "submitter" -> "Dinh-Long Vu", "authors" -> 
            "Ivan Kostov, Didina Serban and Dinh-Long Vu", "title" -> 
            "Boundary TBA, trees and loops", "comments" -> 
            "26 pages, 7 figures version 2: comments about non-diagonal \
scattering\n  added version 3: comments on excited state g-function added, \
goal of section\n  6 changed", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> 
            "10.1016/j.nuclphysb.2019.114817", "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> "hep-th cond-mat.stat-mech", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We derive a graph expansion for the thermal partition function \
of solvable\ntwo-dimensional models with boundaries. This expansion of the \
integration\nmeasure over the virtual particles winding around the time cycle \
is obtained\nwith the help of the matrix-tree theorem. The free energy is a \
sum over all\nconnected graphs, which can be either trees or trees with one \
loop. The\ngenerating function for the connected trees satisfies a non-linear \
integral\nequation, which is equivalent to the TBA equation. The sum over \
connected\ngraphs gives the bulk free energy as well as the exact g-functions \
for the two\nboundaries. We reproduced the integral formula conjectured by \
Dorey,\nFioravanti, Rim and Tateo, and proved subsequently by Pozsgay. The \
method is\neasily extended to the case of non-diagonal bulk scattering and \
diagonal\nreflection matrices. Our method can be extended to the case of \
non-diagonal\nbulk scattering and diagonal reflection matrices with proper \
regularization.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Sat, 15 Sep 2018 12:03:31 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 5 Jul 2019 15:29:52 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v3", "created" -> 
               "Wed, 25 Sep 2019 16:37:17 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2020-01-08", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Kostov", "Ivan", ""}, {
              "Serban", "Didina", ""}, {"Vu", "Dinh-Long", ""}}}], 
          "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{13.845709603594269`, -9.099447998037821}, 0.],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1809.06272\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Usman Naseer\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Usman Naseer\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"(1,0) gauge theories on the six-sphere\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"33 pages, two appendices. v3: more typos fixed, a \
reference added, a\\n  field redefinition that makes the vector-multiplet \
action manifestly regular\\n  is discussed\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"SciPost Phys. 6, 002 (2019)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.21468/SciPostPhys.6.1.002\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"UUITP-38/18, MIT-CTP/5055\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We construct gauge theories with a vector-multiplet and \
hypermultiplets of\\n$(1,0)$ supersymmetry on the six-sphere. The gauge \
coupling on the sphere\\ndepends on the polar angle. This has a natural \
explanation in terms of the\\ntensor branch of $(1,0)$ theories on the \
six-sphere. For the vector-multiplet\\nwe give an off-shell formulation for \
all supersymmetries. For hypermultiplets\\nwe give an off-shell formulation \
for one supersymmetry. We show that the path\\nintegral for the \
vector-multiplet localizes to solutions of the\\nHermitian-Yang-Mills \
equation, which is a generalization of the (anti-)self\\nduality condition to \
higher dimensions. For the hypermultiplet, the path\\nintegral localizes to \
configurations where the field strengths of two complex\\nscalars are related \
by an almost complex structure.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
