                 "\"J. Stat. Mech. (2019) 063103\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1088/1742-5468/ab1c51\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"cond-mat.stat-mech cond-mat.quant-gas quant-ph\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We consider quantum quenches in the integrable \
$SU(3)$-invariant spin chain\\n(Lai-Sutherland model) which admits a Bethe \
ansatz description in terms of two\\ndifferent quasiparticle species, \
providing a prototypical example of a model\\nsolvable by nested Bethe \
ansatz. We identify infinite families of integrable\\ninitial states for \
which analytic results can be obtained. We show that they\\ninclude special \
families of two-site product states which can be related to\\nintegrable \
``soliton non-preserving'' boundary conditions in an appropriate\\nrotated \
channel. We present a complete analytical result for the \
quasiparticle\\nrapidity distribution functions corresponding to the \
stationary state reached\\nat large times after the quench from the \
integrable initial states. Our results\\nare obtained within a Quantum \
Transfer Matrix (QTM) approach, which does not\\nrely on the knowledge of the \
quasilocal conservation laws or of the overlaps\\nbetween the initial states \
and the eigenstates of the Hamiltonian. Furthermore,\\nbased on an analogy \
with previous works, we conjecture analytic expressions for\\nsuch overlaps: \
this allows us to employ the Quench Action method to derive a\\nset of \
integral equations characterizing the quasi-particle distribution\\nfunctions \
of the post-quench steady state. We verify that the solution to the\\nlatter \
coincides with our analytic result found using the QTM approach.\\nFinally, \
we present a direct physical application of our results by \
providing\\npredictions for the propagation of entanglement after the quench \
from such\\nintegrable states.\\n\""}]}, {
