 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  BPS vortex systems on closed Riemann surfaces with \
arbitrary genus are\\nembedded into two-dimensional supersymmetric Yang-Mills \
theory with matters. We\\nturn on a background R-gauge fields to keep half of \
rigid supersymmetry\\n(topological A-twist) on the curved space. We consider \
two complementary\\ndescriptions; Higgs and Coulomb branches. The path \
integral reduces to the zero\\nmode integral by the localization in the Higgs \
branch. The integral over the\\nbosonic zero modes directly gives an integral \
over the volume form of the\\nmoduli space, whereas the fermionic zero modes \
are compensated by an\\nappropriate operator insertion. In the Coulomb branch \
description with the same\\noperator insertion, the path integral reduces to \
a finite-dimensional residue\\nintegral. The operator insertion automatically \
determines a choice of integral\\ncontours, leading to the Jeffrey-Kirwan \
residue formula. This result ensures\\nthe existence of the solution to the \
BPS vortex equation and explains the\\nBradlow bounds of the BPS vortex. We \
also discuss a generating function of the\\nvolume of the vortex moduli space \
and show a reduction of the moduli space from\\nsemi-local to local vortices.\
\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
