               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"The Bremsstrahlung function of $\\\\mathcal{N} \\\\!= \
\\\\!2 $ SCQCD\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"30 pages; v3: JHEP published version\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP03(2019)122\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  For $SU(N)$ superconformal QCD we perform a three-loop \
calculation of the\\ngeneralized cusp anomalous dimension of the BPS Wilson \
loop operator using HQET\\nformalism. We obtain an expression which is valid \
at generic geometric and\\ninternal angles and finite gauge group rank $N$. \
For equal and opposite angles\\nthis expression vanishes, proving that at \
these points the cusp becomes BPS.\\nFrom its small angle expansion we derive \
the corresponding Bremsstrahlung\\nfunction at three loops, matching the \
matrix model prediction given in terms of\\nderivatives of the Wilson loop on \
the ellipsoid. Finally, we discuss possible\\nscenarios at higher loops, with \
respect to the existence of a universal\\neffective coupling in an integrable \
subsector of the model.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
