               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1812.08132\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Alexander Krikun\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Tomas Andrade and Alexander Krikun\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Coherent vs incoherent transport in holographic strange \
insulators\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"21 pages + appendices, 15 figures; v2: revised \
version\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP05(2019)119\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th cond-mat.str-el\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Holographic strange metals are known to have a power \
law resistivity rising\\nwith temperature, which is reminiscent of the \
strange metal phases in condensed\\nmatter systems. In some holographic \
models, however, the exponent of the power\\nlaw in the resistivity can be \
negative. In this case one encounters phases with\\ndiverging resistivity at \
zero temperature: holographic strange insulators.\\n  These states arise as a \
result of translational symmetry breaking in the\\nsystem, which can either \
be strong explicit and relevant in the IR, or\\nspontaneous, but pinned by a \
small explicit source. In some regards, one can\\nassociate these two classes \
to the normal band insulators due to the strong\\nionic potential, and Mott \
insulator due to the commensurate lock in of the\\ncharge density wave.\\n  \
We study different features of these classes on the explicit example of \
a\\nholographic helical model with homogeneous Bianchy VII type translational\
\\nsymmetry breaking, and uncover the main mechanisms underlying transport in\
\\nthese two cases. We find that while transport in the explicit relevant \
case is\\ngoverned by the incoherent conductivity, in the pinned spontaneous \
case the\\nleading contribution comes from the coherent part.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
