            "Coherent vs incoherent transport in holographic strange \
insulators", "comments" -> 
            "21 pages + appendices, 15 figures; v2: revised version", 
            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP05(2019)119", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th cond-mat.str-el", 
            "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Holographic strange metals are known to have a power law \
resistivity rising\nwith temperature, which is reminiscent of the strange \
metal phases in condensed\nmatter systems. In some holographic models, \
however, the exponent of the power\nlaw in the resistivity can be negative. \
In this case one encounters phases with\ndiverging resistivity at zero \
temperature: holographic strange insulators.\n  These states arise as a \
result of translational symmetry breaking in the\nsystem, which can either be \
strong explicit and relevant in the IR, or\nspontaneous, but pinned by a \
small explicit source. In some regards, one can\nassociate these two classes \
to the normal band insulators due to the strong\nionic potential, and Mott \
insulator due to the commensurate lock in of the\ncharge density wave.\n  We \
study different features of these classes on the explicit example of a\n\
holographic helical model with homogeneous Bianchy VII type translational\n\
symmetry breaking, and uncover the main mechanisms underlying transport in\n\
these two cases. We find that while transport in the explicit relevant case \
is\ngoverned by the incoherent conductivity, in the pinned spontaneous case \
the\nleading contribution comes from the coherent part.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
