               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1903.01408\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Georgios Pastras\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Dimitrios Katsinis, Ioannis Mitsoulas and Georgios \
Pastras\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Salient Features of Dressed Elliptic String Solutions \
on\\n  $\\\\mathbb{R}\\\\times$S$^2$\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"75 pages, 27 figures\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Eur.Phys.J.C 79 (2019) 10, 869\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"10.1140/epjc/s10052-019-7369-0\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th nlin.SI\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We analyse several physical aspects of the dressed \
elliptic strings\\npropagating on $\\\\mathbb{R} \\\\times \\\\mathrm{S}^2$ \
and of their counterparts in\\nthe Pohlmeyer reduced theory, i.e. the \
sine-Gordon equation. The solutions are\\ndivided into two wide classes; \
kinks which propagate on top of elliptic\\nbackgrounds and those which are \
non-localised periodic disturbances of the\\nlatter. The former class of \
solutions obey a specific equation of state that is\\nin principle \
experimentally verifiable in systems which realize the \
sine-Gordon\\nequation. Among both of these classes, there appears to be a \
particular class\\nof interest the closed dressed strings. They in turn form \
four distinct\\nsubclasses of solutions. Unlike the closed elliptic strings, \
these four\\nsubclasses, exhibit interactions among their spikes. These \
interactions\\npreserve a carefully defined turning number, which can be \
associated to the\\ntopological charge of the sine-Gordon counterpart. One \
particular class of\\nthose closed dressed strings realizes instabilities of \
the seed elliptic\\nsolutions. The existence of such solutions depends on \
whether a superluminal\\nkink with a specific velocity can propagate on the \
corresponding elliptic\\nsine-Gordon solution. Finally, the dispersion \
relations of the dressed strings\\nare studied. A qualitative difference \
between the two wide classes of dressed\\nstrings is discovered. This would \
be an interesting subject for investigation\\nin the dual field \
theory.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Mon, 4 Mar 2019 18:05:31 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2020-04-20\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
