         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "1904.06259", "submitter" -> "Gabriele Perfetto", 
            "authors" -> "Gabriele Perfetto, Lorenzo Piroli, Andrea Gambassi",
             "title" -> 
            "Quench action and large deviations: work statistics in the\n  \
one-dimensional Bose gas", "comments" -> "24 Pages, 6 figures", "journal-ref" -> 
            "Phys. Rev. E 100, 032114 (2019)", "doi" -> 
            "10.1103/PhysRevE.100.032114", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "cond-mat.stat-mech cond-mat.quant-gas quant-ph", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We study the statistics of large deviations of the intensive \
work done in an\ninteraction quench of a one-dimensional Bose gas with a \
large number N of\nparticles, system size L and fixed density. We consider \
the case in which the\nsystem is initially prepared in the non-interacting \
ground state and a\nrepulsive interaction is suddenly turned on. For large \
deviations of the work\nbelow its mean value, we show that the large \
deviation principle holds by means\nof the quench action approach. Using the \
latter, we compute exactly the\nso-called rate function, and study its \
properties analytically. In particular,\nwe find that fluctuations close to \
the mean value of the work exhibit a marked\nnon- Gaussian behavior, even \
though their probability is always exponentially\nsuppressed below it as L \
increases. Deviations larger than the mean value,\ninstead, exhibit an \
algebraic decay, whose exponent can not be determined\ndirectly by \
large-deviation theory. Exploiting the exact Bethe ansatz\nrepresentation of \
the eigenstates of the Hamiltonian, we calculate this\nexponent for vanishing \
particle density. Our approach can be straightforwardly\ngeneralized to \
quantum quenches in other interacting integrable systems.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 12 Apr 2019 14:47:32 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2019-09-12", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Perfetto", "Gabriele", ""}, {
