 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  This work is a continuation of our previous paper \
arXiv:1812.06473 where we\\nhave constructed ${\\\\cal N}=2$ supersymmetric \
Yang-Mills theory on 4D manifolds\\nwith a Killing vector field with isolated \
fixed points. In this work we expand\\non the mathematical aspects of the \
theory, with a particular focus on its\\nnature as a cohomological field \
theory. The well-known Donaldson-Witten theory\\nis a twisted version of ${\\\
\\cal N}=2$ SYM and can also be constructed using the\\nAtiyah-Jeffrey \
construction. This theory is concerned with the moduli space \
of\\nanti-self-dual gauge connections, with a deformation theory controlled \
by an\\nelliptic complex. More generally, supersymmetry requires \
considering\\nconfigurations that look like either instantons or \
anti-instantons around fixed\\npoints, which we call flipping instantons. The \
flipping instantons of our 4D\\n${\\\\cal N}=2$ theory are derived from the \
5D contact instantons. The novelty is\\nthat their deformation theory is \
controlled by a transversally elliptic\\ncomplex, which we demonstrate here. \
We repeat the Atiyah-Jeffrey construction\\nin the equivariant setting and \
arrive at the Lagrangian (an equivariant Euler\\nclass in the relevant field \
space) that was also obtained from our previous\\nwork arXiv:1812.06473. We \
show that the transversal ellipticity of the\\ndeformation complex is crucial \
for the non-degeneracy of the Lagrangian and the\\ncalculability of the \
theory. Our construction is valid on a large class of\\nquasi toric 4 \
manifolds.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
