           "id" -> "1904.12782", "submitter" -> "Maxim Zabzine", "authors" -> 
            "Guido Festuccia, Jian Qiu, Jacob Winding and Maxim Zabzine", 
            "title" -> 
            "Transversally Elliptic Complex and Cohomological Field Theory", 
            "comments" -> 
            "88 pages, typos corrected, the final version to be published in \
JGP", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1016/j.geomphys.2020.103786", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th math.DG", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  This work is a continuation of our previous paper \
arXiv:1812.06473 where we\nhave constructed ${\\cal N}=2$ supersymmetric \
Yang-Mills theory on 4D manifolds\nwith a Killing vector field with isolated \
fixed points. In this work we expand\non the mathematical aspects of the \
theory, with a particular focus on its\nnature as a cohomological field \
theory. The well-known Donaldson-Witten theory\nis a twisted version of \
${\\cal N}=2$ SYM and can also be constructed using the\nAtiyah-Jeffrey \
construction. This theory is concerned with the moduli space of\n\
anti-self-dual gauge connections, with a deformation theory controlled by an\n\
elliptic complex. More generally, supersymmetry requires considering\n\
configurations that look like either instantons or anti-instantons around \
fixed\npoints, which we call flipping instantons. The flipping instantons of \
our 4D\n${\\cal N}=2$ theory are derived from the 5D contact instantons. The \
novelty is\nthat their deformation theory is controlled by a transversally \
elliptic\ncomplex, which we demonstrate here. We repeat the Atiyah-Jeffrey \
construction\nin the equivariant setting and arrive at the Lagrangian (an \
equivariant Euler\nclass in the relevant field space) that was also obtained \
from our previous\nwork arXiv:1812.06473. We show that the transversal \
ellipticity of the\ndeformation complex is crucial for the non-degeneracy of \
the Lagrangian and the\ncalculability of the theory. Our construction is \
valid on a large class of\nquasi toric 4 manifolds.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 29 Apr 2019 15:49:23 GMT"}, {
