            "  We study the power spectrum of dark matter density \
fluctuations in the\nframework of the Effective Field Theory of Large Scale \
Structures (EFTofLSS) up\nto three loop orders. In principle, several \
counter-terms may be needed to\nhandle the short-distance sensitivity in \
perturbation theory. However, we show\nthat a small number of extra \
coefficients are sufficient to match numerical\nsimulations with percent \
accuracy when a generic renormalization prescription\nis implemented \
(allowing for running of the individual counter-terms). We show\nthat the \
level of accuracy increases with respect to the two loop results, up\nto $k \
\\simeq 0.4\\, h$Mpc$^{-1}$ at redshift $z=0$, although the overall\n\
improvement is somewhat marginal. At the same time, we argue there is \
evidence\nthat the behavior of the loop expansion in the EFTofLSS is typical \
of an\nasymptotic series, already on the brink of its maximum predictive \
power (at\n$z=0$). Hence, the inclusion of higher orders will likely \
deteriorate the\nmatching to data, even at moderate values of $k$. Part of \
the reason for this\nbehavior is due to large contributions to the \
(renormalized) power spectrum at\nthree loop order from mildly non-linear \
scales, even after the UV counter-terms\nare included. In conclusion, the \
EFTofLSS to three loop orders provides the\nbest approximation to the \
(deterministic part of the) power spectrum in the\nweakly non-linear regime \
at $z=0$, and higher loops are not expected to improve\nour level of \
accuracy.\n", 
