Niehoff, Jesse van\\n  Muiden\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"A Holographic Kaleidoscope for \
$\\\\mathcal{N}=1^*$\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"40 pages, six figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP10(2019)185\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study in detail the recently-found family of \
asymptotically AdS$_5\\\\times\\nS^5$ type IIB supergravity solutions dual to \
the $\\\\mathcal{N}=1^*$ SYM theory\\nwith equal masses. The backgrounds \
exhibit a naked singularity and are labelled\\nby a dimensionless parameter, \
$\\\\lambda$, which is interpreted as the ratio of\\nthe gaugino condensate \
and the mass in the dual field theory. When\\n$|\\\\lambda|<1$ we show that \
the naked singularity is due to a smeared\\ndistribution of polarized $(p,q)$ \
five-branes. For this range of parameters we\\nstudy the nature of the \
singularity using probe strings and show that the dual\\nline operators \
exhibit screening behavior. These features are in line with the\\nphysics \
anticipated in the work of Polchinski-Strassler. For $|\\\\lambda|=1$ \
the\\nnaked singularity has qualitatively different behavior which has no \
clear brane\\ninterpretation. We show that when $\\\\lambda=1$ the \
singularity can be excised\\nand replaced by a smooth Euclidean supergravity \
solution with an $S^4$\\nboundary.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
