            "  We study the spectrum of semiclassical rotating strings in de \
Sitter space\nand its consistency. Even though a naive extrapolation of the \
linear Regge\ntrajectory on flat space implies a violation of the Higuchi \
bound (a unitarity\nbound on the mass of higher-spin particles in de Sitter \
space), the curved\nspace effects turn out to modify the trajectory to \
respect the bound.\nInterestingly, as a consequence of accelerated expansion, \
there exists a\nmaximum spin for each Regge trajectory, which is helpful to \
make the spectrum\nconsistent with the Higuchi bound, but at the same time, \
it could be an\nobstruction to stringy UV completion based on an infinite \
higher-spin tower. By\npushing further this observation, we demonstrate that \
the vacuum energy $V$\ninflating the universe has to be bounded by the string \
scale $M_s$ as\n$V\\lesssim M_s^4$, if UV completion is achieved with the \
leading Regge\ntrajectory of higher spin states up to the 4D Planck scale. \
Its application to\ninflation in the early universe implies an upper bound on \
the tensor-to-scalar\nratio, $r\\lesssim 0.01\\times(M_s/10^{16} \
\\text{GeV})^{4}$, which is within the\nscope of the near future CMB \
experiments. We also discuss another possibility\nthat UV completion is \
achieved by multiple Regge trajectories.\n", 
