 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Cosmic inflation may have led to non-Gaussian initial \
conditions that cannot\\nbe fully parametrised by 3- and/or 4-point \
functions. In this work, we discuss\\nvarious strategies to search for \
primordial non-Gaussianity beyond polyspectra\\nwith the help of cosmological \
data. Our starting point is a generalised local\\nansatz for the primordial \
curvature perturbation $\\\\zeta$ of the form $\\\\zeta =\\n\\\\zeta_{\\\\rm \
G} + \\\\mathcal{F}_{\\\\rm NG} (\\\\zeta_{\\\\rm G})$, where \
$\\\\zeta_{\\\\rm G}$ is\\na Gaussian random field and \
$\\\\mathcal{F}_{\\\\rm NG}$ is an arbitrary function\\nparametrising \
non-Gaussianity that, in principle, could be reconstructed from\\ndata. \
Noteworthily, in the case of multi-field inflation, the \
function\\n$\\\\mathcal{F}_{\\\\rm NG}$ can be shown to be determined by the \
shape of\\ntomographic sections of the landscape potential responsible for \
driving\\ninflation. We discuss how this generalised local ansatz leads to a \
probability\\ndistribution functional that may be used to extract information \
about inflation\\nfrom current and future observations. In particular, we \
derive various classes\\nof probability distribution functions suitable for \
the statistical analysis of\\nthe cosmic microwave background and large-scale \
structure.\\n\""}]}, {
