           "id" -> "1907.05332", "submitter" -> "Bruno Scheihing Hitschfeld", 
            "authors" -> 
            "Gonzalo A. Palma, Bruno Scheihing Hitschfeld and Spyros Sypsas", 
            "title" -> 
            "Non-Gaussian CMB and LSS statistics beyond polyspectra", 
            "comments" -> "53 pages, matches published version", 
            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1088/1475-7516/2020/02/027", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "astro-ph.CO hep-ph hep-th", 
            "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Cosmic inflation may have led to non-Gaussian initial \
conditions that cannot\nbe fully parametrised by 3- and/or 4-point functions. \
In this work, we discuss\nvarious strategies to search for primordial \
non-Gaussianity beyond polyspectra\nwith the help of cosmological data. Our \
starting point is a generalised local\nansatz for the primordial curvature \
perturbation $\\zeta$ of the form $\\zeta =\n\\zeta_{\\rm G} + \\mathcal{F}_{\
\\rm NG} (\\zeta_{\\rm G})$, where $\\zeta_{\\rm G}$ is\na Gaussian random \
field and $\\mathcal{F}_{\\rm NG}$ is an arbitrary function\nparametrising \
non-Gaussianity that, in principle, could be reconstructed from\ndata. \
Noteworthily, in the case of multi-field inflation, the function\n\
$\\mathcal{F}_{\\rm NG}$ can be shown to be determined by the shape of\n\
tomographic sections of the landscape potential responsible for driving\n\
inflation. We discuss how this generalised local ansatz leads to a \
probability\ndistribution functional that may be used to extract information \
about inflation\nfrom current and future observations. In particular, we \
derive various classes\nof probability distribution functions suitable for \
the statistical analysis of\nthe cosmic microwave background and large-scale \
structure.\n", 
