           "id" -> "1907.07186", "submitter" -> "Shu-Heng Shao", "authors" -> 
            "Shota Komatsu, Raghu Mahajan, Shu-Heng Shao", "title" -> 
            "An Index for Quantum Integrability", "comments" -> 
            "24 pages, 2 tables", "journal-ref" -> 
            "SciPost Phys. 7, 065 (2019)", "doi" -> 
            "10.21468/SciPostPhys.7.5.065", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "hep-th cond-mat.str-el nlin.SI", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  The existence of higher-spin quantum conserved currents in two \
dimensions\nguarantees quantum integrability. We revisit the question of \
whether\nclassically-conserved local higher-spin currents in two-dimensional \
sigma\nmodels survive quantization. We define an integrability index \
$\\mathcal{I}(J)$\nfor each spin $J$, with the property that \
$\\mathcal{I}(J)$ is a lower bound on\nthe number of quantum conserved \
currents of spin $J$. In particular, a positive\nvalue for the index \
establishes the existence of quantum conserved currents.\nFor a general coset \
model, with or without extra discrete symmetries, we derive\nan explicit \
formula for a generating function that encodes the indices for all\nspins. We \
apply our techniques to the $\\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}$ model, the $O(N)$\nmodel, \
and the flag sigma model $\\frac{U(N)}{U(1)^{N}}$. For the $O(N)$ model,\nwe \
establish the existence of a spin-6 quantum conserved current, in addition\n\
to the well-known spin-4 current. The indices for the $\\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}$ \
model\nfor $N>2$ are all non-positive, consistent with the fact that these \
models are\nnot integrable. The indices for the flag sigma model \
$\\frac{U(N)}{U(1)^{N}}$\nfor $N>2$ are all negative. Thus, it is unlikely \
that the flag sigma models are\nintegrable.\n", 
