               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"19 pages. This work was completed at the 2019 Matrix \
Analysis REU at\\n  College of William & Mary\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"math.SP\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  It is known (see e.g. [2], [4], [5], [6]) that \
continuous variations in the\\nentries of a complex square matrix induce \
continuous variations in its\\neigenvalues. If such a variation arises from \
one real parameter $\\\\alpha \\\\in [0,\\n1]$, then the eigenvalues follow \
continuous paths in the complex plane as\\n${\\\\alpha}$ shifts from $0$ to \
$1$. The intent here is to study the nature of\\nthese eigenpaths, including \
their behavior under small perturbations of the\\nmatrix variations, as well \
as the resulting eigenpairings of the matrices that\\noccur at ${\\\\alpha} = \
0$ and ${\\\\alpha} = 1$. We also give analogs of our results\\nin the \
setting of monic polynomials.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
