          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"1911.07792\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Sara Bonansea\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"S. Bonansea (Florence U.), S. Davoli (Parma U.), L. \
Griguolo (Parma\\n  U.) and D. Seminara (Florence U.)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Circular Wilson loops in defect ${\\\\cal N}$=4 SYM: \
phase transitions,\\n  double-scaling limits and OPE expansion\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"51 pages, 17 Figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP03(2020)084\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We consider circular Wilson loops in a defect version \
of $\\\\mathcal{N}=4$\\nsuper-Yang-Mills theory which is dual to the D3-D5 \
brane system with $k$ units\\nof flux. When the loops are parallel to the \
defect, we can construct both BPS\\nand non-BPS operators, depending on the \
orientation of the scalar couplings in\\nthe R-symmetry directions. At strong \
't Hooft coupling we observe, in the non\\nsupersymmetric case, a \
Gross-Ooguri-like phase transition in the dual\\ngravitational theory: the \
familiar disk solution dominates, as expected, when\\nthe operator is far \
from the defect while a cylindrical string worldsheet,\\nconnecting the \
boundary loop with the probe D5-brane, is favourite below a\\ncertain \
distance (or equivalently for large radii of the circles). In the BPS\\ncase, \
instead, the cylindrical solution does not exist for any choice of \
the\\nphysical parameters, suggesting that the exchange of light supergravity \
modes\\nalways saturate the expectation value at strong coupling. We study \
the\\ndouble-scaling limit for large $k$ and large 't Hooft coupling, finding \
full\\nconsistency in the non-BPS case between the string solution and the \
one-loop\\nperturbative result. Finally we discuss, in the BPS case, the \
failure of the\\ndouble-scaling limit and the OPE expansion of the Wilson \
loop, finding\\nconsistency with the known results for the one-point \
functions of scalar\\ncomposite operators.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Mon, 18 Nov 2019 17:48:10 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
