         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "1911.07792", "submitter" -> "Sara Bonansea", "authors" -> 
            "S. Bonansea (Florence U.), S. Davoli (Parma U.), L. Griguolo \
(Parma\n  U.) and D. Seminara (Florence U.)", "title" -> 
            "Circular Wilson loops in defect ${\\cal N}$=4 SYM: phase \
transitions,\n  double-scaling limits and OPE expansion", "comments" -> 
            "51 pages, 17 Figures", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/JHEP03(2020)084", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We consider circular Wilson loops in a defect version of \
$\\mathcal{N}=4$\nsuper-Yang-Mills theory which is dual to the D3-D5 brane \
system with $k$ units\nof flux. When the loops are parallel to the defect, we \
can construct both BPS\nand non-BPS operators, depending on the orientation \
of the scalar couplings in\nthe R-symmetry directions. At strong 't Hooft \
coupling we observe, in the non\nsupersymmetric case, a Gross-Ooguri-like \
phase transition in the dual\ngravitational theory: the familiar disk \
solution dominates, as expected, when\nthe operator is far from the defect \
while a cylindrical string worldsheet,\nconnecting the boundary loop with the \
probe D5-brane, is favourite below a\ncertain distance (or equivalently for \
large radii of the circles). In the BPS\ncase, instead, the cylindrical \
solution does not exist for any choice of the\nphysical parameters, \
suggesting that the exchange of light supergravity modes\nalways saturate the \
expectation value at strong coupling. We study the\ndouble-scaling limit for \
large $k$ and large 't Hooft coupling, finding full\nconsistency in the \
non-BPS case between the string solution and the one-loop\nperturbative \
result. Finally we discuss, in the BPS case, the failure of the\n\
double-scaling limit and the OPE expansion of the Wilson loop, finding\n\
consistency with the known results for the one-point functions of scalar\n\
composite operators.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 18 Nov 2019 17:48:10 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2020-04-22", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Bonansea", "S.", "", "Florence U."}, {
              "Davoli", "S.", "", "Parma U."}, {
              "Griguolo", "L.", "", "Parma\n  U."}, {
              "Seminara", "D.", "", "Florence U."}}}], "Tooltip"]& ], 
