Jean-S\\\\'ebastien\\n  Caux\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"On computing non-equilibrium dynamics following a \
quench\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"50 pages, 21 figures; v4\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"SciPost Phys. 11, 104 (2021)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.21468/SciPostPhys.11.6.104\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"cond-mat.stat-mech cond-mat.quant-gas \
cond-mat.str-el\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Computing the non-equilibrium dynamics that follows a \
quantum quench is\\ndifficult, even in exactly solvable models. Results are \
often predicated on the\\nability to compute overlaps between the initial \
state and eigenstates of the\\nHamiltonian that governs time evolution. \
Except for a handful of known cases,\\nit is generically not possible to find \
these overlaps analytically. Here we\\ndevelop a numerical approach to \
preferentially generate the states with high\\noverlaps for a quantum quench \
starting from the ground state or an excited\\nstate of an initial \
Hamiltonian. We use these preferentially generated states,\\nin combination \
with a \\\"high overlap states truncation scheme\\\" and a\\nmodification of \
the numerical renormalization group, to compute non-equilibrium\\ndynamics \
following a quench in the Lieb-Liniger model. The method \
is\\nnon-perturbative, works for reasonable numbers of particles, and applies \
to\\nboth continuum and lattice systems. It can also be easily extended to \
more\\ncomplicated scenarios, including those with integrability breaking.\\n\
\""}]}, {
