            "cond-mat.stat-mech cond-mat.quant-gas cond-mat.str-el", 
            "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Computing the non-equilibrium dynamics that follows a quantum \
quench is\ndifficult, even in exactly solvable models. Results are often \
predicated on the\nability to compute overlaps between the initial state and \
eigenstates of the\nHamiltonian that governs time evolution. Except for a \
handful of known cases,\nit is generically not possible to find these \
overlaps analytically. Here we\ndevelop a numerical approach to \
preferentially generate the states with high\noverlaps for a quantum quench \
starting from the ground state or an excited\nstate of an initial \
Hamiltonian. We use these preferentially generated states,\nin combination \
with a \"high overlap states truncation scheme\" and a\nmodification of the \
numerical renormalization group, to compute non-equilibrium\ndynamics \
following a quench in the Lieb-Liniger model. The method is\n\
non-perturbative, works for reasonable numbers of particles, and applies to\n\
both continuum and lattice systems. It can also be easily extended to more\n\
complicated scenarios, including those with integrability breaking.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
