         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "2004.05071", "submitter" -> "Shota Komatsu", "authors" -> 
            "Joao Caetano, Shota Komatsu", "title" -> 
            
            "Functional Equations and Separation of Variables for Exact \
g-Function", "comments" -> "27 pages + appendices; v2 typos corrected", 
            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP09(2020)180", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th math-ph math.MP", 
            "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  The g-function is a measure of degrees of freedom associated \
to a boundary of\ntwo-dimensional quantum field theories. In integrable \
theories, it can be\ncomputed exactly in a form of the Fredholm determinant, \
but it is often hard to\nevaluate numerically. In this paper, we derive \
functional equations---or\nequivalently integral equations of the \
thermodynamic Bethe ansatz (TBA)\ntype---which directly compute the \
g-function in the simplest integrable theory;\nthe sinh-Gordon theory at the \
self-dual point. The derivation is based on the\nclassic result by Tracy and \
Widom on the relation between Fredholm determinants\nand TBA, which was used \
also in the context of topological string. We\ndemonstrate the efficiency of \
our formulation through the numerical computation\nand compare the results in \
the UV limit with the Liouville CFT. As a side\nresult, we present multiple \
integrals of Q-functions which we conjecture to\ndescribe a universal part of \
the g-function, and discuss its implication to\nintegrable spin chains.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 10 Apr 2020 15:24:04 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Sun, 19 Apr 2020 02:52:53 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2020-10-28", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Caetano", "Joao", ""}, {
              "Komatsu", "Shota", ""}}}], "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
