          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2004.07592\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Jia Tian\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Jun-bao Wu, Jia Tian and Bin Chen\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Loop Operators in Three-Dimensional $\\\\mathcal{N}=2$ \
Fishnet Theories\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"36 pages, 13 figures, version4, match the published \
version\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP07(2020)215\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP07(2020)215\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"CJQS-202-023\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  In this work, we study the line and loop operators in \
three-dimensional\\n${\\\\mathcal N}=2$ fishnet theories in detail. We \
construct the straight line and\\ncircular loop operators which are at least \
classically half-BPS. We develop a\\nnew regularization scheme at frame $-1$ \
which is suitable for the study of the\\nfermionic BPS loops in general \
super-Chern-Simons-matter theories. We\\ninitialize the perturbative \
computation for the vacuum expectation values of\\nthe circular BPS loop \
operators based on this scheme. We construct the cusped\\nline operators as \
well, and compute the vacuum expectation values of these\\ncusped line \
operators up to two-loop order. We find that the universal cusp\\nanomalous \
dimension vanishes, if we put aside the fact that the generalized\\npotential \
has a double pole in the $1/\\\\epsilon$ expansion.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
