               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"M. Beccaria, M. Billo, F. Galvagno, A. Hasan and A. Lerda\
\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"N=2 Conformal SYM theories at large N\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"58 pages, several figures. v2: some comments added in the \
Conclusion\\n  section, a few references added. Version to be published on \
JHEP\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP09(2020)116\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We consider a class of N=2 conformal SU(N) SYM theories \
in four dimensions\\nwith matter in the fundamental, two-index symmetric and \
anti-symmetric\\nrepresentations, and study the corresponding matrix model \
provided by\\nlocalization on a sphere S4, which also encodes information on \
flat-space\\nobservables involving chiral operators and circular BPS Wilson \
loops. We review\\nand improve known techniques for studying the matrix model \
in the large-N\\nlimit, deriving explicit expressions in perturbation theory \
for these\\nobservables. We exploit both recursive methods in the so-called \
full Lie\\nalgebra approach and the more standard Cartan sub-algebra approach \
based on the\\neigenvalue distribution. The sub-class of conformal theories \
for which the\\nnumber of fundamental hypermultiplets does not scale with N \
differs in the\\nplanar limit from the N=4 SYM theory only in observables \
involving chiral\\noperators of odd dimension. In this case we are able to \
derive compact\\nexpressions which allow to push the small 't Hooft coupling \
expansion to very\\nhigh orders. We argue that the perturbative series have a \
finite radius of\\nconvergence and extrapolate them numerically to \
intermediate couplings. This is\\npreliminary to an analytic investigation of \
the strong coupling behavior, which\\nwould be very interesting given that \
for such theories holographic duals have\\nbeen proposed.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
