            "  We study the quantum properties of a Galilean-invariant \
abelian gauge theory\ncoupled to a Schr\\\"odinger scalar in 2+1 dimensions. \
At the classical level,\nthe theory with minimal coupling is obtained from a \
null-reduction of\nrelativistic Maxwell theory coupled to a complex scalar \
field in 3+1 dimensions\nand is closely related to the Galilean \
electromagnetism of Le-Bellac and\nL\\'evy-Leblond. Due to the presence of a \
dimensionless, gauge-invariant scalar\nfield in the Galilean multiplet of the \
gauge-field, we find that at the quantum\nlevel an infinite number of \
couplings is generated. We explain how to handle\nthe quantum corrections \
systematically using the background field method. Due\nto a \
non-renormalization theorem, the beta function of the gauge coupling is\n\
found to vanish to all orders in perturbation theory, leading to a continuous\
\nfamily of fixed points where the non-relativistic conformal symmetry is\n\
preserved.\n", 
