               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2007.10998\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Shouvik Datta\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Diptarka Das, Shouvik Datta, Madhusudhan Raman\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Virasoro blocks and quasimodular forms\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"35 pages, 2 figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP11(2020)010\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"CERN-TH-2020-119\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We analyse Virasoro conformal blocks in the regime of \
heavy intermediate\\nexchange $(h_p \\\\rightarrow \\\\infty)$. For the \
1-point block on the torus and the\\n4-point block on the sphere, we show \
that each order in the large-$h_p$\\nexpansion can be written in closed form \
as polynomials in the Eisenstein\\nseries. The appearance of this structure \
is explained using the fusion kernel\\nand, more markedly, by invoking the \
modular anomaly equations via the 2d/4d\\ncorrespondence. We observe that the \
existence of these constraints allows us to\\ndevelop a faster algorithm to \
recursively construct the blocks in this regime.\\nWe then apply our results \
to find corrections to averaged heavy-heavy-light \
OPE\\ncoefficients.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
