               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Enej Ilievski\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Enej Ilievski, Jacopo De Nardis, Sarang Gopalakrishnan, \
Romain\\n  Vasseur, Brayden Ware\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Superuniversality of superdiffusion\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"44 pages, 9 figures, 5 tables (minor corrections)\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Phys. Rev. X 11, 031023 (2021)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevX.11.031023\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"cond-mat.stat-mech cond-mat.str-el math-ph math.MP\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Anomalous finite-temperature transport has recently \
been observed in\\nnumerical studies of various integrable models in one \
dimension; these models\\nshare the feature of being invariant under a \
continuous non-abelian global\\nsymmetry. This work offers a comprehensive \
group-theoretic account of this\\nelusive phenomenon. For an integrable \
quantum model invariant under a global\\nnon-abelian simple Lie group $G$, we \
find that finite-temperature transport of\\nNoether charges associated with \
symmetry $G$ in thermal states that are\\ninvariant under $G$ is universally \
superdiffusive and characterized by\\ndynamical exponent $z = 3/2$. This \
conclusion holds regardless of the Lie\\nalgebra symmetry, local degrees of \
freedom (on-site representations), Lorentz\\ninvariance, or particular \
realization of microscopic interactions: we\\naccordingly dub it as \
superuniversal. The anomalous transport behavior is\\nattributed to \
long-lived giant quasiparticles dressed by thermal fluctuations.\\nWe provide \
an algebraic viewpoint on the corresponding dressing transformation\\nand \
elucidate formal connections to fusion identities amongst the \
quantum-group\\ncharacters. We identify giant quasiparticles with nonlinear \
soliton modes of\\nclassical field theories that describe low-energy \
excitations above\\nferromagnetic vacua. Our analysis of these field theories \
also provides a\\ncomplete classification of the low-energy (i.e., \
Goldstone-mode) spectra of\\nquantum isotropic ferromagnetic \
chains.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
