observed in\nnumerical studies of various integrable models in one dimension; \
these models\nshare the feature of being invariant under a continuous \
non-abelian global\nsymmetry. This work offers a comprehensive \
group-theoretic account of this\nelusive phenomenon. For an integrable \
quantum model invariant under a global\nnon-abelian simple Lie group $G$, we \
find that finite-temperature transport of\nNoether charges associated with \
symmetry $G$ in thermal states that are\ninvariant under $G$ is universally \
superdiffusive and characterized by\ndynamical exponent $z = 3/2$. This \
conclusion holds regardless of the Lie\nalgebra symmetry, local degrees of \
freedom (on-site representations), Lorentz\ninvariance, or particular \
realization of microscopic interactions: we\naccordingly dub it as \
superuniversal. The anomalous transport behavior is\nattributed to long-lived \
giant quasiparticles dressed by thermal fluctuations.\nWe provide an \
algebraic viewpoint on the corresponding dressing transformation\nand \
elucidate formal connections to fusion identities amongst the quantum-group\n\
characters. We identify giant quasiparticles with nonlinear soliton modes of\n\
classical field theories that describe low-energy excitations above\n\
ferromagnetic vacua. Our analysis of these field theories also provides a\n\
complete classification of the low-energy (i.e., Goldstone-mode) spectra of\n\
quantum isotropic ferromagnetic chains.\n", 
