                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Christian B. Jepsen, Igor R. Klebanov, and Fedor K. Popov\
\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"RG Limit Cycles and Unconventional Fixed Points in \
Perturbative QFT\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"v4: typo in eq. (41) corrected and discussion near it \
improved\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Phys. Rev. D 103, 046015 (2021)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.103.046015\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study quantum field theories with sextic \
interactions in $3-\\\\epsilon$\\ndimensions, where the scalar fields \
$\\\\phi^{ab}$ form irreducible\\nrepresentations under the $O(N)^2$ or \
$O(N)$ global symmetry group. We\\ncalculate the beta functions up to \
four-loop order and find the Renormalization\\nGroup fixed points. In an \
example of large $N$ equivalence, the parent $O(N)^2$\\ntheory and its \
anti-symmetric projection exhibit identical large $N$ beta\\nfunctions which \
possess real fixed points. However, for projection to the\\nsymmetric \
traceless representation of $O(N)$, the large $N$ equivalence is\\nviolated \
by the appearance of an additional double-trace operator not inherited\\nfrom \
the parent theory. Among the large $N$ fixed points of this daughter\\ntheory \
we find complex CFTs. The symmetric traceless $O(N)$ model also \
exhibits\\nvery interesting phenomena when it is analytically continued to \
small\\nnon-integer values of $N$. Here we find unconventional fixed points, \
which we\\ncall \\\"spooky.\\\" They are located at real values of the \
coupling constants $g^i$,\\nbut two eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix \
$\\\\partial \\\\beta^i/\\\\partial g^j$ are\\ncomplex. When these complex \
conjugate eigenvalues cross the imaginary axis, a\\nHopf bifurcation occurs, \
giving rise to RG limit cycles. This crossing occurs\\nfor $N_{\\\\rm crit} \
\\\\approx 4.475$, and for a small range of $N$ above this value\\nwe find RG \
flows which lead to limit cycles.\\n\""}]}, {
