            "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We study quantum field theories with sextic interactions in \
$3-\\epsilon$\ndimensions, where the scalar fields $\\phi^{ab}$ form \
irreducible\nrepresentations under the $O(N)^2$ or $O(N)$ global symmetry \
group. We\ncalculate the beta functions up to four-loop order and find the \
Renormalization\nGroup fixed points. In an example of large $N$ equivalence, \
the parent $O(N)^2$\ntheory and its anti-symmetric projection exhibit \
identical large $N$ beta\nfunctions which possess real fixed points. However, \
for projection to the\nsymmetric traceless representation of $O(N)$, the \
large $N$ equivalence is\nviolated by the appearance of an additional \
double-trace operator not inherited\nfrom the parent theory. Among the large \
$N$ fixed points of this daughter\ntheory we find complex CFTs. The symmetric \
traceless $O(N)$ model also exhibits\nvery interesting phenomena when it is \
analytically continued to small\nnon-integer values of $N$. Here we find \
unconventional fixed points, which we\ncall \"spooky.\" They are located at \
real values of the coupling constants $g^i$,\nbut two eigenvalues of the \
Jacobian matrix $\\partial \\beta^i/\\partial g^j$ are\ncomplex. When these \
complex conjugate eigenvalues cross the imaginary axis, a\nHopf bifurcation \
occurs, giving rise to RG limit cycles. This crossing occurs\nfor $N_{\\rm \
crit} \\approx 4.475$, and for a small range of $N$ above this value\nwe find \
RG flows which lead to limit cycles.\n", 
