         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "2011.09506", "submitter" -> "Skenderis Kostas", "authors" -> 
            "George Katsianis, Ioannis Papadimitriou, Kostas Skenderis, \
Marika\n  Taylor", "title" -> 
            "Supersymmetry anomaly in the superconformal Wess-Zumino model", 
            "comments" -> 
            "37 pages + 45 pages appendices v2: minor improvements and \
amendments;\n  v3: minor additions, version to appear in JHEP", "journal-ref" -> 
            "J. High Energ. Phys. 2021, 209 (2021)", "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/JHEP02(2021)209", "report-no" -> "KIAS-P20067", 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We present a comprehensive analysis of supersymmetry anomalies \
in the free\nand massless Wess-Zumino (WZ) model in perturbation theory. At \
the classical\nlevel the model possesses ${\\mathcal N}=1$ superconformal \
symmetry, which is\npartially broken by quantum anomalies. The form of the \
anomalies and the part\nof the symmetry they break depend on the multiplet of \
conserved currents used.\nIt was previously shown that the R-symmetry anomaly \
of the conformal current\nmultiplet induces an anomaly in Q-supersymmetry, \
which appears first in 4-point\nfunctions. Here we confirm this result by an \
explicit 1-loop computation using\na supersymmetric Pauli-Villars regulator.\n\
  The conformal current multiplet does not exist in the regulated theory\n\
because the regulator breaks conformal invariance, R-symmetry and\n\
S-supersymmetry explicitly. The minimal massive multiplet is the \
Ferrara-Zumino\n(FZ) one and the supersymmetry preserved by the regulator is \
a specific field\ndependent combination of Q- and S- supersymmetry of the \
conformal multiplet.\nWhile this supersymmetry is non anomalous, conformal \
invariance, R-symmetry and\nthe original Q- and S-supersymmetries are \
explicitly broken by finite contact\nterms, both in the regulated and \
renormalized theories.\n  A conformal current multiplet does exist for the \
renormalized theory and may\nbe obtained from the FZ multiplet by a set of \
finite local counterterms that\neliminate the explicit symmetry breaking, \
thus restoring superconformal\ninvariance up to anomalies. However, this \
necessarily renders both Q- and\nS-supersymmetries anomalous, as is manifest \
starting at 4-point functions of\nconformal multiplet currents. The paper \
contains a detailed discussion of a\nnumber of issues and subtleties related \
to Ward identities that may be useful\nin a wider context.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Wed, 18 Nov 2020 19:19:50 GMT"}, {
