Kashani-Poor\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Weyl invariant Jacobi forms along Higgsing trees\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"57 pages; v2 64 pages: discussion of specialization of \
Gromov-Witten\\n  invariants enhanced and moved to body of paper, with \
additional tables in\\n  appendix\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP04(2021)224\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th math.AG\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Using topological string techniques, we compute BPS \
counting functions of 5d\\ngauge theories which descend from 6d \
superconformal field theories upon circle\\ncompactification. Such theories \
are naturally organized in terms of nodes of\\nHiggsing trees. We demonstrate \
that the specialization of the partition\\nfunction as we move from the crown \
to the root of a tree is determined by\\nhomomorphisms between rings of Weyl \
invariant Jacobi forms. Our computations\\nare made feasible by the fact that \
symmetry enhancements of the gauge theory\\nwhich are manifest on the \
massless spectrum are inherited by the entire tower\\nof BPS particles. In \
some cases, these symmetry enhancements have a nice\\nrelation to the 1-form \
symmetry of the associated gauge theory.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
