            "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  This review is dedicated to two-dimensional sigma models with \
flag manifold\ntarget spaces, which are generalizations of the familiar \
$CP^{n-1}$ and\nGrassmannian models. They naturally arise in the description \
of continuum\nlimits of spin chains, and their phase structure is sensitive \
to the values of\nthe topological angles, which are determined by the \
representations of spins in\nthe chain. Gapless phases can in certain cases \
be explained by the presence of\ndiscrete 't Hooft anomalies in the continuum \
theory. We also discuss integrable\nflag manifold sigma models, which provide \
a generalization of the theory of\nintegrable models with symmetric target \
spaces. These models, as well as their\ndeformations, have an alternative \
equivalent formulation as bosonic Gross-Neveu\nmodels, which proves useful \
for demonstrating that the deformed geometries are\nsolutions of the \
renormalization group (Ricci flow) equations, as well as for\nthe analysis of \
anomalies and for describing potential couplings to fermions.\n", 
