hot carriers in\nconventional metals. This is predominantly the result of \
graphene's linear\nenergy-momentum dispersion, its phonon properties, its \
all-interface character,\nand the tunability of its carrier density down to \
very small values, and from\nelectron- to hole-doping. Since a few years, we \
have witnessed an increasing\ninterest in technological applications enabled \
by hot carriers in graphene. Of\nparticular interest are optical and \
optoelectronic applications, where hot\ncarriers are used to detect \
(photodetection), convert (nonlinear photonics), or\nemit (luminescence) \
light. Graphene-enabled systems in these application areas\ncould find \
widespread use and have a disruptive impact, for example in the\nfield of \
data communication, high-frequency electronics, and industrial quality\n\
control. The aim of this review is to provide an overview of the most \
relevant\nphysics and working principles that are relevant for applications \
exploiting\nhot carriers in graphene.\n", 
