               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Supersymmetry breaking, brane dynamics and Swampland \
conjectures\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"41 pages, 6 figures. v2: references added\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP10(2021)080\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th gr-qc\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We investigate interactions between branes of various \
dimensions, both\\ncharged and uncharged, in three non-supersymmetric string \
models. These include\\nthe $USp(32)$ and $U(32)$ orientifold projections of \
the type IIB and type $0$B\\nstrings, as well as the $SO(16) \\\\times \
SO(16)$ projection of the exceptional\\nheterotic string. The resulting \
ten-dimensional spectra are free of tachyons,\\nand the combinations of \
branes that they contain give rise to rich and varied\\ndynamics. We compute \
static potentials for parallel stacks of branes in three\\ncomplementary \
regimes: the probe regime, in which one of the two stacks is\\nparametrically \
heavier than the other, the string-amplitude regime, in which\\nboth stacks \
are light, and the holographic regime. Whenever comparisons are\\npossible, \
we find qualitative agreement despite the absence of supersymmetry.\\nFor \
charged branes, our analysis reveals that the Weak Gravity Conjecture \
is\\nsatisfied in a novel way via a renormalization of the \
effective\\ncharge-to-tension ratio.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
