            "  In theories with renormalons the perturbative series is \
factorially divergent\neven after restricting to a given order in $1/N$, \
making the $1/N$ expansion a\nnatural testing ground for the theory of \
resurgence. We study in detail the\ninterplay between resurgent properties \
and the $1/N$ expansion in various\nintegrable field theories with \
renormalons. We focus on the free energy in the\npresence of a chemical \
potential coupled to a conserved charge, which can be\ncomputed exactly with \
the thermodynamic Bethe ansatz (TBA). In some examples,\nlike the first $1/N$ \
correction to the free energy in the non-linear sigma\nmodel, the terms in \
the $1/N$ expansion can be fully decoded in terms of a\nresurgent \
trans-series in the coupling constant. In the principal chiral field\nwe find \
a new, explicit solution for the large $N$ free energy which can be\nwritten \
as the median resummation of a trans-series with infinitely many,\n\
analytically computable IR renormalon corrections. However, in other \
examples,\nlike the Gross-Neveu model, each term in the $1/N$ expansion \
includes\nnon-perturbative corrections which can not be predicted by a \
resurgent analysis\nof the corresponding perturbative series. We also study \
the properties of the\nseries in $1/N$. In the Gross-Neveu model, where this \
is convergent, we\nanalytically continue the series beyond its radius of \
convergence and show how\nthe continuation matches with known dualities with \
sine-Gordon theories.\n", 
