               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Standard Model EFTs via On-Shell Methods\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"39 + 14 pages; v3:matched published version, typos \
corrected\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th hep-ph\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We present the Standard Model Effective Field Theories \
(SMEFT) from purely\\non-shell arguments. Starting from a few basic \
assumptions such as Poincar\\\\'e\\ninvariance and locality, we classify all \
the renormalisable and\\nnon-renormalisable interactions at the lowest order \
in the couplings. From\\nthese building blocks, we review how locality and \
unitarity enforce Lie algebra\\nstructures to appear in the S-matrix elements \
together with relations among\\ncouplings (and hypercharges). Furthermore, we \
give a fully on-shell algorithm\\nto compute any higher-point tree-level \
amplitude (or form factor) in generic\\nEFTs, bypassing BCFW-like recursion \
relations which are known to be problematic\\nwhen non-renormalisable \
interactions are involved. Finally, using known\\namplitudes techniques we \
compute the mixing matrix of SMEFT irrelevant\\ninteractions up to mass \
dimension 8, to linear order in the effective\\ninteractions.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
