                 "\"Bootstrapping holographic defect correlators in \
$\\\\mathcal{N}=4$ super\\n  Yang-Mills\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"31 pages, 1 Mathematica notebook\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP04(2022)093\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study two-point functions of single-trace half-BPS \
operators in the\\npresence of a supersymmetric Wilson line in \
$\\\\mathcal{N}=4$ SYM. We use\\ninversion formula technology in order to \
reconstruct the CFT data starting from\\na single discontinuity of the \
correlator. In the planar strong coupling limit\\nonly a finite number of \
conformal blocks contributes to the discontinuity,\\nwhich allows us to \
obtain elegant closed-form expressions for two-point\\nfunctions of \
single-trace operators $\\\\mathcal{O}_J$ of weight $J=2,3,4$. Our\\nfinal \
result passes a number of non-trivial consistency checks: it has \
the\\ncorrect discontinuity, it satisfies the superconformal Ward identities, \
it has\\na sensible expansion in both defect and bulk OPEs, and is consistent \
with\\navailable results coming from localization. The method is \
completely\\nalgorithmic and can be implemented to calculate correlators of \
arbitrary\\nweight.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
