Bertini\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Real-Time Evolution in the Hubbard Model with Infinite \
Repulsion\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"25 pages, 4 figures\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"SciPost Phys. 12, 028 (2022)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.21468/SciPostPhys.12.1.028\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"cond-mat.stat-mech cond-mat.quant-gas cond-mat.str-el \
quant-ph\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We consider the real-time evolution of the Hubbard \
model in the limit of\\ninfinite coupling. In this limit the Hamiltonian of \
the system is mapped into a\\nnumber-conserving quadratic form of spinless \
fermions, i.e. the tight binding\\nmodel. The relevant local observables, \
however, do not transform well under\\nthis mapping and take very complicated \
expressions in terms of the spinless\\nfermions. Here we show that for two \
classes of interesting observables the\\nquench dynamics from product states \
in the occupation basis can be determined\\nexactly in terms of correlations \
in the tight-binding model. In particular, we\\nshow that the time evolution \
of any function of the total density of particles\\nis mapped directly into \
that of the same function of the density of spinless\\nfermions in the \
tight-binding model. Moreover, we express the two-point\\nfunctions of the \
spin-full fermions at any time after the quench in terms of\\ncorrelations of \
the tight binding model. This sum is generically very\\ncomplicated but we \
show that it leads to simple explicit expressions for the\\ntime evolution of \
the densities of the two separate species and the\\ncorrelations between a \
point at the boundary and one in the bulk when evolving\\nfrom the so called \
generalised nested N\\\\'eel states.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Thu, 30 Sep 2021 17:51:01 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
