           "id" -> "2109.15305", "submitter" -> "Bruno Bertini", "authors" -> 
            "Elena Tartaglia, Pasquale Calabrese, and Bruno Bertini", "title" -> 
            "Real-Time Evolution in the Hubbard Model with Infinite \
Repulsion", "comments" -> "25 pages, 4 figures", "journal-ref" -> 
            "SciPost Phys. 12, 028 (2022)", "doi" -> 
            "10.21468/SciPostPhys.12.1.028", "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> 
            "cond-mat.stat-mech cond-mat.quant-gas cond-mat.str-el quant-ph", 
            "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We consider the real-time evolution of the Hubbard model in \
the limit of\ninfinite coupling. In this limit the Hamiltonian of the system \
is mapped into a\nnumber-conserving quadratic form of spinless fermions, i.e. \
the tight binding\nmodel. The relevant local observables, however, do not \
transform well under\nthis mapping and take very complicated expressions in \
terms of the spinless\nfermions. Here we show that for two classes of \
interesting observables the\nquench dynamics from product states in the \
occupation basis can be determined\nexactly in terms of correlations in the \
tight-binding model. In particular, we\nshow that the time evolution of any \
function of the total density of particles\nis mapped directly into that of \
the same function of the density of spinless\nfermions in the tight-binding \
model. Moreover, we express the two-point\nfunctions of the spin-full \
fermions at any time after the quench in terms of\ncorrelations of the tight \
binding model. This sum is generically very\ncomplicated but we show that it \
leads to simple explicit expressions for the\ntime evolution of the densities \
of the two separate species and the\ncorrelations between a point at the \
boundary and one in the bulk when evolving\nfrom the so called generalised \
nested N\\'eel states.\n", 
