           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2110.07614\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Monica Guica\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Monica Guica\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"$J\\\\bar T$-deformed CFTs as non-local CFTs\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"34 pp\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Various holographic set-ups in string theory suggest \
the existence of\\nnon-local, UV complete two-dimensional QFTs that possess \
Virasoro symmetry, in\\nspite of their non-locality. We argue that $J\\\\bar \
T$-deformed CFTs are the\\nfirst concrete realisation of such \\\"non-local \
CFTs\\\", through a detailed\\nanalysis of their classical and quantum \
symmetry algebra. Classically, the\\nsymmetries consist of an infinite set of \
left-moving conformal and affine\\n$U(1)$ transformations that generate a \
Witt-Kac-Moody algebra, as well as a set\\nof non-local, field-dependent \
generalizations of right-moving conformal and\\naffine $U(1)$ \
transformations, whose algebra depends on the chosen basis.\\nNotably, there \
exists a basis, denoted as the \\\"flowed\\\" representation, in which\\nthe \
right-moving charge algebra is simply Witt-Kac-Moody. At the quantum \
level,\\nwe provide a concrete prescription for constructing the symmetry \
generators via\\na combination of the flow equations they satisfy and the \
Sugawara construction,\\nand use this to explicitly resolve the ordering \
ambiguities and the quantum\\ncorrections to the generators up to second \
order in the $J\\\\bar T$ coupling\\nparameter. This construction naturally \
produces the \\\"flowed\\\" generators, whose\\nalgebra is Virasoro-Kac-Moody \
to all orders in the coupling, with the same\\ncentral extension as that of \
the undeformed CFT. We use this input to work out\\nthe quantum modifications \
to the \\\"unflowed\\\" generator algebra. A peculiarity of\\nthe Virasoro \
generators we study is that their zero mode does not equal the\\nHamiltonian, \
but is a quadratic function of it; this helps reconcile the\\nVirasoro \
symmetry with the non-locality of the model. We argue that also \
$T\\\\bar\\nT$-deformed CFTs posses Virasoro symmetry, and discuss the \
existence of such a\\nsymmetry in more general non-local QFTs.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Thu, 14 Oct 2021 18:00:01 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2021-10-18\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Guica\"", ",", "\"Monica\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}], "}"}]}]}}, RowSpacings -> 1, ColumnAlignments -> 
