            "  Various holographic set-ups in string theory suggest the \
existence of\nnon-local, UV complete two-dimensional QFTs that possess \
Virasoro symmetry, in\nspite of their non-locality. We argue that $J\\bar \
T$-deformed CFTs are the\nfirst concrete realisation of such \"non-local CFTs\
\", through a detailed\nanalysis of their classical and quantum symmetry \
algebra. Classically, the\nsymmetries consist of an infinite set of \
left-moving conformal and affine\n$U(1)$ transformations that generate a \
Witt-Kac-Moody algebra, as well as a set\nof non-local, field-dependent \
generalizations of right-moving conformal and\naffine $U(1)$ transformations, \
whose algebra depends on the chosen basis.\nNotably, there exists a basis, \
denoted as the \"flowed\" representation, in which\nthe right-moving charge \
algebra is simply Witt-Kac-Moody. At the quantum level,\nwe provide a \
concrete prescription for constructing the symmetry generators via\na \
combination of the flow equations they satisfy and the Sugawara construction,\
\nand use this to explicitly resolve the ordering ambiguities and the quantum\
\ncorrections to the generators up to second order in the $J\\bar T$ coupling\
\nparameter. This construction naturally produces the \"flowed\" generators, \
whose\nalgebra is Virasoro-Kac-Moody to all orders in the coupling, with the \
same\ncentral extension as that of the undeformed CFT. We use this input to \
work out\nthe quantum modifications to the \"unflowed\" generator algebra. A \
peculiarity of\nthe Virasoro generators we study is that their zero mode does \
not equal the\nHamiltonian, but is a quadratic function of it; this helps \
reconcile the\nVirasoro symmetry with the non-locality of the model. We argue \
that also $T\\bar\nT$-deformed CFTs posses Virasoro symmetry, and discuss the \
existence of such a\nsymmetry in more general non-local QFTs.\n", 
