            "authors" -> "Dionysios Anninos and Beatrix M\\\"uhlmann", 
            "title" -> 
            "The semiclassical gravitational path integral and random \
matrices", "comments" -> 
            "21 pages + appendices, v2 typos corrected + references added, v3\
\n  typos corrected", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null, "report-no" -> 
            Null, "categories" -> "hep-th gr-qc", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We study the genus expansion on compact Riemann surfaces of \
the gravitational\npath integral $\\mathcal{Z}^{(m)}_{\\text{grav}}$ in two \
spacetime dimensions\nwith cosmological constant $\\Lambda>0$ coupled to one \
of the non-unitary\nminimal models $\\mathcal{M}_{2m-1,2}$. In the \
semiclassical limit,\ncorresponding to large $m$, \
$\\mathcal{Z}^{(m)}_{\\text{grav}}$ admits a\nEuclidean saddle for genus \
$h\\geq 2$. Upon fixing the area of the metric, the\npath integral admits a \
round two-sphere saddle for $h=0$. We show that the OPE\ncoefficients for the \
minimal weight operators of $\\mathcal{M}_{2m-1,2}$ grow\nexponentially in \
$m$ at large $m$. Employing the sewing formula, we use these\nOPE \
coefficients to obtain the large $m$ limit of the partition function of\n\
$\\mathcal{M}_{2m-1,2}$ for genus $h\\ge 2$. Combining these results we \
arrive at\na semiclassical expression for \
$\\mathcal{Z}^{(m)}_{\\text{grav}}$.\nConjecturally, \
$\\mathcal{Z}^{(m)}_{\\text{grav}}$ admits a completion in terms\nof an \
integral over large random Hermitian matrices, known as a multicritical\n\
matrix integral. This matrix integral is built from an even polynomial\n\
potential of order $2m$. We obtain explicit expressions for the large $m$ \
genus\nexpansion of multicritical matrix integrals in the double scaling \
limit. We\ncompute invariant quantities involving contributions at different \
genera, both\nfrom a matrix as well as a gravity perspective, and establish a \
link between\nthe two pictures. Inspired by the proposal of Gibbons and \
Hawking relating the\nde Sitter entropy to a gravitational path integral, our \
setup paves a possible\npath toward a microscopic picture of a \
two-dimensional de Sitter universe.\n", 
