 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study 1/2-BPS Wilson loop (WL) operators in \
maximally supersymmetric\\nYang-Mills theory (MSYM) on $\\\\mathbf{S}^5$. \
Their vacuum expectation value\\n(vev) can be computed exactly at large $N$ \
thanks to supersymmetric\\nlocalization. The holographic dual to MSYM on \
$\\\\mathbf{S}^5$ is geometrically\\nrealized by a stack of $N$ D4-branes \
with spherical worldvolume in ten\\ndimensions. We compute the vev of the \
circular WL using holography by\\nevaluating the partition function of a \
fundamental string in this background.\\nOur focus is on the next-to-leading \
order correction to the string partition\\nfunction which is composed of two \
parts; the dilaton coupling to the worldsheet\\nand the one-loop fluctuations \
of the worldsheet itself. We face a variety of\\nissues, some related to the \
presence of a non-constant dilaton, and others that\\nare common to its AdS \
analogue. However, the universality of UV divergences as\\nwell as the \
importance of a proper choice of an IR regulator have been \
recently\\nstressed in the literature. Inspired by this, we resolve our \
issues by first\\ncarefully treating the Weyl anomaly which receives \
contributions from the\\nnon-constant dilaton, and then by computing the \
ratio of our partition function\\nand the one of a string in AdS$_4\\\\times \
\\\\mathbf{C}P^3$, which is dual to a\\n1/2-BPS WL in ABJM. Crucially, this \
approach yields a finite result which\\nmatches the corresponding ratio of WL \
vevs on the gauge theory side.\\n\""}]}, {
