               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2112.14270\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Guillermo A. Silva\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Matias N. Semp\\\\'e and Guillermo A. Silva\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Fermionic Matrix Models and Bosonization\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"30 pages, 7 figures. Final version accepted in PRD\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.106.106005\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"hep-th gr-qc math-ph math.MP\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We explore different limits of exactly solvable vector \
and matrix fermionic\\nquantum mechanical models with quartic interactions at \
finite temperature. The\\nmodels preserve a $U(1)\\\\times SU(N)\\\\times \
SU(L)$ symmetry at the classical\\nlevel and we analyze them through \
bosonization techniques introducing scalar\\n(singlet) and matrix \
(non-singlet) bosonic fields. The bosonic path integral\\nrepresentations in \
the vector limits $(N,1)$ and $(1,L)$ are matched to\\nfermionic Fock space \
Hamiltonians expressed in terms of quadratic Casimirs and\\nsome additional \
terms involving the Cartan subalgebra, which makes explicit the\\nsymmetries \
preserved by scalar and matrix bosonizations at the quantum level.\\nFor the \
case of non-singlet bosonization we find an equivalence between the\\nvector \
model and the Polychronakos+Frahm spin model. Using this relation \
we\\ncompute the free energy. Finally, we compute the eigenvalue distribution \
in the\\nlarge $N,L$-limit with $ \\\\alpha = \\\\frac{L}{N}$ fixed. The \
model displays a\\nthird order phase transition as we vary the temperature \
which, in the\\n$\\\\alpha\\\\gg1$ limit, can be characterized analytically. \
We conclude finding the\\ncritical curve in the parameter space were the \
eigenvalue distribution\\ntransitions from single to double cut.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
