            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "hep-th gr-qc math-ph math.MP", "license" -> 
            "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We explore different limits of exactly solvable vector and \
matrix fermionic\nquantum mechanical models with quartic interactions at \
finite temperature. The\nmodels preserve a $U(1)\\times SU(N)\\times SU(L)$ \
symmetry at the classical\nlevel and we analyze them through bosonization \
techniques introducing scalar\n(singlet) and matrix (non-singlet) bosonic \
fields. The bosonic path integral\nrepresentations in the vector limits \
$(N,1)$ and $(1,L)$ are matched to\nfermionic Fock space Hamiltonians \
expressed in terms of quadratic Casimirs and\nsome additional terms involving \
the Cartan subalgebra, which makes explicit the\nsymmetries preserved by \
scalar and matrix bosonizations at the quantum level.\nFor the case of \
non-singlet bosonization we find an equivalence between the\nvector model and \
the Polychronakos+Frahm spin model. Using this relation we\ncompute the free \
energy. Finally, we compute the eigenvalue distribution in the\nlarge \
$N,L$-limit with $ \\alpha = \\frac{L}{N}$ fixed. The model displays a\nthird \
order phase transition as we vary the temperature which, in the\n\
$\\alpha\\gg1$ limit, can be characterized analytically. We conclude finding \
the\ncritical curve in the parameter space were the eigenvalue distribution\n\
transitions from single to double cut.\n", 
