               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2202.01591\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Paul Ryan\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Nikolay Gromov, Nicolo Primi, Paul Ryan\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Form-factors and complete basis of observables via \
separation of\\n  variables for higher rank spin chains\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP11(2022)039\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"hep-th math-ph math.MP math.QA nlin.SI\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Integrable sl(N) spin chains, which we consider in this \
paper, are not only\\nthe prototypical example of quantum integrable systems \
but also systems with a\\nwide range of applications. For these models we use \
the Functional Separation\\nof Variables (FSoV) technique with a new tool \
called Character Projection to\\ncompute all matrix elements of a complete \
set of operators, which we call\\nprincipal operators, in the basis \
diagonalising the tower of conserved charges\\nas determinants in \
Q-functions. Building up on these results we then derive\\nsimilar \
determinant forms for the form-factors of combinations of \
multiple\\nprincipal operators between arbitrary factorizable states, which \
include, in\\nparticular, off-shell Bethe vectors and Bethe vectors with \
arbitrary twists. We\\nprove that the set of principal operators generates \
the complete spin chain\\nYangian. Furthermore, we derive the representation \
of these operators in the\\nSoV bases allowing one to compute correlation \
functions with an arbitrary\\nnumber of principal operators. Finally, we show \
that the available combinations\\nof multiple insertions includes Sklyanin`s \
SoV B operator. As a result, we are\\nable to derive the B operator for sl(N) \
spin chains using a minimal set of\\ningredients, namely the FSoV method and \
the structure of the SoV basis.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Thu, 3 Feb 2022 13:56:37 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v2\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Thu, 17 Feb 2022 14:14:41 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v3\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Tue, 25 Oct 2022 15:03:45 GMT\""}]}], "}"}]}], 
                   "}"}]}]}, {
