               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Large Charges on the Wilson Loop in $\\\\mathcal{N}=4$ \
SYM: II. Quantum\\n  Fluctuations, OPE, and Spectral Curve\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"77 + 35 pages, 18 figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP08(2022)011\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We continue our study of large charge limits of the \
defect CFT defined by the\\nhalf-BPS Wilson loop in planar $\\\\mathcal{N}=4$ \
supersymmetric Yang-Mills\\ntheory. In this paper, we compute $1/J$ \
corrections to the correlation function\\nof two heavy insertions of charge \
$J$ and two light insertions, in the double\\nscaling limit where the charge \
$J$ and the 't Hooft coupling $\\\\lambda$ are sent\\nto infinity with the \
ratio $J/\\\\sqrt{\\\\lambda}$ fixed. Holographically, they\\ncorrespond to \
quantum fluctuations around a classical string solution with\\nlarge angular \
momentum, and can be computed by evaluating Green's functions on\\nthe \
worldsheet. We derive a representation of the Green's functions in terms \
of\\na sum over residues in the complexified Fourier space, and show that it \
gives\\nrise to the conformal block expansion in the heavy-light channel. \
This allows\\nus to extract the scaling dimensions and structure constants \
for an infinite\\ntower of non-protected dCFT operators. We also find a close \
connection between\\nour results and the semi-classical integrability of the \
string sigma model. The\\nseries of poles of the Green's functions in Fourier \
space corresponds to points\\non the spectral curve where the so-called \
quasi-momentum satisfies a\\nquantization condition, and both the scaling \
dimensions and the structure\\nconstants in the heavy-light channel take \
simple forms when written in terms of\\nthe spectral curve. These \
observations suggest extensions of the results by\\nGromov, Schafer-Nameki \
and Vieira on the semiclassical energy of closed\\nstrings, and in particular \
hint at the possibility of determining the structure\\nconstants directly \
from the spectral curve.\\n\""}]}, {
