            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We describe the mapping at high density of topological \
structure of baryonic\nmatter to a nuclear effective field theory that \
implements hidden symmetries\nemergent from strong nuclear correlations. The \
theory so constructed is found\nto be consistent with no conflicts with the \
presently available observations in\nboth normal nuclear matter and \
compact-star matter. The hidden symmetries\ninvolved are \"local flavor \
symmetry\" of the vector mesons identified to be\n(Seiberg-)dual to the \
gluons of QCD and hidden \"quantum scale symmetry\" with an\nIR fixed point \
with a \"genuine dilaton (GD)\" characterized by non-vanishing\npion and \
dilaton decay constants. Both the skyrmion topology for $N_f \\geq 2$\n\
baryons and the fractional quantum Hall (FQH) droplet topology for $N_f=1$\n\
baryons are unified in the \"homogeneous/hidden\" Wess-Zumino term in the \
hidden\nlocal symmetry (HLS) Lagrangian. The possible indispensable role of \
the FQH\ndroplets in going beyond the density regime of compact stars \
approaching\nscale-chiral restoration is explored by moving toward the limit \
where both the\ndilaton and the pion go massless.\n", 
