         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "2205.09775", "submitter" -> "Davide Bonomi", "authors" -> 
            "Lorenzo Bianchi, Davide Bonomi", "title" -> 
            "Conformal dispersion relations for defects and boundaries", 
            "comments" -> "36 pages, 3 figures", "journal-ref" -> 
            "SciPost Phys. 15, 055 (2023)", "doi" -> 
            "10.21468/SciPostPhys.15.2.055", "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We derive a dispersion relation for two-point correlation \
functions in defect\nconformal field theories. The correlator is expressed as \
an integral over a\n(single) discontinuity that is controlled by the bulk \
channel operator product\nexpansion (OPE). This very simple relation is \
particularly useful in\nperturbative settings where the discontinuity is \
determined by a subset of bulk\noperators. In particular, we apply it to \
holographic correlators of two chiral\nprimary operators in $\\mathcal{N}= 4$ \
Super Yang-Mills theory in the presence\nof a supersymmetric Wilson line. \
With a very simple computation, we are able to\nreproduce and extend existing \
results. We also propose a second relation, which\nreconstructs the \
correlator from a double discontinuity, and is controlled by\nthe defect \
channel OPE. Finally, for the case of codimension-one defects\n(boundaries \
and interfaces) we derive a dispersion relation which receives\ncontributions \
from both OPE channels and we apply it to the boundary correlator\nin the \
$O(N)$ critical model. We reproduce the order $\\epsilon^2$ result in the\n\
$\\epsilon$-expansion using as input a finite number of boundary CFT data.\n",
             "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 19 May 2022 18:00:05 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 23 Jan 2023 17:58:08 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v3", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 31 Mar 2023 15:34:37 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2023-08-02", 
