mistaken,\n  not to the algebraic approach, and correct results will be \
published in a\n  different context", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null, 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th hep-ph", "license" -> 
            "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We determine complete one-loop beta functions of the \
multi-scalar four-point\ncouplings in four-dimensional $SU(N)$ gauge theories \
with $M$ adjoint scalar\nmultiplets. For adjoints scalars, the sign of the \
one loop gauge coupling beta\nfunction depends solely on $M$, vanishing and \
changing sign precisely at\n$M=22$. For the multi-scalar potential at fixed \
gauge coupling we find several\nfixed points with different stability \
properties at large $N$. The analysis\ncrucially involves the full set of \
four $SU(N)$ and $O(M)$ invariant single\ntrace and double trace couplings. \
Taking the gauge coupling into account, there\nare asymptotically free RG \
flows for $M<22$ and non-trivial fixed points for\n$M=22$ at one loop, while \
$M>22$ appears to ruin the UV properties of the\ntheory. Surprisingly, \
uniquely between $M=22$ and $M=21$ the number of fixed\nflows drops from \
eight to four in the large $N$ limit. There seems to be\nsomething very \
special about $M=22$. More speculatively, the $M=22$ one-loop\nconformal \
fixed point theory with $M$ adjoint scalars in $d=4$ suggests the\n\
possibility of an isolated non-supersymmetric, purely bosonic AdS$_{4+1}\n\
\\times$S$^{22-1}$/CFT$_4$ correspondence.\n  Our example suggests that \
extending the potential to the complete set of\nterms allowed by symmetries \
may lead to real fixed points also in\nnon-supersymmetric theories descending \
from $\\mathcal{N}=4$ super-Yang-Mills\ntheory.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
