\\times S^5$", "comments" -> 
            "57 pages, 5 appendices, 1 figure; v2: published version in PRD, \
added\n  clarifications (particularly on superisometries, shifts of spin, and \
choice\n  of cut-off) and references, fixed typos, v3: fixed relative sign in \
eq (2.7)", "journal-ref" -> "Phys. Rev. D 106, 066015 (2022)", "doi" -> 
            "10.1103/PhysRevD.106.066015", "report-no" -> "DMUS-MP-22/09", 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We study a Jordanian deformation of the $AdS_5 \\times S^5$ \
superstring that\npreserves 12 superisometries. It is an example of \
homogeneous Yang-Baxter\ndeformations, a class that generalises TsT \
deformations to the non-abelian\ncase. Many of the attractive features of TsT \
carry over to this more general\nclass, from the possibility of generating \
new supergravity solutions to the\npreservation of worldsheet integrability. \
In this paper, we exploit the fact\nthat the deformed $\\sigma$-model with \
periodic boundary conditions can be\nreformulated as an undeformed one with \
twisted boundary conditions, to discuss\nthe construction of the classical \
spectral curve and its semi-classical\nquantisation. First, we find global \
coordinates for the deformed background,\nand identify the global time \
corresponding to the energy that should be\ncomputed in the spectral problem. \
Using the curve of the twisted model, we\nobtain the one-loop correction to \
the energy of a particular solution, and we\nfind that the charge encoding \
the twisted boundary conditions does not receive\nan anomalous correction. \
Finally, we give evidence suggesting that the\nunimodular version of the \
deformation (giving rise to a supergravity\nbackground) and the \
non-unimodular one (whose background does not solve the\nsupergravity \
equations) have the same spectrum at least to one-loop.\n", 
