               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Light-cone limits of large rectangular fishnets\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"24 pages, 5 figures, references added\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP03(2023)156\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Basso-Dixon integrals evaluate rectangular fishnets -- \
Feynman graphs with\\nmassless scalar propagators which form a $m\\\\times n$ \
rectangular grid -- which\\narise in certain one-trace four-point correlators \
in the `fishnet' limit of\\n$\\\\mathcal{N}=4$ SYM. Recently, Basso {\\\\it \
et al} explored the thermodynamical\\nlimit $m\\\\to\\\\infty$ with fixed \
aspect ratio $n/m$ of a rectangular fishnet and\\nshowed that in general the \
dependence on the coordinates of the four operators\\nis erased, but it \
reappears in a scaling limit with two of the operators\\ngetting close in a \
controlled way. In this note I investigate the most general\\ndouble scaling \
limit which describes the thermodynamics when one of two pairs\\nof operators \
become nearly light-like. In this double scaling limit, the\\nrectangular \
fishnet depends on both coordinate cross ratios. I show that all\\nsingular \
limits of the fishnet can be attained within the double scaling \
limit,\\nincluding the null limit with the four points approaching the cusps \
of a null\\nsquare. A direct evaluation of the fishnet in the null limit is \
presented any\\n$m$ and $n$.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
