               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Bo Sundborg\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Nadia Flodgren and Bo Sundborg\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"One-loop algebras and fixed flow trajectories in adjoint \
multi-scalar\\n  gauge theory\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"22 pages, 3 figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP04(2023)129\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th hep-ph\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study the one loop renormalisation of 4d $SU(N)$ \
Yang-Mills theory with\\n$M$ adjoint representation scalar multiplets related \
by $O(M)$ symmetry.\\nGeneral $M$ are of field theoretic interest, and the 4d \
one loop beta function\\nof the gauge coupling $g^2$ vanishes for the case \
$M=22$, which is intriguing\\nfor string theory. This case is related to D3 \
branes of critical bosonic string\\ntheory in $D=22+4=26$. An RG fixed point \
could have provided a definition for a\\npurely bosonic AdS/CFT, but we show \
that scalar self-couplings $\\\\lambda$ ruin\\none-loop conformal invariance \
in the large $N$ limit. There are real fixed\\nflows (fixed points of \
$\\\\lambda/g^2$) only for $M\\\\ge 406$, rendering one-loop\\nfixed points \
of the gauge coupling and scalar couplings incompatible.\\n  We develop and \
check an algebraic approach to the one-loop renormalisation\\ngroup which we \
find to be characterised by a non-associative algebra of\\nmarginal \
couplings. In the large $N$ limit, the resulting RG flows typically\\nsuffer \
from strong coupling in both the ultraviolet and the infrared. Only \
for\\n$M\\\\ge 406$ fine-tuned solutions exist which are weakly coupled in \
the infrared.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
