               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Jim Lundin, Roman Mauch, Lorenzo Ruggeri\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"From 5d Flat Connections to 4d Fluxes (the Art of Slicing \
the Cone)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"36 pages; erroneous square roots removed in sec.6\""}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP10(2023)155\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We compute the Coulomb branch partition function of the \
4d $\\\\mathcal{N}=2$\\nvector multiplet on closed simply-connected \
quasi-toric manifolds $B$. This\\nincludes a large class of theories, \
localising to either instantons or\\nanti-instantons at the torus fixed \
points (including Donaldson-Witten and\\nPestun-like theories as examples). \
The main difficulty is to obtain flux\\ncontributions from the localisation \
procedure. We achieve this by taking a\\ndetour via the 5d $\\\\mathcal{N}=1$ \
vector multiplet on closed simply-connected\\ntoric Sasaki-manifolds $M$ \
which are principal $S^1$-bundles over $B$. The\\nperturbative partition \
function can be expressed as a product over slices of\\nthe toric cone. By \
taking finite quotients $M/\\\\mathbb{Z}_h$ along the $S^1$,\\nthe locus \
picks up non-trivial flat connections which, in the \
limit\\n$h\\\\to\\\\infty$, provide the sought-after fluxes on $B$. We \
compute the one-loop\\npartition functions around each topological sector on \
$M/\\\\mathbb{Z}_h$ and $B$\\nexplicitly, and then factorise them into \
contributions from the torus fixed\\npoints. This enables us to also write \
down the conjectured instanton part of\\nthe partition function on \
$B$.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
