Cone)", "comments" -> "36 pages; erroneous square roots removed in sec.6", 
            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP10(2023)155", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We compute the Coulomb branch partition function of the 4d \
$\\mathcal{N}=2$\nvector multiplet on closed simply-connected quasi-toric \
manifolds $B$. This\nincludes a large class of theories, localising to either \
instantons or\nanti-instantons at the torus fixed points (including \
Donaldson-Witten and\nPestun-like theories as examples). The main difficulty \
is to obtain flux\ncontributions from the localisation procedure. We achieve \
this by taking a\ndetour via the 5d $\\mathcal{N}=1$ vector multiplet on \
closed simply-connected\ntoric Sasaki-manifolds $M$ which are principal \
$S^1$-bundles over $B$. The\nperturbative partition function can be expressed \
as a product over slices of\nthe toric cone. By taking finite quotients \
$M/\\mathbb{Z}_h$ along the $S^1$,\nthe locus picks up non-trivial flat \
connections which, in the limit\n$h\\to\\infty$, provide the sought-after \
fluxes on $B$. We compute the one-loop\npartition functions around each \
topological sector on $M/\\mathbb{Z}_h$ and $B$\nexplicitly, and then \
factorise them into contributions from the torus fixed\npoints. This enables \
us to also write down the conjectured instanton part of\nthe partition \
function on $B$.\n", 
