         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "2307.06841", "submitter" -> "Shubham Kumar Sinha", 
            "authors" -> "Shiroman Prakash and Shubham Kumar Sinha", "title" -> 
            "Emergent Supersymmetry at Large $N$", "comments" -> 
            "80 pages, 19 figures. v2: fixed typos and added references; v3:\n\
  accepted for publication in JHEP", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/JHEP01(2024)025", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "hep-th cond-mat.str-el", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We search for infrared fixed points of Gross-Neveu Yukawa \
models with matrix\ndegrees of freedom in $d=4-\\varepsilon$. We consider \
three models -- a model\nwith $SU(N)$ symmetry in which the scalar and \
fermionic fields both transform\nin the adjoint representation, a model with \
$SO(N)$ symmetry in which the\nscalar and fermion fields both transform as \
real symmetric-traceless matrices,\nand a model with $SO(N)$ symmetry in \
which the scalar field transforms as a\nreal symmetric-traceless matrix, \
while the fermion transforms in the adjoint\nrepresentation. These models \
differ at finite $N$, but their large-$N$ limits\nare perturbatively \
equivalent. The first two models contain a supersymmetric\nfixed point for \
all $N$, which is attractive to all classically-marginal\ndeformations for \
$N$ sufficiently large. The third model possesses a stable\nfixed point that, \
although non-supersymmetric, gives rise to many correlation\nfunctions that \
are identical to those of a supersymmetric fixed point when $N$\nis \
sufficiently large. We also find several non-supersymmetric fixed points at\n\
finite and large-$N$. Planar diagrams dominate the large-$N$ limit of these\n\
fixed points, which suggests the possibility of a stringy holographic dual\n\
description.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 13 Jul 2023 16:05:40 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 8 Sep 2023 07:48:31 GMT"}, {
