            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2312.11612\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Paul Ryan\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Simon Ekhammar, Nikolay Gromov, Paul Ryan\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Boundary Overlaps from Functional Separation of Variables\
\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"V3: Updated references, fixed typos\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"hep-th math-ph math.MP nlin.SI\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  In this paper we show how the Functional Separation of \
Variables (FSoV)\\nmethod can be applied to the problem of computing overlaps \
with integrable\\nboundary states in integrable systems. We demonstrate our \
general method on the\\nexample of a particular boundary state, a singlet of \
the symmetry group, in an\\nsu(3) rational spin chain in an alternating \
fundamental--anti-fundamental\\nrepresentation. The FSoV formalism allows us \
to compute in determinant form not\\nonly the overlaps of the boundary state \
with the eigenstates of the transfer\\nmatrix, but in fact with any \
factorisable state. This includes off-shell Bethe\\nstates, whose overlaps \
with the boundary state have been out of reach with\\nother methods. \
Furthermore, we also found determinant representations for\\ninsertions of \
so-called Principal Operators (forming a complete algebra of \
all\\nobservables) between the boundary and the factorisable state as well as \
certain\\ntypes of multiple insertions of Principal Operators. Concise \
formulas for the\\nmatrix elements of the boundary state in the SoV basis and \
su(N)\\ngeneralisations are presented. Finally, we managed to construct a \
complete\\nbasis of integrable boundary states by repeated action of \
conserved charges on\\nthe singlet state. As a result, we are also able to \
compute the overlaps of all\\nof these states with integral of motion \
eigenstates.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
