                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  BPS algebras are the symmetries of a wide class of \
brane-inspired models.\\nThey are closely related to Yangians -- the peculiar \
and somewhat sophisticated\\nlimit of DIM algebras. Still they possess some \
simple and explicit\\nrepresentations. We explain here that for \
$Y(\\\\widehat{\\\\mathfrak{gl}}_r)$ these\\nrepresentations are related to \
Uglov polynomials, whose families are also\\nlabeled by natural $r$. They \
arise in the limit $\\\\hbar\\\\longrightarrow 0$ from\\nMacdonald \
polynomials, and generalize the well-known Jack \
polynomials\\n($\\\\beta$-deformation of Schur functions), associated with \
$r=1$. For $r=2$ they\\napproximate Macdonald polynomials with the accuracy \
$O(\\\\hbar^2)$, so that they\\nare eigenfunctions of {\\\\it two} \
immediately available commuting operators,\\narising from the \
$\\\\hbar$-expansion of the first Macdonald Hamiltonian. These\\noperators \
have a clear structure, which is easily generalizable, -- what\\nprovides a \
technically simple way to build an explicit representation of\\nYangian $Y(\\\
\\widehat{\\\\mathfrak{gl}}_2)$, where $U^{(2)}$ are associated with \
the\\nstates $|\\\\lambda\\\\rangle$, parametrized by chess-colored Young \
diagrams. An\\ninteresting feature of this representation is that the odd \
time-variables\\n$p_{2n+1}$ can be expressed through mutually commuting \
operators from Yangian,\\nhowever even time-variables $p_{2n}$ are \
inexpressible. Implications to higher\\n$r$ become now straightforward, yet \
we describe them only in a sketchy way.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
