           "id" -> "2402.05920", "submitter" -> "Nikita Tselousov", "authors" -> 
            "Dmitry Galakhov, Alexei Morozov, Nikita Tselousov", "title" -> 
            "Simple Representations of BPS Algebras: the case of\n  \
$Y(\\widehat{\\mathfrak{gl}}_2)$", "comments" -> Null, "journal-ref" -> Null, 
            "doi" -> "10.1140/epjc/s10052-024-12952-x", "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> "hep-th math-ph math.MP math.QA math.RT", 
            "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  BPS algebras are the symmetries of a wide class of \
brane-inspired models.\nThey are closely related to Yangians -- the peculiar \
and somewhat sophisticated\nlimit of DIM algebras. Still they possess some \
simple and explicit\nrepresentations. We explain here that for \
$Y(\\widehat{\\mathfrak{gl}}_r)$ these\nrepresentations are related to Uglov \
polynomials, whose families are also\nlabeled by natural $r$. They arise in \
the limit $\\hbar\\longrightarrow 0$ from\nMacdonald polynomials, and \
generalize the well-known Jack polynomials\n($\\beta$-deformation of Schur \
functions), associated with $r=1$. For $r=2$ they\napproximate Macdonald \
polynomials with the accuracy $O(\\hbar^2)$, so that they\nare eigenfunctions \
of {\\it two} immediately available commuting operators,\narising from the \
$\\hbar$-expansion of the first Macdonald Hamiltonian. These\noperators have \
a clear structure, which is easily generalizable, -- what\nprovides a \
technically simple way to build an explicit representation of\nYangian \
$Y(\\widehat{\\mathfrak{gl}}_2)$, where $U^{(2)}$ are associated with the\n\
states $|\\lambda\\rangle$, parametrized by chess-colored Young diagrams. An\n\
interesting feature of this representation is that the odd time-variables\n\
$p_{2n+1}$ can be expressed through mutually commuting operators from \
Yangian,\nhowever even time-variables $p_{2n}$ are inexpressible. \
Implications to higher\n$r$ become now straightforward, yet we describe them \
only in a sketchy way.\n", 
