Guo\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"A (2+1)-Dimensional Domain Wall at One-Loop\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"21 pages, 1figure\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We consider the domain wall in the (2+1)-dimensional \
$\\\\phi^4$ double well\\nmodel, created by extending the $\\\\phi^4$ kink in \
an additional infinite\\ndirection. Classically, the tension is \
$m^3/3\\\\lambda$ where $\\\\lambda$ is the\\ncoupling and $m$ is the meson \
mass. At order $O(\\\\lambda^0)$ all ultraviolet\\ndivergences can be removed \
by normal ordering, less trivial divergences arrive\\nonly at the next order. \
This allows us to easily quantize the domain wall,\\nworking at order \
$O(\\\\lambda^0)$. We calculate the leading quantum correction to\\nits \
tension as a two-dimensional integral over a function which is \
determined\\nanalytically. This integral is performed numerically, resulting \
in\\n$-0.0866m^2$. This correction has previously been computed twice in \
the\\nliterature, and the results of these two computations disagreed. Our \
result\\nagrees with and so confirms that of Jaimunga, Semenoff and Zarembo. \
We also\\nfind, at this order, the excitation spectrum and a general \
expression for the\\none-loop tensions of domain walls in other scalar \
models.\\n\""}]}, {
