Yixin Xu", "title" -> "Angular fractals in thermal QFT", "comments" -> 
            "45 pages + appendices, 7 figures; v2: references added", 
            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null, "report-no" -> 
            "CALT-TH 2024-021", "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We show that thermal effective field theory controls the \
long-distance\nexpansion of the partition function of a $d$-dimensional QFT, \
with an insertion\nof any finite-order spatial isometry. Consequently, the \
thermal partition\nfunction on a sphere displays a fractal-like structure as \
a function of angular\ntwist, reminiscent of the behavior of a modular form \
near the real line. As an\nexample application, we find that for CFTs, the \
effective free energy of\neven-spin minus odd-spin operators at high \
temperature is smaller than the\nusual free energy by a factor of $1/2^d$. \
Near certain rational angles, the\npartition function receives subleading \
contributions from \"Kaluza-Klein vortex\ndefects\" in the thermal EFT, which \
we classify. We illustrate our results with\nexamples in free and holographic \
theories, and also discuss nonperturbative\ncorrections from worldline \
instantons.\n", 
