               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2409.08735\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Colin Rylands\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Colin Rylands and Eric Vernier and Pasquale \
Calabrese\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Dynamical symmetry restoration in the Heisenberg spin \
chain\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"19+5 pages, 5 figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"cond-mat.stat-mech quant-ph\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The entanglement asymmetry is an observable independent \
tool to investigate\\nthe relaxation of quantum many body systems through the \
restoration of an\\ninitially broken symmetry of the dynamics. In this paper \
we use this to\\ninvestigate the effects of interactions on quantum \
relaxation in a paradigmatic\\nintegrable model. Specifically, we study the \
dynamical restoration of the\\n$U(1)$ symmetry corresponding to rotations \
about the $z$-axis in the XXZ model\\nquenched from a tilted ferromagnetic \
state. We find two distinct patterns of\\nbehaviour depending upon the \
interaction regime of the model. In the gapless\\nregime, at roots of unity, \
we find that the symmetry restoration is\\npredominantly carried out by bound \
states of spinons of maximal length. The\\nvelocity of these bound states is \
suppressed as the anisotropy is decreased\\ntowards the isotropic point \
leading to slower symmetry restoration. By varying\\nthe initial tilt angle, \
one sees that symmetry restoration is slower for an\\ninitally smaller tilt \
angle, signifying the presence of the quantum Mpemba\\neffect. In the gapped \
regime however, spin transport for non maximally tilted\\nstates, is \
dominated by smaller bound states with longer bound states becoming\\nfrozen. \
This leads to a much longer time scales for restoration compared to \
the\\ngapless regime. In addition, the quantum Mpemba effect is absent in the \
gapped\\nregime.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Fri, 13 Sep 2024 11:35:09 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2024-09-16\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
