            "  The entanglement asymmetry is an observable independent tool \
to investigate\nthe relaxation of quantum many body systems through the \
restoration of an\ninitially broken symmetry of the dynamics. In this paper \
we use this to\ninvestigate the effects of interactions on quantum relaxation \
in a paradigmatic\nintegrable model. Specifically, we study the dynamical \
restoration of the\n$U(1)$ symmetry corresponding to rotations about the \
$z$-axis in the XXZ model\nquenched from a tilted ferromagnetic state. We \
find two distinct patterns of\nbehaviour depending upon the interaction \
regime of the model. In the gapless\nregime, at roots of unity, we find that \
the symmetry restoration is\npredominantly carried out by bound states of \
spinons of maximal length. The\nvelocity of these bound states is suppressed \
as the anisotropy is decreased\ntowards the isotropic point leading to slower \
symmetry restoration. By varying\nthe initial tilt angle, one sees that \
symmetry restoration is slower for an\ninitally smaller tilt angle, \
signifying the presence of the quantum Mpemba\neffect. In the gapped regime \
however, spin transport for non maximally tilted\nstates, is dominated by \
smaller bound states with longer bound states becoming\nfrozen. This leads to \
a much longer time scales for restoration compared to the\ngapless regime. In \
addition, the quantum Mpemba effect is absent in the gapped\nregime.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
