            "report-no" -> "NBI-HE-00-24, NORDITA-2000/50-HE", "categories" -> 
            "hep-th hep-lat", "license" -> Null, "abstract" -> 
            "  The low-energy effective theory of the IIB matrix model \
developed by H. Aoki\net al. is written down explicitly in terms of bosonic \
variables only. The\neffective theory is then studied by Monte Carlo \
simulations in order to\ninvestigate the possibility of a spontaneous \
breakdown of Lorentz invariance.\nThe imaginary part of the effective action, \
which causes the so-called sign\nproblem in the simulation, is dropped by \
hand. The extent of the eigenvalue\ndistribution of the bosonic matrices \
shows a power-law large N behavior,\nconsistent with a simple \
branched-polymer prediction. We observe, however, that\nthe eigenvalue \
distribution becomes more and more isotropic in the\nten-dimensional \
space-time as we increase N. This suggests that if the\nspontaneous breakdown \
of Lorentz invariance really occurs in the IIB matrix\nmodel, a crucial role \
must be played by the imaginary part of the effective\naction.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
