           "id" -> "hep-th/0108075", "submitter" -> "Eva Silverstein", 
            "authors" -> "A. Adams, J. Polchinski, and E. Silverstein", 
            "title" -> 
            "Don't Panic! Closed String Tachyons in ALE Spacetimes", 
            "comments" -> 
            "48 pages, harvmac big; 13 figures. v2: added references; comment \
on\n  gravity regime added to section 5.2. v3: added references", 
            "journal-ref" -> "JHEP 0110:029,2001", "doi" -> 
            "10.1088/1126-6708/2001/10/029", "report-no" -> 
            "NSF-ITP-01-75, SLAC-PUB-8955", "categories" -> "hep-th", 
            "license" -> Null, "abstract" -> 
            "  We consider closed string tachyons localized at the fixed \
points of\nnoncompact nonsupersymmetric orbifolds. We argue that tachyon \
condensation\ndrives these orbifolds to flat space or supersymmetric ALE \
spaces. The decay\nproceeds via an expanding shell of dilaton gradients and \
curvature which\ninterpolates between two regions of distinct angular \
geometry. The string\ncoupling remains weak throughout. For small tachyon \
VEVs, evidence comes from\nquiver theories on D-branes probes, in which \
deformations by twisted couplings\nsmoothly connect non-supersymmetric \
orbifolds to supersymmetric orbifolds of\nreduced order. For large tachyon \
VEVs, evidence comes from worldsheet RG flow\nand spacetime gravity. For \
$\\IC^2/\\IZ_n$, we exhibit infinite sequences of\ntransitions producing SUSY \
ALE spaces via twisted closed string condensation\nfrom non-supersymmetric \
ALE spaces. In a $T$-dual description this provides a\nmechanism for creating \
NS5-branes via {\\it closed} string tachyon condensation\nsimilar to the \
creation of D-branes via {\\it open} string tachyon condensation.\nWe also \
apply our results to recent duality conjectures involving fluxbranes\nand the \
type 0 string.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 10 Aug 2001 22:12:22 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Sun, 2 Sep 2001 19:47:34 GMT"}, {
