                RowBox[{
                 "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"A.A. Tseytlin, K. Zarembo\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Wilson loops in N=4 SYM theory: rotation in S5\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"22 pages, 2 figures\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Phys.Rev.D66:125010,2002\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.66.125010\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"ITEP-TH-34/02\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  We study Wilson loops in N=4 SYM theory which are \
non-constant in the scalar\\n(S5) directions and open string solutions \
associated with them in the context\\nof AdS/CFT correspondence. An interplay \
between Minkowskian and Euclidean\\npictures turns out to be non-trivial for \
time-dependent Wilson loops. We find\\nthat in the S5-rotating case there \
appears to be no direct open-string duals\\nfor the Minkowskian Wilson loops, \
and their expectation values should be\\nobtained by analytic continuation \
from the Euclidean-space results. In the\\nEuclidean case, we determine the \
dependence of the ``quark - anti-quark''\\npotential on the rotation \
parameter, both at weak coupling (i.e. in the 1-loop\\nperturbative SYM \
theory) and at strong coupling (i.e. in the classical string\\ntheory in AdS5 \
x S5).\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
