           "id" -> "hep-th/0210157", "submitter" -> "Douglas J. Smith", 
            "authors" -> "Douglas J. Smith", "title" -> 
            "Intersecting brane solutions in string and M-theory", "comments" -> 
            "85 pages. Prepared as a Topical Review for Classical and Quantum\
\n  Gravity. v2 Added references. v3 Added a short discussion of entropy of\n \
 non-SUSY (but extremal or classically BPS) black holes to section 4.2.2. \
Also\n  added/updated references", "journal-ref" -> 
            "Class.Quant.Grav.20:R233,2003", "doi" -> 
            "10.1088/0264-9381/20/9/203", "report-no" -> "DTP/02/67", 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> Null, "abstract" -> 
            "  We review various aspects of configurations of intersecting \
branes, including\nthe conditions for preservation of supersymmetry. In \
particular, we discuss the\nprojection conditions on the Killing spinors for \
given brane configurations and\nthe relation to calibrations. This highlights \
the close connection between\nintersecting branes and branes wrapping \
supersymmetric cycles as well as\nspecial holonomy manifolds. We also explain \
how these conditions can be used to\nfind supergravity solutions without \
directly solving the Einstein equations.\nThe description of intersecting \
branes is considered both in terms of the brane\nworldvolume theories and as \
supergravity solutions. There are well-known simple\nprocedures (harmonic \
function rules) for writing down the supergravity\nsolutions for \
supersymmetric configurations of orthogonally intersecting\nbranes. However, \
such solutions involve smeared or delocalised branes. We\ndescribe several \
methods of constructing solutions with less smearing,\nincluding some fully \
localised solutions. Some applications of these\nsupergravity solutions are \
also considered - in particular the study of black\nholes and gauge theories.\
\n", "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Wed, 16 Oct 2002 15:27:53 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 29 Oct 2002 16:48:34 GMT"}, {
