         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "hep-th/0303086", "submitter" -> "Chi-Wei Herbert Lee", 
            "authors" -> "C.-W. H. Lee", "title" -> 
            "Noncommutative probability, matrix models, and quantum orbifold \
geometry", "comments" -> 
            "22 pages, 8 eps figures, LaTeX2.09; title changed, mistakes \
corrected", "journal-ref" -> "JHEP 0306 (2003) 044", "doi" -> 
            "10.1088/1126-6708/2003/06/044", "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> "hep-th gr-qc math-ph math.MP math.OA", "license" -> 
            Null, "abstract" -> 
            "  Inspired by the intimate relationship between Voiculescu's \
noncommutative\nprobability theory (of type A) and large-N matrix models in \
physics, we look\nfor physical models related to noncommutative probability \
theory of type B.\nThese turn out to be fermionic matrix-vector models at the \
double large-N\nlimit. In the context of string theory, they describe \
different orbifolded\nstring worldsheets with boundaries. Their critical \
exponents coincide with that\nof ordinary string worldsheets, but their \
renormalised tree-level one-boundary\namplitudes differ.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 10 Mar 2003 20:25:01 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 7 Jul 2003 17:59:29 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2009-11-10", "authors_parsed" -> {{"Lee", "C. -W. H.", ""}}}], 
          "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{11.901219994717295`, -4.457347890862534}, 0.],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th/0303141\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Wang Xiao-jun\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Xiao-Jun Wang and Sen Hu\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Confinement of N=1 Super Yang-Mills from \
Supergravity\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"use revtex4.cls, 8 pages, 4 eps figs\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We calculate circular Wilson loop expectation value of \
pure ${\\\\cal N}=1$\\nsuper Yang-Mills from the Klebanov-Strassker-Tseytlin \
solution of supergravity\\nand the proposed gauge/gravity duality. The \
calculation is performed\\nnumerically via searching world-sheet minimal \
surface. It is shown that Wilson\\nloop exhibits area law for large radius \
which implies that ${\\\\cal N}=1$ super\\nYang-Mills is confined at large \
distance or low energy scale. Meanwhile, Wilson\\nloop exhibits logarithmic \
behavior for small radius and it indicates\\nasymptotical freedom of \
${\\\\cal N}=1$ super Yang-Mills at short distance or high\\nenergy scale.\\n\
\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
