               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th/0307017\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Raimar Wulkenhaar\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Harald Grosse (Vienna) and Raimar Wulkenhaar \
(Leipzig)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Renormalisation of \\\\phi^4-theory on noncommutative R^2 \
in the matrix\\n  base\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"26 pages, 44 figures, LaTeX\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP 0312 (2003) 019\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1088/1126-6708/2003/12/019\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  As a first application of our renormalisation group \
approach to non-local\\nmatrix models [hep-th/0305066], we prove \
(super-)renormalisability of Euclidean\\ntwo-dimensional noncommutative \
\\\\phi^4-theory. It is widely believed that this\\nmodel is renormalisable \
in momentum space arguing that there would be\\nlogarithmic UV/IR-divergences \
only. Although momentum space Feynman graphs can\\nindeed be computed to any \
loop order, the logarithmic UV/IR-divergence appears\\nin the renormalised \
two-point function -- a hint that the renormalisation is\\nnot completed. In \
particular, it is impossible to define the squared mass as\\nthe value of the \
two-point function at vanishing momentum. In contrast, in our\\nmatrix \
approach the renormalised N-point functions are bounded everywhere \
and\\nnevertheless rely on adjusting the mass only. We achieve this by \
introducing\\ninto the cut-off model a translation-invariance breaking \
regulator which is\\nscaled to zero with the removal of the cut-off. The \
naive treatment without\\nregulator would not lead to a renormalised \
theory.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
