         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "hep-th/0307017", "submitter" -> "Raimar Wulkenhaar", 
            "authors" -> 
            "Harald Grosse (Vienna) and Raimar Wulkenhaar (Leipzig)", "title" -> 
            "Renormalisation of \\phi^4-theory on noncommutative R^2 in the \
matrix\n  base", "comments" -> "26 pages, 44 figures, LaTeX", "journal-ref" -> 
            "JHEP 0312 (2003) 019", "doi" -> "10.1088/1126-6708/2003/12/019", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> Null, 
            "abstract" -> 
            "  As a first application of our renormalisation group approach \
to non-local\nmatrix models [hep-th/0305066], we prove \
(super-)renormalisability of Euclidean\ntwo-dimensional noncommutative \
\\phi^4-theory. It is widely believed that this\nmodel is renormalisable in \
momentum space arguing that there would be\nlogarithmic UV/IR-divergences \
only. Although momentum space Feynman graphs can\nindeed be computed to any \
loop order, the logarithmic UV/IR-divergence appears\nin the renormalised \
two-point function -- a hint that the renormalisation is\nnot completed. In \
particular, it is impossible to define the squared mass as\nthe value of the \
two-point function at vanishing momentum. In contrast, in our\nmatrix \
approach the renormalised N-point functions are bounded everywhere and\n\
nevertheless rely on adjusting the mass only. We achieve this by introducing\n\
into the cut-off model a translation-invariance breaking regulator which is\n\
scaled to zero with the removal of the cut-off. The naive treatment without\n\
regulator would not lead to a renormalised theory.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 1 Jul 2003 20:32:32 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2016-09-06", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Grosse", "Harald", "", "Vienna"}, {
              "Wulkenhaar", "Raimar", "", "Leipzig"}}}], "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{17.39650962432551, -2.054247034639216}, 0.],
