            "title" -> "Multi-Spin Giants", "comments" -> 
            "45 pages, v3: Supersymmetry of the two-spin solution corrected \
from\n  1/4 to 1/2. Sections 2.6 and 3.6 improved. A reference and comments \
added", "journal-ref" -> "Phys.Rev. D69 (2004) 106006", "doi" -> 
            "10.1103/PhysRevD.69.106006", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "hep-th", "license" -> Null, "abstract" -> 
            "  We examine spherical p-branes in AdS_m x S^n, that wrap an S^p \
in either\nAdS_m (p=m-2) or S^n (p=n-2). We first construct a two-spin giant \
solution\nexpanding in S^n and has spins both in AdS_m and S^n. For\n\
(m,n)={(5,5),(4,7),(7,4)}, it is 1/2 supersymmetric, and it reduces to the\n\
single-spin giant graviton when the AdS spin vanishes. We study some of its\n\
basic properties such as instantons, non-commutativity, zero-modes, and the\n\
perturbative spectrum. All vibration modes have real and positive frequencies\
\ndetermined uniquely by the spacetime curvature, and evenly spaced. We next\n\
consider the (0+1)-dimensional sigma-models obtained by keeping generally\n\
time-dependent transverse coordinates, describing warped product of a\n\
breathing-mode and a point-particle on S^n or AdS_m x S^1. The BPS bounds \
show\nthat the only spherical supersymmetric solutions are the single and the\
\ntwo-spin giants. Moreover, we integrate the sigma-model and separate the\n\
canonical variables. We quantize exactly the point-particle part of the \
motion,\nwhich in local coordinates gives Poschl-Teller type potentials, and \
calculate\nits contribution to the anomalous dimension.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
