                 "\"AEI-2004-001, SPhT-T04/002\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We investigate whether the (planar, two complex scalar) \
dilatation operator\\nof N=4 gauge theory can be, perturbatively and, \
perhaps, non-perturbatively,\\ndescribed by an integrable long range spin \
chain with elliptic exchange\\ninteraction. Such a chain was introduced some \
time ago by Inozemtsev. In the\\nlimit of sufficiently ``long'' operators a \
Bethe ansatz exists, which we apply\\nat the perturbative two- and three-loop \
level. Spectacular agreement is found\\nwith spinning string predictions of \
Frolov and Tseytlin for the two-loop\\nenergies of certain large charge \
operators. However, we then go on to show that\\nthe agreement between \
perturbative gauge theory and semi-classical string\\ntheory begins to break \
down, in a subtle fashion, at the three-loop level. This\\ncorroborates a \
recently found disagreement between three-loop gauge theory and\\nnear \
plane-wave string theory results, and quantitatively explains a \
previously\\nobtained puzzling deviation between the string proposal and a \
numerical\\nextrapolation of finite size three-loop anomalous dimensions. At \
four loops and\\nbeyond, we find that the Inozemtsev chain exhibits a generic \
breakdown of\\nperturbative BMN scaling. However, our proposal is not \
necessarily limited to\\nperturbation theory, and one would hope that the \
string theory results can be\\nrecovered from the Inozemtsev chain at strong \
't Hooft coupling.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
