            "comments" -> "28 pages", "journal-ref" -> "JHEP 0404 (2004) 058",
             "doi" -> "10.1088/1126-6708/2004/04/058", "report-no" -> 
            "KEK-TH-938", "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> Null, 
            "abstract" -> 
            "  In matrix models, higher dimensional D-branes are obtained by \
imposing a\nnoncommutative relation to coordinates of lower dimensional \
D-branes. On the\nother hand, a dual description of this noncommutative space \
is provided by\nhigher dimensional D-branes with gauge fields. Fuzzy spheres \
can appear as a\nconfiguration of lower dimensional D-branes in a constant \
R-R field strength\nbackground. In this paper, we consider a dual description \
of higher dimensional\nfuzzy spheres by introducing nonabelian gauge fields \
on higher dimensional\nspherical D-branes. By using the Born-Infeld action, \
we show that a fuzzy\n$2k$-sphere and spherical D$2k$-branes with a \
nonabelian gauge field whose\nChern character is nontrivial are the same \
objects when $n$ is large. We\ndiscuss a relationship between the \
noncommutative geometry and nonabelian gauge\nfields. Nonabelian gauge fields \
are represented by noncommutative matrices\nincluding the coordinate \
dependence. A similarity to the quantum Hall system is\nalso studied.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 5 Feb 2004 15:40:33 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 16 Feb 2004 09:28:25 GMT"}, {
