         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "hep-th/0505075", "submitter" -> "Laurence G. Yaffe", 
            "authors" -> "Pavel Kovtun, Mithat Unsal and Laurence G. Yaffe", 
            "title" -> 
            "Can large Nc equivalence between supersymmetric Yang-Mills \
theory and\n  its orbifold projections be valid?", "comments" -> 
            "21 pages, added discussion of anomaly matching in parent and\n  \
daughter, and differences between N=4 and N=1 SYM cases", "journal-ref" -> 
            "Phys.Rev. D72 (2005) 105006", "doi" -> 
            "10.1103/PhysRevD.72.105006", "report-no" -> "UW/PT 05-12", 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> Null, "abstract" -> 
            "  Necessary and sufficient conditions for large Nc equivalence \
between parent\nand daughter theories, for a wide class of orbifold \
projections of U(Nc) gauge\ntheories, are just the natural requirements that \
the discrete symmetry used to\ndefine the projection not be spontaneously \
broken in the parent theory, and the\ndiscrete symmetry permuting equivalent \
gauge group factors not be spontaneously\nbroken in the daughter theory. In \
this paper, we discuss the application of\nthis result to Z_k projections of \
N=1 supersymmetric Yang-Mills theory in four\ndimensions, as well as various \
multi-flavor generalizations. Z_k projections\nwith k > 2 yielding chiral \
gauge theories violate the symmetry realization\nconditions needed for large \
Nc equivalence, due to the spontaneous symmetry\nbreaking of discrete chiral \
symmetry in the parent super-Yang-Mills theory. But\nfor Z_2 projections, we \
show that previous assertions of large Nc\ninequivalence, in infinite volume, \
between the parent and daughter theories\nwere based on incorrect mappings of \
vacuum energies, theta angles, or connected\ncorrelators between the two \
theories. With the correct identifications, there\nis no sign of any \
inconsistency. A subtle but essential feature of the\nconnection between \
parent and daughter theories involves multi-valuedness in\nthe mapping of \
theta parameters from parent to daughter.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 9 May 2005 19:03:50 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 24 Jun 2005 23:44:54 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2009-11-11", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Kovtun", "Pavel", ""}, {
              "Unsal", "Mithat", ""}, {"Yaffe", "Laurence G.", ""}}}], 
          "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{13.21921388470873, -0.584466412885277}, 0.],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th/0505099\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Radu Roiban\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"A. Dymarsky, I.R. Klebanov, R. Roiban\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Perturbative Search for Fixed Lines in Large N Gauge \
Theories\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"31 pages, 4 figures, latex v2: Clarifications and \
reference added\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP 0508 (2005) 011\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1088/1126-6708/2005/08/011\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th hep-ph\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The logarithmic running of marginal double-trace \
operators is a general\\nfeature of 4-d field theories containing scalar \
fields in the adjoint or\\nbifundamental representation. Such operators \
provide leading contributions in\\nthe large N limit; therefore, the leading \
terms in their beta functions must\\nvanish for a theory to be large N \
conformal. We calculate the one-loop beta\\nfunctions in orbifolds of the N=4 \
SYM theory by a discrete subgroup Gamma of\\nthe SU(4) R-symmetry, which are \
dual to string theory on AdS_5 x S^5/Gamma. We\\npresent a general strategy \
for determining whether there is a fixed line\\npassing through the origin of \
the coupling constant space. Then we study in\\ndetail some classes of \
non-supersymmetric orbifold theories, and emphasize the\\nimportance of \
decoupling the U(1) factors. Among our examples, which include\\norbifolds \
acting freely on the S^5, we do not find any large N\\nnon-supersymmetric \
theories with fixed lines passing through the origin.\\nConnection of these \
results with closed string tachyon condensation in AdS_5 x\\nS^5/Gamma is \
discussed.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Wed, 11 May 2005 19:39:03 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v2\""}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Tue, 7 Jun 2005 13:24:35 GMT\""}]}], "}"}]}], 
                   "}"}]}]}, {
