         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "hep-th/0510136", "submitter" -> "Wung-Hong Huang", 
            "authors" -> "Wung-Hong Huang", "title" -> 
            "Multi-spin String Solutions in Magnetic-flux Deformed $AdS_n \
\\times S^m$\n  Spacetime", "comments" -> 
            "Latex 15 pages, add a little comment in discussion", 
            "journal-ref" -> "JHEP0512:013,2005", "doi" -> 
            "10.1088/1126-6708/2005/12/013", "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> Null, "abstract" -> 
            "  We perform the dimensional reduction of the spacetime of a \
stack of N\nD3-branes by the ``twist'' identification of a circle to obtain a \
new Melvin\nbackground. In the near-horizon limit the background becomes the \
magnetic-flux\ndeformed $AdS_5 \\times S^4$ or $AdS_4 \\times S^5$ spacetime. \
After analyzing\nthe classical closed string solutions with several angular \
momenta in different\ndirections of the deformed spacetimes we obtain two \
string solutions. The first\nsolution describes a circular closed string \
located at a fixed value of\ndeformed $AdS_5$ radius while rotating \
simultaneously in two planes in deformed\n$AdS_5$ with equal spins $S$. The \
second solution describes a string rotating\nin deformed $S^5$ with two equal \
angular momenta $J$ in the two rotation\nplanes. We investigate the small \
fluctuations therein and show that the\nmagnetic fluxes have inclination to \
improve the stability of these classical\nstring solutions.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 17 Oct 2005 14:03:41 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 20 Oct 2005 08:45:42 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v3", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 25 Oct 2005 13:23:36 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v4", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 5 Dec 2005 07:59:27 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2009-11-11", "authors_parsed" -> {{"Huang", "Wung-Hong", ""}}}], 
          "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{11.8465237010087, -5.877051651406333}, 0.],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th/0510150\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Akitsugu Miwa\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Hiroyuki Hata, Akitsugu Miwa\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Loop Equation in D=4, N=4 SYM and String Field Equation \
on AdS_5 \\\\times\\n  S^5\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"35 pages, no figures, v2: references added\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Phys.Rev. D73 (2006) 046001\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.73.046001\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"KUNS-1990\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We consider the loop equation in four-dimensional N=4 \
SYM, which is a\\nfunctional differential equation for the Wilson loop W(C) \
and expresses the\\npropagation and the interaction of the string C. Our W(C) \
consists of the\\nscalar and the gaugino fields as well as the gauge field. \
The loop C is\\nspecified by six bosonic coordinates y^i(s) and two fermionic \
coordinates\\n\\\\zeta(s) and \\\\eta(s) besides the four-dimensional \
spacetime coordinates\\nx^\\\\mu(s). We have successfully determined, to \
quadratic order in \\\\zeta and\\n\\\\eta, the parameters in W(C) and the \
loop differential operator so that the\\nequation of motion of SYM can be \
correctly reproduced to give the non-linear\\nterm of W(C). We extract the \
most singular and linear part of our loop equation\\nand compare it with the \
Hamiltonian constraint of the string propagating on\\nAdS_5 \\\\times S^5 \
background.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
