                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Giovanni Feverati, Davide Fioravanti, Paolo Grinza and \
Marco Rossi\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"On the finite size corrections of anti-ferromagnetic \
anomalous\\n  dimensions in ${\\\\cal N}=4$ SYM\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"38 pages, Latex revised version: draft formulae indicator \
deleted,\\n  one reference added, typos corrected, few minor text \
modifications\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP 0605:068,2006\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1088/1126-6708/2006/05/068\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Non-linear integral equations derived from Bethe Ansatz \
are used to evaluate\\nfinite size corrections to the highest (i.e. {\\\\it \
anti-ferromagnetic}) and\\nimmediately lower anomalous dimensions of scalar \
operators in ${\\\\cal N}=4$ SYM.\\nIn specific, multi-loop corrections are \
computed in the SU(2) operator\\nsubspace, whereas in the general SO(6) case \
only one loop calculations have\\nbeen finalised. In these cases, the leading \
finite size corrections are given\\nby means of explicit formul\\\\ae and \
compared with the exact numerical\\nevaluation. In addition, the method here \
proposed is quite general and\\nespecially suitable for numerical \
evaluations.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
