               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Alessio Marrani\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Spin-Bits and N=4 SYM\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"29 pages; contribution to the Proceedings of the 43rd \
Erice\\n  International School of Subnuclear Physics ``Towards New Milestones \
in our\\n  Quest to go Beyond the Standard Model'', Erice, Italy (29 \
August--7 September\\n  2005); v2: some references added\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1142/9789812779120_0015\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We briefly review the spin-bit formalism, describing \
the non-planar dynamics\\nof the $\\\\mathcal{N}=4,d=4$ Super Yang-Mills \
SU(N) gauge theory. After\\nconsidering its foundations, we apply such a \
formalism to the $su(2)$ sector of\\npurely scalar operators. In particular, \
we report an algorithmic formulation of\\na deplanarizing procedure for local \
operators in the planar gauge theory, used\\nto obtain planarly-consistent, \
testable conjectures for the higher-loop $su(2)$\\nspin-bit Hamiltonians. \
Finally, we outlook some possible developments and\\napplications.\\n\""}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
