            "report-no" -> "TIFR/TH/06-35", "categories" -> "hep-th", 
            "license" -> Null, "abstract" -> 
            "  We discuss finite regions of the deconfining phase of a \
confining gauge\ntheory (plasma balls/kinks) as solitons of the large $N$, \
long wavelength,\neffective Lagrangian of the thermal gauge theory expressed \
in terms of suitable\norder parameters. We consider a class of confining \
gauge theories whose\neffective Lagrangian turns out to be a generic 1 dim. \
unitary matrix model. The\ndynamics of this matrix model can be studied by an \
exact mapping to a\nnon-relativistic many fermion problem on a circle. We \
present an approximate\nsolution to the equations of motion which corresponds \
to the motion (in\nEuclidean time) of the Fermi surface interpolating between \
the phase where the\nfermions are uniformly distributed on the circle \
(confinement phase) and the\nphase where the fermion distribution has a gap \
on the circle (deconfinement\nphase). We later self-consistently verify that \
the approximation is a good one.\nWe discuss some properties and implications \
of the solution including the\nsurface tension which turns out to be \
positive. As a by product of our\ninvestigation we point out the problem of \
obtaining time dependent solutions in\nthe collective field theory formalism \
due to generic shock formation.\n", 
