         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "hep-th/0701052", "submitter" -> "Satoshi Yamaguchi", 
            "authors" -> "Satoshi Yamaguchi", "title" -> 
            "Semi-classical open string corrections and symmetric Wilson \
loops", "comments" -> 
            "14 pages, 4 figures. v2: explanation improved. v3: argument in\n \
 section 2 is improved, result not changed", "journal-ref" -> 
            "JHEP 0706:073,2007", "doi" -> "10.1088/1126-6708/2007/06/073", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> Null, 
            "abstract" -> 
            "  In the AdS/CFT correspondence, an AdS_2 x S^2 D3-brane with \
electric flux in\nAdS_5 x S^5 spacetime corresponds to a circular Wilson loop \
in the symmetric\nrepresentation or a multiply wound one in N=4 super \
Yang-Mills theory. In order\nto distinguish the symmetric loop and the \
multiply wound loop, one should see\nan exponentially small correction in \
large 't Hooft coupling. We study\nsemi-classically the disk open string \
attached to the D3-brane. We obtain the\nexponent of the term and it agrees \
with the result of the matrix model\ncalculation of the symmetric Wilson \
loop.\n", "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Sun, 7 Jan 2007 22:00:43 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 26 Jan 2007 13:35:16 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v3", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 4 May 2007 09:49:22 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2009-11-13", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Yamaguchi", "Satoshi", ""}}}], 
          "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{12.406723819461332`, -5.237164624378252}, 0.],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th/0701069\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Wung-Hong Huang\""}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Wung-Hong Huang\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Dual String Description of Wilson Loop in Non-commutative \
Gauge Theory\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Latex 12 pages\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Phys.Lett.B647:519-525,2007; \
Erratum-ibid.B652:388-389,2007\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"10.1016/j.physletb.2007.02.052 \
10.1016/j.physletb.2007.07.020\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The Wilson loop in some non-commutative gauge theories \
is studied by using\\nthe dual string description in which the corresponding \
string is on the curved\\nbackground with B field. For the theory in which a \
constant B field is turned\\non along the brane worldvolume the Wilson loop \
always shows a Coulomb phase, as\\nstudied in the previous literature. We \
extend the examination to the theory\\nwith a non-constant B field, which \
duals to the gauge theory with non-constant\\nnon-commutativity, and \
re-examine the theory in the presence of a nonzero B\\nfield with one leg \
along the brane worldvolume and other transverse to it,\\nwhich duals to a \
non-commutative dipole theory. The expectation value of the\\nWilson loop is \
calculated to the lowest order by evaluating the area of the\\nstring \
worldsheet. The results show that, while the non-commutativity could\\nmodify \
the Coulomb type potential in IR it may produce a strong repulsive \
force\\nbetween the quark and anti-quark if they are close enough. In \
particular, we\\nfind that there presents a minimum distance between the \
quarks and that the\\ndistance is proportional to the value of the \
non-commutativity, exhibiting the\\nnature of the non-commutative \
theory.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Tue, 9 Jan 2007 14:08:41 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v2\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Sat, 24 Feb 2007 06:06:27 GMT\""}]}], "}"}]}], 
                   "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2007-09-06\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"\"Huang\"", ",", "\"Wung-Hong\"", ",", "\"\""}], 
                    "}"}], "}"}]}]}}, RowSpacings -> 1, ColumnAlignments -> 
             Left, ColumnAlignments -> Left], Column], 
           Function[BoxForm`e$, 
            TableForm[BoxForm`e$]]]],
