         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "hep-th/0701281", "submitter" -> "Alessandro Torrielli", 
            "authors" -> "Alessandro Torrielli", "title" -> 
            "Classical r-matrix of the su(2|2) SYM spin-chain", "comments" -> 
            "14 pages, LaTeX, no figures; corrections made, further analysis \
of\n  the residue and references added", "journal-ref" -> 
            "Phys.Rev.D75:105020,2007", "doi" -> "10.1103/PhysRevD.75.105020",
             "report-no" -> "MIT-CTP 3809", "categories" -> "hep-th", 
            "license" -> Null, "abstract" -> 
            "  In this note we straightforwardly derive and make use of the \
quantum R-matrix\nfor the su(2|2) SYM spin-chain in the manifest \
su(1|2)-invariant formulation,\nwhich solves the standard quantum Yang-Baxter \
equation, in order to obtain the\ncorrespondent (undressed) classical \
r-matrix from the first order expansion in\nthe ``deformation'' parameter 2 \
\\pi / \\sqrt{\\lambda}, and check that this last\nsolves the standard \
classical Yang-Baxter equation. We analyze its bialgebra\nstructure, its \
dependence on the spectral parameters and its pole structure. We\nnotice that \
it still preserves an su(1|2) subalgebra, thereby admitting an\nexpression in \
terms of a combination of projectors, which spans only a subspace\nof su(1|2) \
\\otimes su(1|2). We study the residue at its simple pole at the\norigin, and \
comment on the applicability of the classical Belavin-Drinfeld type\nof \
analysis.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 30 Jan 2007 18:56:02 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 13 Feb 2007 16:24:57 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v3", "created" -> 
               "Wed, 25 Apr 2007 13:43:37 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2008-11-26", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Torrielli", "Alessandro", ""}}}], 
          "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[DiskBox[{18.58687725000702, -4.922126377040724}, 0.],
