               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th/0703068\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Diego Hernan Correa\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Heng-Yu Chen, Diego H. Correa and Guillermo A. \
Silva\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Geometry and topology of bubble solutions from gauge \
theory\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"21 pages, 2 figures. v2: references added, typos \
corrected\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Phys.Rev.D76:026003,2007\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.76.026003\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"DAMTP-07-15\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study how geometrical and topological aspects of \
certain 1/2 BPS type IIB\\nsupergravity solutions are captured by the N=4 \
Super Yang-Mills gauge theory in\\nthe AdS/CFT context. The type IIB \
solutions are completely characterized by\\narbitrary droplets in a plane and \
we consider, in particular, concentric\\ndroplets. We probe the dual 1/2 BPS \
operators of the gauge theory with single\\ntraces and extract their one-loop \
anomalous dimensions. The action of the\\none-loop dilatation operator can be \
reformulated as the Hamiltonian of a\\nbosonic lattice. The operators \
defining the Hamiltonian encode the topology of\\nthe droplet. The axial \
symmetry of the droplets turns out to be essential for\\nobtaining the \
spectrum of the Hamiltonians. In appropriate BMN limits, the\\nnear-BPS \
spectrum reproduces the spectrum of near-BPS string excitations\\npropagating \
along each individual edge of the droplet of the dual geometric\\nbackground. \
We also study semiclassical regimes for the Hamiltonians. We show\\nthat for \
droplets having disconnected constituents, the Hamiltonian admits\\ndifferent \
complimentary semiclassical descriptions, each one replicating \
the\\nsemiclassical description for closed strings extending in each of \
the\\nconstituents.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
