$U_\\theta (N)$\ngauge theory with the parameter of noncommutativity \
$\\theta$. For a generic\nrectangular contour $C$, a concise integral \
representation is derived\n(non-perturbatively both in the coupling constant \
$g^{2}$ and in $\\theta$) for\nthe next-to-leading term of the $1/\\theta$ \
expansion. In turn, in the limit\nwhen ${\\theta}$ is much larger than the \
area $A(C)$ of the surface bounded by\n$C$, the large $\\theta$ asymptote of \
this representation is argued to yield the\nnext-to-leading term of the \
$1/\\theta$ series. For both of the expansions, the\nnext-to-leading \
contribution exhibits only a power-like decay for areas\n$A(C)>>\\sigma^{-1}$ \
(but $A(C)<<{\\theta}$) much larger than the inverse of the\nstring tension $\
\\sigma$ defining the range of the exponential decay of the\nleading term. \
Consequently, for large $\\theta$, it hinders a direct stringy\n\
interpretation of the subleading terms of the 1/N expansion in the spirit of\n\
Gross-Taylor proposal for the $\\theta=0$ commutative D=2 gauge theory.\n", 
