               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th/9605140\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Dr R. Szabo\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Gordon W. Semenoff and Richard J. Szabo\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Fermionic Matrix Models\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"139 pages Latex (99 pages in landscape, two-column \
option); Section\\n  on Supersymmetric Matrix Models expanded, additional \
references included\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Int.J.Mod.Phys. A12 (1997) 2135-2292\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1142/S0217751X97001328\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"UBC/S-96/2 , OUTP-96-22P\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 
                 "\"  We review a class of matrix models whose degrees of \
freedom are matrices with\\nanticommuting elements. We discuss the properties \
of the adjoint fermion one-,\\ntwo- and gauge invariant D-dimensional matrix \
models at large-N and compare\\nthem with their bosonic counterparts which \
are the more familiar Hermitian\\nmatrix models. We derive and solve the \
complete sets of loop equations for the\\ncorrelators of these models and use \
these equations to examine critical\\nbehaviour. The topological large-N \
expansions are also constructed and their\\nrelation to other aspects of \
modern string theory such as integrable\\nhierarchies is discussed. We use \
these connections to discuss the applications\\nof these matrix models to \
string theory and induced gauge theories. We argue\\nthat as such the \
fermionic matrix models may provide a novel generalization of\\nthe \
discretized random surface representation of quantum gravity in which \
the\\ngenus sum alternates and the sums over genera for correlators have \
better\\nconvergence properties than their Hermitian counterparts. We discuss \
the use of\\nadjoint fermions instead of adjoint scalars to study induced \
gauge theories. We\\nalso discuss two classes of dimensionally reduced \
models, a fermionic vector\\nmodel and a supersymmetric matrix model, and \
discuss their applications to the\\nbranched polymer phase of string theories \
in target space dimensions D>1 and\\nalso to the meander problem.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Tue, 21 May 1996 09:53:56 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v2\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Thu, 7 Nov 1996 10:18:35 GMT\""}]}], "}"}]}], 
                   "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2009-10-30\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
