            "Fermionic Matrix Models", "comments" -> 
            "139 pages Latex (99 pages in landscape, two-column option); \
Section\n  on Supersymmetric Matrix Models expanded, additional references \
included", "journal-ref" -> "Int.J.Mod.Phys. A12 (1997) 2135-2292", "doi" -> 
            "10.1142/S0217751X97001328", "report-no" -> 
            "UBC/S-96/2 , OUTP-96-22P", "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            Null, "abstract" -> 
            "  We review a class of matrix models whose degrees of freedom \
are matrices with\nanticommuting elements. We discuss the properties of the \
adjoint fermion one-,\ntwo- and gauge invariant D-dimensional matrix models \
at large-N and compare\nthem with their bosonic counterparts which are the \
more familiar Hermitian\nmatrix models. We derive and solve the complete sets \
of loop equations for the\ncorrelators of these models and use these \
equations to examine critical\nbehaviour. The topological large-N expansions \
are also constructed and their\nrelation to other aspects of modern string \
theory such as integrable\nhierarchies is discussed. We use these connections \
to discuss the applications\nof these matrix models to string theory and \
induced gauge theories. We argue\nthat as such the fermionic matrix models \
may provide a novel generalization of\nthe discretized random surface \
representation of quantum gravity in which the\ngenus sum alternates and the \
sums over genera for correlators have better\nconvergence properties than \
their Hermitian counterparts. We discuss the use of\nadjoint fermions instead \
of adjoint scalars to study induced gauge theories. We\nalso discuss two \
classes of dimensionally reduced models, a fermionic vector\nmodel and a \
supersymmetric matrix model, and discuss their applications to the\nbranched \
polymer phase of string theories in target space dimensions D>1 and\nalso to \
the meander problem.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 21 May 1996 09:53:56 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 7 Nov 1996 10:18:35 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
