corrected\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Mod.Phys.Lett. A13 (1998) 263-274\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1142/S0217732398000322\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"YITP-97-40, DTP-97-37\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The infinite limit of Matrix Theory in 4 and 10 \
dimensions is described in\\nterms of Moyal Brackets. In those dimensions \
there exists a Bogomol'nyi bound\\nto the Euclideanized version of these \
equations, which guarantees that\\nsolutions of the first order equations \
also solve the second order Matrix\\nTheory equations. A general construction \
of such solutions in terms of a\\nrepresentation of the target space \
co-ordinates as non-local spinor bilinears,\\nwhich are generalisations of \
the standard Wigner functions on phase space, is\\ngiven.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
