            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th/9903195\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Micha Berkooz\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"M. Berkooz (IAS, Princeton) and A. Kapustin (IAS, \
Princeton)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"A Comment on Nonsupersymmetric Fixed Points and Duality \
at large N\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"14 pages, 1 figure, Harvmac. References added\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Adv.Theor.Math.Phys.3:479-494,1999\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We review some of the problems associated with deriving \
field theoretic\\nresults from nonsupersymmetric AdS, focusing on how to \
control the behavior of\\nthe field theory along the flat directions. We \
discuss an example in which the\\norigin of the moduli space remains a stable \
vacuum at finite $N$, and argue\\nthat it corresponds to an interacting CFT \
in three dimensions. Associated to\\nthis fixed point is a statement of \
nonsupersymmetric duality. Because 1/N\\ncorrections may change the global \
picture of the RG flow, the statement of\\nduality is much weaker than in the \
supersymmetric case.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
